{"version":"6.9","hitCount":5186,"nextCursorMark":"AoIIQPIt9ig1NTgwNDMwOQ==","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=((\"large language model\" OR LLM OR chatbot) AND (\"human oversight\" OR \"human-in-the-loop\" OR contestability OR redress OR appeal OR escalation OR handoff)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]&cursorMark=AoIIQPIt9ig1NTgwNDMwOQ==&resultType=core&pageSize=100&format=json","request":{"queryString":"((\"large language model\" OR LLM OR chatbot) AND (\"human oversight\" OR \"human-in-the-loop\" OR contestability OR redress OR appeal OR escalation OR handoff)) AND FIRST_PDATE:[1900-01-01 TO 2026-07-31]","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":100,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42293851","source":"MED","pmid":"42293851","pmcid":"PMC13263806","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13263806"]},"doi":"10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097","title":"Large language models for post-discharge follow-up in erectile dysfunction care: a narrative review.","authorString":"Zhang Y, Lv B, Xu R, Shi C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhang Y","firstName":"Yiman","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lv B","firstName":"Bodong","lastName":"Lv","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu R","firstName":"Runnan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Urology, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shi C","firstName":"Chenghao","lastName":"Shi","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-4402-814X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4217778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Translational andrology and urology","medlineAbbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","isoabbreviation":"Transl Androl Urol","nlmid":"101581119","essn":"2223-4691","issn":"2223-4691"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"185","abstractText":"<h4>Background and objective</h4>Erectile dysfunction (ED) is common and closely linked to cardiometabolic disease, psychological distress, and relationship problems. Although effective treatments are available, outcomes depend on sustained post-discharge follow-up to assess response, adherence, adverse effects, and psychosocial needs. In practice, ED follow-up is often hindered by stigma, embarrassment, limited access, and fragmented communication. This narrative review examines the potential role, risks, and implementation requirements of large language model (LLM)-assisted follow-up in ED care.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a targeted literature search in PubMed and Embase up to 15 January 2026, supplemented by Google Scholar for citation tracking. We selected clinically relevant evidence and conceptual work on ED follow-up, conversational artificial intelligence (AI), and safety governance, and synthesized findings thematically.<h4>Key content and findings</h4>Among 280 included sources, only 10 were ED-specific, while 270 provided transferable evidence from chronic disease, oncology, and mental health follow-up settings. LLMs may reduce communication barriers, enable low-threshold symptom check-ins, reinforce discharge education, and collect patient-reported outcomes more frequently than traditional follow-up. Potential clinical value is greatest for structured, low-risk tasks such as education reinforcement, adherence support, and routine symptom monitoring. However, ED follow-up includes medication safety, nuanced counseling, mental health and relationship contexts, and sensitive data governance. Key risks include inaccurate or unsafe recommendations, privacy breaches, inappropriate handling of mental health red flags, and unclear accountability. A risk-stratified, human-in-the-loop implementation model can balance feasibility and safety by reserving autonomous LLM interactions for low-risk content while triggering clinician review and escalation for predefined high-risk scenarios.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLM-assisted follow-up could become a useful adjunct in ED care if implemented with clear boundaries, auditability, privacy protection, and robust human oversight. A nurse-supervised, trigger-based governance model, which aligns with current follow-up workflows, may enhance continuity and responsiveness without compromising clinical accountability. Prospective evaluations should prioritize patient acceptability, safety outcomes, workload impact, and implementation feasibility.","affiliation":"Department of Nursing, The Second Affiliated Hospital Zhejiang University School of Medicine, Hangzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Erectile dysfunction (ED)","Human-in-the-loop","Post-discharge Follow-up","Large Language Model (Llm)","Conversational Artificial Intelligence (Conversational Ai)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21037/tau-2026-1-0097"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263806?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-26"},{"id":"42211738","source":"MED","pmid":"42211738","pmcid":"PMC13214549","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13214549"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181","title":"Current themes of AI in mental health: Actionable evidence and guardrails for mood and anxiety care.","authorString":"Paulus MP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Paulus MP","firstName":"Martin P","lastName":"Paulus","initials":"MP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Oxley College of Health and Natural Sciences, University of Tulsa, Tulsa, OK, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0825-3606"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4182059,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of mood and anxiety disorders","medlineAbbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","isoabbreviation":"J Mood Anxiety Disord","nlmid":"9918646056406676","essn":"2950-0044","issn":"2950-0044"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100181","abstractText":"Currently, artificial intelligence (AI) is clinically relevant to mood and anxiety care, but the evidence base is uneven across use cases. This narrative review synthesizes recent literature most relevant to clinicians and investigators. Five themes dominate the current field: patient-facing adjunctive tools, failure modes and safety risks, clinician-facing decision support, passive sensing and measurement infrastructure, and governance. Recent randomized evidence supports a narrow efficacy claim for structured chatbot interventions, with small improvements in depressive and anxiety symptoms and more consistent effects on engagement than on symptom superiority. These studies do not support autonomous psychotherapy, and they do not establish a therapeutic advantage for open-ended large language model systems over more constrained designs. Safety studies, by contrast, identify active concerns: harmful endorsement, weak youth risk assessment, inconsistent crisis handling, and anxiety/OCD reassurance loops. The strongest current clinical signal lies in supervised clinician-facing decision support, where recent trials of AI-assisted antidepressant selection improved treatment persistence and some downstream symptom outcomes. Passive sensing detects behaviorally meaningful signals, but evidence that alert-driven deployment improves care remains insufficient for routine practice. Across stakeholder and policy sources, the most defensible deployment model is human-in-the-loop, stepped, and bounded by explicit handoff rules.","affiliation":"Laureate Institute for Brain Research, Tulsa, OK, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIGMS","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Depression","Phenotyping","Implementation","Anxiety","Governance","Chatbot"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xjmad.2026.100181"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13214549?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-14"},{"id":"42308509","source":"MED","pmid":"42308509","pmcid":"PMC13324317","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13324317"]},"doi":"10.2196/92240","title":"Ethical Considerations in Personal Health Large Language Models.","authorString":"Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e92240","abstractText":"Personal health large language models (PH-LLMs) have rapidly evolved from research prototypes into consumer-facing, data-linked systems that support symptom triage, medication questions, mental health check-ins, and longitudinal self-management. Their direct-to-consumer use without clinical oversight creates a distinct ethical risk profile that general artificial intelligence governance frameworks do not fully address. This viewpoint focuses on text-based, platform-mediated PH-LLMs and synthesizes PH-LLM-specific challenges across 6 domains: privacy, accuracy, equity, transparency, human-artificial intelligence interaction, and regulatory governance. These risks may be amplified by health literacy gaps, longitudinal data aggregation, persuasive conversational design, and fragmented oversight across the consumer-clinical boundary. Grounded in the 4 principles of biomedical ethics, we propose a governance framework that operationalizes beneficence, nonmaleficence, autonomy, and justice through design and deployment controls, including health literacy-aligned communication, crisis and pharmacological safeguards, hallucination mitigation, role disclosure, granular consent, fairness auditing, and accessible design. We further outline implementation mechanisms, including risk-tiered certification, tiered accountability, and postdeployment oversight through adverse-event reporting, transparency reporting, and independent safety evaluation. This framework is intended as an evidence-informed but partly anticipatory approach to governing PH-LLMs in personal health management.","affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Health Literacy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Ethics","Patient Safety","Health Literacy","Privacy","Governance","Health Equity","Digital Health","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Health Ai","Personal Health Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/92240"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13324317?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-17"},{"id":"42194487","source":"MED","pmid":"42194487","pmcid":"PMC13205439","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13205439"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14101395","title":"Safety Mechanisms and Risk Mitigation in Generative AI Mental Health Chatbots: A Systematic Scoping Review.","authorString":"Olisaeloka L, Richardson CG, Wang AY, Munthali RJ, Vigo DV.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Olisaeloka L","firstName":"Lotenna","lastName":"Olisaeloka","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Richardson CG","firstName":"Chris G","lastName":"Richardson","initials":"CG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang AY","firstName":"Angel Y","lastName":"Wang","initials":"AY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2001-2220"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Munthali RJ","firstName":"Richard J","lastName":"Munthali","initials":"RJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8492-7409"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Vigo DV","firstName":"Daniel V","lastName":"Vigo","initials":"DV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z8, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8492-7409"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2001-2220"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4201779,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1395","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Generative AI (GenAI) mental health chatbots are increasingly being developed to help address persistent barriers to mental healthcare. Unlike earlier rule-based and retrieval-based systems, GenAI chatbots generate open-ended outputs that can be inaccurate and unsafe. Documented harms from general-purpose GenAI chatbots have highlighted the need for purpose-built interventions with dedicated safeguards, yet how safety is implemented in such interventions remains poorly understood.<h4>Methods</h4>This scoping review followed the Joanna Briggs Institute methodology and PRISMA-ScR guidelines, with a prospectively registered and peer-reviewed protocol. A systematic search of seven academic databases and search engines including MEDLINE, Scopus, PsycINFO, ACM Digital Library, IEEE Xplore, Google Scholar and Consensus was conducted in July 2025. Two reviewers independently screened records and extracted data. Safety mechanisms and risk mitigation strategies were narratively synthesised across three pre-specified domains: technical safeguards, pre-deployment safety considerations, and delivery-phase risk mitigation strategies.<h4>Results</h4>Twenty-one studies across 11 countries were included. Most interventions incorporated at least one technical safety mechanism, most commonly fine-tuning and prompt engineering. A smaller subset implemented layered safety architectures combining retrieval systems, content filters or risk classifiers, and rule-based algorithms. Pre-deployment safeguards included clinical expert and user co-design approaches, research ethics procedures, and data privacy measures. During intervention delivery, detailed onboarding with role clarification was common, but human oversight was limited. Crisis referral protocols varied in rigour but were mostly underdeveloped, and systematic adverse event monitoring was sparse. Documented safety failures included missed suicidal ideation and provision of inaccurate clinical information.<h4>Conclusions</h4>GenAI chatbot interventions require a robust sociotechnical approach that integrates technical safeguards with user co-design, procedural controls, and human oversight. Future research is needed to evaluate efficacy, improve safeguards and standardise safety outcome measurement. Regulatory oversight proportional to the risks these systems carry is required to enable integration into stepped or blended mental healthcare.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 0A6, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Mental Health Chatbots","Ai Safety","Generative Ai","Chatgpt","Ai Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14101395"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13205439"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13205439?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1174289","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"42297358","source":"MED","pmid":"42297358","pmcid":"PMC13315997","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13315997"]},"doi":"10.2196/76924","title":"Developing a Large Language Model-Based Feedback System for Case Report Writing in Rehabilitation Education: Tutorial.","authorString":"Tonouchi Y, Nakai S, Murakami K, Kataoka Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tonouchi Y","firstName":"Yuuto","lastName":"Tonouchi","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-4813-4829"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Rehabilitation, Kyoto Min-iren Asukai Hospital, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nakai S","firstName":"Shunsuke","lastName":"Nakai","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-8775-0715"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Occupational Therapy, Faculty of Rehabilitation, Morinomiya University of Medical Sciences, Osaka, Osaka, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Graduate School of Rehabilitation Science, Osaka Metropolitan University, Osaka, Osaka, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Murakami K","firstName":"Kayo","lastName":"Murakami","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-1516-8452"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Rehabilitation, Kyoto Min-iren Asukai Hospital, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kataoka Y","firstName":"Yuki","lastName":"Kataoka","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7982-5213"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Postgraduate Clinical Training and Career Development, Nagoya University Hospital, Nagoya, Aichi, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Center for Medical Education, Graduate School of Medicine, Nagoya University, Nagoya, Aichi, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Epidemiology, Graduate School of Medicine and Public Health, Kyoto University, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Systematic Reviewers, Scientific Research Works Peer Support Group (SRWS-PSG), Osaka, Osaka, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, Kyoto Min-iren Asukai Hospital, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7982-5213"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-1516-8452"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-8775-0715"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-4813-4829"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4204590,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical education","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","nlmid":"101684518","essn":"2369-3762","issn":"2369-3762"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e76924","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Novice health care staff often write case reports during early clinical training. However, many institutions lack structured feedback systems because of time constraints and instructor shortages. Large language models, a form of artificial intelligence (AI), offer new opportunities to enhance educational feedback, yet their application in clinical training requires careful design to ensure pedagogically appropriate and ethically sound outputs.<h4>Objective</h4>This tutorial provides a practical guide for educators without programming experience to develop an AI-based feedback system using 3 accessible tools: Dify (an AI workflow platform), Slack (a messaging app), and Google Apps Script. The system balances educational quality with operational efficiency while incorporating data privacy safeguards for clinical educational content.<h4>Methods</h4>We developed a feedback system comprising 4 AI chatbots with 2 distinct approaches: \"loop-based\" bots that promote clinical reasoning through iterative, comment-based feedback and \"single-shot\" bots for efficient proofreading and summarization tasks. The tutorial describes the system architecture; feedback design principles grounded in formative assessment theory; a step-by-step implementation guide; and data privacy safeguards, including a deidentification protocol and application programming interface-based data protection measures. To illustrate feasibility, we conducted a pilot implementation at a community care hospital from April to June 2024, involving 5 novice staff members and 5 instructors.<h4>Results</h4>A pilot implementation at a community care hospital demonstrated that the system was feasible to deploy and operate within routine clinical education workflows. Participant feedback indicated high usability and suggested that the iterative, comment-based feedback approach supported learner engagement while also identifying areas where feedback criteria required refinement to better match institutional educational goals.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This tutorial provides a reproducible framework for building a customized AI feedback system that combines comment-based iterative feedback with human-in-the-loop oversight and a multilayered data privacy framework. By following this guide, educators can implement an adaptive system tailored to their institutional context and clinical domain.","affiliation":"Department of Rehabilitation, Kyoto Min-iren Asukai Hospital, Kyoto, Kyoto, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Rehabilitation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ED","qualifierName":"education","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Feedback"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Writing"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Feedback","Rehabilitation","AI","Education","Case Report","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/76924"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13315997"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13315997?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-15"},{"id":"42211192","source":"MED","pmid":"42211192","pmcid":"PMC13212461","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212461"]},"doi":"10.3389/fpsyt.2026.1847854","title":"Conversational AI for perinatal mental health: promise, limits, and a human-AI stepped-care framework.","authorString":"Yang H, Lin DM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang H","firstName":"Haiyan","lastName":"Yang","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Reproductive Medicine Center, The First Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical University, Wenzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin DM","firstName":"Dong-Mei","lastName":"Lin","initials":"DM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Third Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical University (Ruian People's Hospital), Wenzhou, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4112086,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in psychiatry","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Psychiatry","isoabbreviation":"Front Psychiatry","nlmid":"101545006","essn":"1664-0640","issn":"1664-0640"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1847854","abstractText":"Perinatal mental health conditions are common yet persistently underdetected and undertreated. Recent advances in generative artificial intelligence (AI) and multimodal interaction offer new opportunities for scalable, continuous support, making the concept of the \"digital doula\" particularly compelling. Because perinatal care relies heavily on emotional support, repeated contact, and timely escalation, conversational AI is attractive as a potential adjunct to existing care. We argue that digital doulas should not be framed as autonomous substitutes for clinicians or human doulas, but rather conceptualized as AI-enabled relational interfaces embedded within a stepped-care model. We propose four core functions for digital doulas: companionship, symptom interpretation, navigation, and sentinel monitoring. These roles can reduce disclosure barriers, extend support between clinical visits, and strengthen linkage to appropriate care. However, conversational AI also introduces significant risks, including the illusion of empathy, crisis recognition failures, algorithmic bias against vulnerable populations, data privacy vulnerabilities, and unsafe deployment. To address these risks, we outline a safety-by-design agenda emphasizing human oversight, clear escalation protocols with medico-legal accountability, workflow integration, and equity-centered evaluation. Ultimately, the clinical value of digital doulas depends on safely strengthening the perinatal mental health care cascade without replacing its core human relationships.","affiliation":"Reproductive Medicine Center, The First Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical University, Wenzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Stepped Care","Postpartum Depression","Perinatal Mental Health","Conversational Ai","Generative Ai","Digital Doula"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2026.1847854"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212461"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212461?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-13"},{"id":"42342371","source":"MED","pmid":"42342371","pmcid":"PMC13295973","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13295973"]},"doi":"10.1136/bmjment-2026-302626","title":"Leveraging simulation to provide a practical framework for estimating the novel scope of risk of large language models in healthcare.","authorString":"Kalinich M, Luccarelli J, Santa Maria J, Moss F, Torous J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kalinich M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Kalinich","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8794-1289"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, Massachusetts, USA mkalinic@bidmc.harvard.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Luccarelli J","firstName":"James","lastName":"Luccarelli","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1606-3917"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Santa Maria J Jr","firstName":"John","lastName":"Santa Maria","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Parabilis Medicines, Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Moss F","firstName":"Frank","lastName":"Moss","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Strategic Software Ventures, LLC, East Orleans, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Torous J","firstName":"John","lastName":"Torous","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5362-7937"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1606-3917"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5362-7937"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8794-1289"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"29","journalIssueId":4204774,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"BMJ mental health","medlineAbbreviation":"BMJ Ment Health","isoabbreviation":"BMJ Ment Health","nlmid":"9918521385306676","essn":"2755-9734","issn":"2755-9734"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e302626","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are rapidly entering clinical and consumer use, yet their probabilistic outputs have delivered a variety of unsafe user responses. Difficulties in quantifying and mitigating risks posed by LLMs threaten to stall regulatory evaluation and clinical deployment of LLM-based software as a medical device (LLM-SaMD). Practical approaches are needed to extend existing medical-device regulations to LLM-SaMDs.<h4>Objective</h4>To demonstrate how simulation can extend existing medical-device risk management frameworks for addressing LLM-SaMD-specific risks.<h4>Methods</h4>We implement a simulation-based methodology for estimating LLM-SaMD risk. Fourteen open-source models were evaluated on three safety-classification tasks: suicidal-ideation, therapy-request and therapy-like interaction detection. Synthetic datasets were generated by Gemini 2.5 Pro and evaluated by psychiatrists. Model false-negative rates informed estimates of P<sub>1</sub>, the likelihood that a hazard progresses to a hazardous situation, and P<sub>2</sub>, the likelihood that that situation results in harm.<h4>Findings</h4>LLM success at generating synthetic datasets varied by task, with strong performance for neutral and non-therapeutic content but frequent errors in suicidal-ideation and therapy-like interactions. Performance generally improved with model size. Estimated P<sub>1</sub> values ranged from 1.1×10⁻⁸ to 1.6×10⁻⁴ and P<sub>2</sub> from 4.9×10⁻⁵ to 5.1×10⁻³, spanning four orders of magnitude.<h4>Conclusions</h4>By linking model failure modes to structured pathways to harm, simulation can extend existing medical-device risk frameworks to help address the probabilistic and context-dependent risks of LLM-SaMDs.<h4>Clinical implications</h4>Simulation-based risk estimation offers a practical way to characterise the risk landscape for specific LLM-SaMD, patient population and clinical context combinations.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center, Boston, Massachusetts, USA mkalinic@bidmc.harvard.edu.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Assessment","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Computer Simulation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Risk Management","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Suicidal Ideation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Depressive Disorder","Anxiety Disorders","Behavioral Sciences"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjment-2026-302626"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295973"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295973?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24"},{"id":"42160748","source":"MED","pmid":"42160748","pmcid":"PMC13234539","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13234539"]},"doi":"10.2196/84683","title":"Development, Feasibility, Acceptability, and Usability of an Artificial Intelligence-Powered Chatbot (Suzy) to Support Patients in Substance Use Disorder Recovery: Multiphase Study.","authorString":"Comulada WS, Swendeman D, Ho YX, Streck JM, Lewis DM, Rezai R, Warren D, Pachas G, Chandler M, Jackson JL, Gelberg L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Comulada WS","firstName":"Warren Scott","lastName":"Comulada","initials":"WS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1340-6371"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Biobehavioral Sciences, David Geffen School of Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."},{"affiliation":"Department of Health Policy and Management, Fielding School of Public Health, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Swendeman D","firstName":"Dallas","lastName":"Swendeman","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4570-6352"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Biobehavioral Sciences, David Geffen School of Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."},{"affiliation":"Department of Epidemiology, Fielding School of Public Health, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Ho YX","firstName":"Y Xian","lastName":"Ho","initials":"YX","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1885-6136"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dimagi, Inc, Cambridge, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Streck JM","firstName":"Joanna M","lastName":"Streck","initials":"JM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7842-9080"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Addiction Medicine, Department of Psychiatry, Massachusetts General Hospital/Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, United States."},{"affiliation":"Tobacco Research and Treatment Center, Division of General Internal Medicine, Massachusetts General Hospital/Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Lewis DM","firstName":"Delta-Marie","lastName":"Lewis","initials":"DM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5707-1796"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dimagi, Inc, Cambridge, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Rezai R","firstName":"Roxana","lastName":"Rezai","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5614-9483"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Epidemiology, Fielding School of Public Health, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Warren D","firstName":"David","lastName":"Warren","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-1536-0489"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dimagi, Inc, Cambridge, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Pachas G","firstName":"Gladys","lastName":"Pachas","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2983-2954"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Addiction Medicine, Department of Psychiatry, Massachusetts General Hospital/Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Chandler M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Chandler","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0409-2428"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"TCC Family Health aka The Children's Clinic, Long Beach, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Jackson JL","firstName":"Jonathan L","lastName":"Jackson","initials":"JL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7746-2129"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Dimagi, Inc, Cambridge, MA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Gelberg L","firstName":"Lillian","lastName":"Gelberg","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9772-0116"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Policy and Management, Fielding School of Public Health, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."},{"affiliation":"Department of Family Medicine, David Geffen School of Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9772-0116"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1340-6371"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1885-6136"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4570-6352"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5614-9483"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5707-1796"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7746-2129"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7842-9080"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2983-2954"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-1536-0489"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0409-2428"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4187512,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e84683","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Substance use disorder (SUD) remains a major public health crisis in the United States, with significant challenges in treatment access, retention, and workforce capacity. SUD care teams, including addiction medicine physicians and peer recovery coaches (PRCs), support patients receiving SUD treatment but face heavy workloads and burnout. Artificial intelligence (AI) innovations, particularly large language model (LLM)-based chatbots, may extend PRC support and provide patients with on-demand recovery support between clinic visits and PRC contacts. However, evidence on their development, feasibility, acceptability, and usability in addiction services remains limited.<h4>Objective</h4>This study describes the development, feasibility, acceptability, and usability of an AI-powered health coaching chatbot (Suzy) designed to support patients in SUD recovery.<h4>Methods</h4>A total of 2 clinicians, 5 researchers, and 2 technology developers led a small, multiphase pilot study. In the formative phase, they conducted focus groups and qualitative in-depth interviews with 12 health care professionals and 8 patients with substance use histories to specify chatbot functions and develop a rule-based chatbot. In phase 2, they conducted usability testing of the rule-based chatbot with 8 patients who reported substance use and completed standardized tasks, surveys, and qualitative interviews. Measures included the System Usability Scale (SUS), Net Promoter Score (NPS), and Single Ease of Use Question (SEQ). In phase 3, they developed an LLM-based chatbot co-designed and fine-tuned with PRCs and other SUD experts.<h4>Results</h4>Rule-based chatbot functions included craving management, appointment reminders, resource referrals, care team contacts, and goal setting. Usability task testing supported feasibility. In this small pilot sample, quantitative and qualitative feedback indicated acceptability and usability, with an average SUS score of 93 (benchmark 68), an NPS of 63 (benchmark 35), and a mean SEQ score of 6.5/7. Patients valued Suzy's approachable, nonjudgmental language and features that promoted accountability, self-monitoring, and 24/7 availability, while emphasizing that chatbots should supplement but not replace human support. The LLM-based chatbot development emphasized information accuracy, safety escalation protocols to mitigate risks of inappropriate chatbot responses, human-in-the-loop features, and expanded conversational flexibility and personal tailoring.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In this pilot study, a rule-based chatbot designed to support SUD care demonstrated feasibility, usability, and acceptability. LLM-based chatbot development required more robust safety and emergency reporting features, while offering more patient-responsive conversational functions. By providing on-demand coaching, referrals, and reminders, Suzy may extend the reach of care teams, alleviate provider burden, and enhance patient engagement. Additional work is needed to understand how to best integrate Suzy into patients' recovery journeys to ensure human support remains accessible and prioritized. LLM evaluation was based on expert testing and safety review. Clinical effectiveness, including the impact on substance use, was not evaluated. Next steps include evaluating the LLM chatbot in real-world settings with larger samples and assessing its efficacy in reducing substance use.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Biobehavioral Sciences, David Geffen School of Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"R43 DA050218","agency":"NIDA NIH HHS","acronym":"DA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R44 DA050218","agency":"NIDA NIH HHS","acronym":"DA","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Substance-Related Disorders","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"RH","qualifierName":"rehabilitation","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Focus Groups","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Feasibility Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pilot Projects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Qualitative Research"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","GPT","Substance Use Disorder","Human-centered Design","Health Coach","Chatbot","Iterative Development","Peer Recovery Coach","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/84683"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13234539"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13234539?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"42080195","source":"MED","pmid":"42080195","pmcid":"PMC13129269","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13129269"]},"doi":"10.1177/17562848261441693","title":"Responsible use of large language models in gastroenterology and hepatology.","authorString":"Khanna S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Khanna S","firstName":"Sahil","lastName":"Khanna","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7619-8338"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4085889,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Therapeutic advances in gastroenterology","medlineAbbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","isoabbreviation":"Ther Adv Gastroenterol","nlmid":"101478893","essn":"1756-2848","issn":"1756-283X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"17562848261441693","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) and related generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems are rapidly entering clinical workflows, including in gastroenterology and hepatology, where text-heavy documentation, guideline-driven care, and high-volume patient messaging create a strong demand for decision support and automation. Deployment raises distinctive risks: hallucinations and unsafe recommendations, automation bias, privacy and confidentiality threats, inequitable care, intellectual property and licensing uncertainty, and unclear allocation of responsibility across clinicians, institutions, and vendors. Regulators are increasingly emphasizing a lifecycle approach to trustworthy AI, including validation, risk management, human oversight, cybersecurity, monitoring, and transparency. Frameworks for healthcare protection regulate how health information may be shared with external model providers and how outputs should be logged, audited, and retained. This review synthesizes practical guidance for responsible LLM use, organized around (1) tiered use cases and risk stratification; (2) ethical principles (beneficence, non-maleficence, autonomy, justice, and accountability); (3) core legal and regulatory considerations; and (4) operational governance, evaluation, and monitoring strategies. Actionable checklists to support institutional adoption, use, and transparency are provided. Responsible use requires aligning LLM capabilities with risk, preventing inappropriate data sharing, validating performance in representative populations, ensuring human oversight and clear accountability, mitigating harm, and maintaining an evidence-based, continuously monitored deployment lifecycle.","affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Mayo Clinic, 200 1st Street SW, Rochester, MN 55905, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Regulation","Ethics","Gastroenterology","Hepatology","Law","Privacy","Clinical Decision Support","Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/17562848261441693"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13129269?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-26"},{"id":"42037115","source":"MED","pmid":"42037115","pmcid":"PMC13111917","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13111917"]},"doi":"10.1111/cts.70566","title":"Methodological Considerations for AI-Powered Chatbots in Oral Cancer Awareness: Stakeholder Diversity, Instrument Transparency, and Safety Auditing.","authorString":"Mourão CF, Juliasse LE, Dos Santos Pereira R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mourão CF","firstName":"Carlos Fernando","lastName":"Mourão","initials":"CF","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5775-0222"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Basic and Clinical Translational Sciences, Tufts University School of Dental Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Juliasse LE","firstName":"Luiz Eduardo","lastName":"Juliasse","initials":"LE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-9697-4777"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Basic and Clinical Translational Sciences, Tufts University School of Dental Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Dos Santos Pereira R","firstName":"Rodrigo","lastName":"Dos Santos Pereira","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2509-8633"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery, Universidade Federal do Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5775-0222"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2509-8633"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-9697-4777"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4182405,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Clinical and translational science","medlineAbbreviation":"Clin Transl Sci","isoabbreviation":"Clin Transl Sci","nlmid":"101474067","essn":"1752-8062","issn":"1752-8054"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e70566","affiliation":"Department of Basic and Clinical Translational Sciences, Tufts University School of Dental Medicine, Boston, Massachusetts, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Oral Cancer","Chatbots"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/cts.70566"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13111917"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13111917?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-01"},{"id":"42286516","source":"MED","pmid":"42286516","doi":"10.1186/s12888-026-08288-3","title":"Psychotic episode concurrent with interaction with a large language model (llm): a case report.","authorString":"Başaran AS, Coşar B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Başaran AS","firstName":"Ahmet Selim","lastName":"Başaran","initials":"AS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9954-3085"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Unye State Hospital, Ordu, Turkey. ahmetselimbasaran@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Coşar B","firstName":"Behcet","lastName":"Coşar","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, Gazi University, Ankara, Turkey."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9954-3085"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4203808,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"BMC psychiatry","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Psychiatry","isoabbreviation":"BMC Psychiatry","nlmid":"100968559","essn":"1471-244X","issn":"1471-244X"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language model (LLM)-based chat systems generate fluent, second-person, turn-by-turn dialogue that may be anthropomorphized by users. In psychotic-spectrum vulnerability, neutral guidance may be personalized and misread as intentional \"signals,\" potentially lowering the threshold for referential interpretation.<h4>Case presentation</h4>A 33-year-old man with schizophrenia and a prior history of persecutory and referential themes had been stable on oral paliperidone 9 mg/day. Approximately 20 days before presentation, he began using ChatGPT for everyday tasks. After querying the system about home and device monitoring, he received generic safety tips (e.g., review microphone/camera/location permissions; check Wi-Fi security; inspect for unknown devices; update operating system; use strong passwords). Within days, he interpreted repeated keywords and typographical emphasis as \"hidden signs\" and personalized warnings (\"my file has been opened\"), which escalated into checking behaviors (repeated outlet/device inspections; toggling app permissions) and ultimately led to self-discontinuation of paliperidone in order to \"read the signals\" more clearly. On Day 0, he was alert and cooperative; persecutory and referential content dominated his thought, with markedly limited insight; Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS) total score was 102, Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS) score was 58; basic laboratory investigations and a prior brain MRI were unremarkable. Management included psychoeducation emphasizing that similar interpretations had occurred during previous relapses and that abrupt medication cessation likely contributed to symptom escalation; oral paliperidone was reinstated at the prior effective dose.<h4>Conclusions</h4>In this case, neutral safety statements produced by an LLM were seemingly reinterpreted as personally targeted messages, likely through the combined influence of anthropomorphic attributions, aberrant salience, and reasoning biases. Incorporating structured assessments of digital media use, including AI chatbot interactions, into routine psychiatric evaluations, explicitly addressing misinterpretations in the therapeutic dialogue, and maintaining continuity of effective antipsychotic treatment may help reduce the risk of similar text-centered personalizations and their contribution to relapse in psychosis.<h4>Clinical trial number</h4>Not applicable.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Unye State Hospital, Ordu, Turkey. ahmetselimbasaran@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Schizophrenia","Anthropomorphism","Reasoning Biases","Aberrant Salience","Persecutory Delusions","Ideas Of Reference","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12888-026-08288-3"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-12"},{"id":"42388443","source":"MED","pmid":"42388443","pmcid":"PMC13318555","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13318555"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.ccrj.2026.100194","title":"A pragmatic risk-stratified framework for using large language models in intensive care medicine: A narrative review.","authorString":"Devanand NA, Verghese S, Bacchi S, Bain C, Subramaniam A, Sundararajan K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Devanand NA","firstName":"Nilesh Anand","lastName":"Devanand","initials":"NA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0742-520X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Royal Adelaide Hospital, Port Road, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Verghese S","firstName":"Santosh","lastName":"Verghese","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Flinders Medical Centre, Southern Adelaide Local Health Network, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Office of the Chief Medical Information Officer, Digital Health, Adelaide, South Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Bacchi S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Bacchi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5130-8628"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Neurology, Lyell McEwin Hospital, Haydown Road, Elizabeth Vale, South Australia, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Bain C","firstName":"Christopher","lastName":"Bain","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5281-2364"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Digital Health, Faculty of Information Technology, Monash University, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Subramaniam A","firstName":"Ashwin","lastName":"Subramaniam","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8292-7357"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care Medicine, Monash Health, Dandenong, Victoria, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Peninsula Health, Frankston, Victoria, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Peninsula Clinical School, Monash University, Frankston, Victoria, Australia."},{"affiliation":"Australian and New Zealand Intensive Care Research Centre, Department of Epidemiology and Preventive Medicine, Monash University, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Sundararajan K","firstName":"Krishnaswamy","lastName":"Sundararajan","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3377-6062"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Royal Adelaide Hospital, Port Road, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."},{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, The University of Adelaide, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5130-8628"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0742-520X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3377-6062"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5281-2364"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8292-7357"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"28","journalIssueId":4219710,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"Critical care and resuscitation : journal of the Australasian Academy of Critical Care Medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Crit Care Resusc","isoabbreviation":"Crit Care Resusc","nlmid":"100888170","essn":"2652-9335","issn":"1441-2772"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100194","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To provide Australian intensive care clinicians with a pragmatic framework for the safe integration of large language models (LLMs) into intensive care unit (ICU) practise, addressing the current lack of Australian-specific guidance and limited local evidence.<h4>Design</h4>Narrative review.<h4>Data sources</h4>Peer-reviewed publications, preprints, and policy documents relating to LLM use in health care, with a focus on critical care applications and governance.<h4>Review methods</h4>Evidence and expert commentary were synthesised to develop a clinician-led, risk-stratified framework for ICU implementation, with emphasis on safety, oversight, and applicability within Australian health systems. Clinical use cases, risks, governance considerations, and practical safeguards for day-to-day ICU practise were identified.<h4>Results</h4>LLMs have potential utility in data-dense ICU environments, including summarising complex clinical information, supporting documentation, assisting clinical reasoning, and facilitating research tasks. However, evidence for LLM performance in ICU contexts remains limited, particularly in Australia. Key risks include inaccurate or fabricated outputs (\"<i>hallucinations</i>\"), bias, lack of situational awareness, privacy concerns, and over-reliance in high-stakes decision-making. We propose a risk-stratified framework that categorises LLM applications by clinical risk and reversibility, and aligns each category with proportional oversight, verification processes, and governance safeguards, emphasising clinician-in-the-loop decision-making.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLMs may serve as adjunctive cognitive tools in Australian ICUs when used in clearly defined, low-to intermediate-risk contexts under clinician oversight. Safe integration requires robust governance frameworks emphasising transparency, data protection, and proportionate clinician decision-making. Further Australian-based evaluation is needed before high-risk clinical applications can be considered for routine practise.","affiliation":"Department of Intensive Care, Royal Adelaide Hospital, Port Road, Adelaide, South Australia, Australia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","intensive care unit","Clinical Decision Support","Health Governance","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccrj.2026.100194"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13318555"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13318555?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42170120","source":"MED","pmid":"42170120","pmcid":"PMC13189089","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13189089"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.107391","title":"Conversational Artificial Intelligence in Medical Education: A Scoping Review.","authorString":"Aktar T, Farquhar RJ, Jacobs C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Aktar T","firstName":"Tasniya","lastName":"Aktar","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9838-3967"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Farquhar RJ","firstName":"Robert J","lastName":"Farquhar","initials":"RJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7028-112X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR."}]}},{"fullName":"Jacobs C","firstName":"Chris","lastName":"Jacobs","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, University of Bath, Bath, GBR."},{"affiliation":"Postgraduate Medical Education, Great Western Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, Swindon, GBR."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9838-3967"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7028-112X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4172604,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e107391","abstractText":"Conversational artificial intelligence (AI), encompassing chatbots and large language models (LLMs), is rapidly emerging in the sphere of medical education as a dynamic tool for interactive learning. By generating realistic dialogue, simulating patient encounters, and providing adaptive feedback, these systems create new possibilities for learners to practise communication, clinical reasoning, and decision-making in a flexible and accessible way. Yet, despite increasing enthusiasm, evidence regarding their educational value remains fragmented. This scoping review examines the existing literature on conversational AI in undergraduate medical education, focusing on three key domains: educational utility, technology usability, and fidelity. A comprehensive search was conducted across PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science in August 2025. After deduplication, 496 unique studies were screened, and 20 met the inclusion criteria. These studies employed diverse methodologies and evaluation approaches. Methodological rigour was assessed using a validated framework designed for medical education research.  Across the literature, conversational AI demonstrates considerable potential to enhance engagement, support self-directed learning, and expand access to experiential practice. Learners generally view these systems as intuitive and motivating, and many studies suggest benefits for clinical reasoning and communication training. However, limitations in reliability, realism, and technical accuracy persist, and outcome measures remain inconsistent. Few studies assess the impact of response latency or the long-term transfer of skills to clinical settings, and methodological rigour is often modest. Overall, conversational AI appears to be a promising adjunct to traditional medical teaching rather than a replacement. Its value lies in scalability, interactivity, and adaptability, but effective integration requires thoughtful design, validated evaluation frameworks, and ongoing human oversight. As technology advances, further research should focus on standardising assessment methods, exploring learning outcomes beyond user satisfaction, and addressing fidelity and responsiveness to ensure meaningful, safe, and sustainable implementation within modern medical curricula.","affiliation":"Faculty of Life Sciences and Medicine, King's College London, London, GBR.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial Intelligence In Medicine","Medical Education Technology","Chatbots","Medical Education Curriculum","Conversational Artificial Intelligence"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.107391"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189089"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189089?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-22","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-20"},{"id":"42284599","source":"MED","pmid":"42284599","pmcid":"PMC13263014","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13263014"]},"doi":"10.2196/84296","title":"Breaking Barriers in Student Mental Health Care With AI-Enhanced Group Cognitive Behavioral Therapy: Pilot Feasibility Study.","authorString":"Tulbure BT, Spătaru B, Rusu A, Faur D, Precupaș D, Trancă LM, Popa ȘG, Spătaru A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tulbure BT","firstName":"Bogdan Tudor","lastName":"Tulbure","initials":"BT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8101-1753"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, West University of Timisoara, 4 Vasile Parvan Bvd, Timisoara, 300223, Romania, 40 745-753-061."}]}},{"fullName":"Spătaru B","firstName":"Bianca","lastName":"Spătaru","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4039-385X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute for Advanced Environmental Research, West University of Timisoara, Timisoara, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Rusu A","firstName":"Andrei","lastName":"Rusu","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0036-4149"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, West University of Timisoara, 4 Vasile Parvan Bvd, Timisoara, 300223, Romania, 40 745-753-061."}]}},{"fullName":"Faur D","firstName":"Darian","lastName":"Faur","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7086-9444"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, West University of Timisoara, 4 Vasile Parvan Bvd, Timisoara, 300223, Romania, 40 745-753-061."}]}},{"fullName":"Precupaș D","firstName":"Daniel","lastName":"Precupaș","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-6995-9932"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, West University of Timisoara, Timisoara, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Trancă LM","firstName":"Loredana Marcela","lastName":"Trancă","initials":"LM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4560-0571"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Work, West University of Timisoara, Timisoara, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Popa ȘG","firstName":"Șerban Gabriel","lastName":"Popa","initials":"ȘG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4816-3372"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, West University of Timisoara, 4 Vasile Parvan Bvd, Timisoara, 300223, Romania, 40 745-753-061."}]}},{"fullName":"Spătaru A","firstName":"Adrian","lastName":"Spătaru","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0702-6276"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, West University of Timisoara, Timisoara, Romania."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8101-1753"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0702-6276"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7086-9444"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0036-4149"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4039-385X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4560-0571"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-4816-3372"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-6995-9932"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4204625,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e84296","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>University students experience elevated psychological distress, with limited access to mental health services. While cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) demonstrates efficacy for anxiety and depression, treatment gaps persist due to access barriers and insufficient between-session support. Large language model (LLM) chatbots could improve and scale CBT delivery. However, the scientific evaluation of chatbot-enhanced protocols is just emerging.<h4>Objective</h4>This pilot study aimed to assess the feasibility, acceptability, and preliminary efficacy of an LLM-based ChatBot as an adjunct to group Unified Protocol (UP) therapy for between-session support in university students with subclinical anxiety and depression symptoms.<h4>Methods</h4>A single-arm feasibility trial recruited university students aged 18 years and older with moderate subclinical symptoms (Social Phobia Inventory: 21-40, Patient Health Questionnaire-9: 5-14, or Generalized Anxiety Disorder-7: 5-14), excluding those with current psychiatric disorders, suicidal ideation, or psychotropic medication use. The intervention comprised 4 weekly group UP counseling sessions complemented by an adjunctive Claude 3.7-Sonnet LLM ChatBot programmed with UP-based therapeutic prompts for between-session support rather than a stand-alone therapeutic agent. Primary feasibility outcomes included treatment adherence, chatbot engagement metrics, and system usability (System Usability Scale). Secondary outcomes assessed changes in generalized anxiety (Generalized Anxiety Disorder-7 Scale), social anxiety (Social Phobia Inventory), depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9), and well-being (Short Warwick-Edinburgh Mental Wellbeing Scale) using paired t tests. Qualitative feedback was collected through focus group interviews and analyzed using thematic analysis.<h4>Results</h4>Of 72 screened participants, 37 met eligibility criteria and 19 initiated treatment (mean age 22.06, SD 1.78 years; 70.6% female). Retention was high with 17 completers (10.5% dropout rate). Among completers, 94.1% (16/17) attended ≥3 group sessions. The engagement with the CBT ChatBot was substantial: participants were active on a median of 23 days during the 34-day study period and exchanged a median of 15 messages in total. System usability was rated as excellent (mean 84.94, SD 10.98 out of 100). Pre-to-post comparisons revealed significant improvements in generalized anxiety (mean change -3.00, SD 3.46; t16=3.01, P=.004; Cohen d=0.71) and mental well-being (mean change +2.29, SD 3.65; t16=-2.17, P=.02; Cohen d=0.69). Social anxiety and depression showed nonsignificant trends toward improvement. Qualitative feedback highlighted the CBT ChatBot's accessibility and nonjudgmental support while noting limitations in personalization. No adverse events or inappropriate chatbot interactions occurred.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Augmenting a group UP therapy with an LLM ChatBot demonstrated high feasibility, acceptability, and preliminary efficacy signals for university students with subclinical symptoms. The hybrid intervention package achieved strong retention and engagement while maintaining safety. These findings support progression to a randomized controlled trial to definitively evaluate this technology-enhanced approach for expanding access to evidence-based mental health interventions.","affiliation":"Department of Psychology, West University of Timisoara, 4 Vasile Parvan Bvd, Timisoara, 300223, Romania, 40 745-753-061.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Feasibility Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pilot Projects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health Services","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Psychotherapy, Group","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Students","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Universities","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Young Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Surveys and Questionnaires"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cognitive Behavioral Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mental Health Teletherapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Cognitive Behavioral Therapy","university students","Transdiagnostic","Chatbots","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/84296"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263014"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13263014?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-12"},{"id":"42351930","source":"MED","pmid":"42351930","pmcid":"PMC13295611","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13295611"]},"doi":"10.3390/bioengineering13060686","title":"An Agentic LLM Framework for Autonomous Surgical Continuum Monitoring: ReAct-Driven Tool-Use Agents for Presurgical, Intraoperative, and Postsurgical Cardiopulmonary Care.","authorString":"Pylarinou C, Gortzis L, Leivaditis V, Liolis E, Antzoulas A, Papadoulas S, Nikolakopoulos K, Panagiotopoulos I, Mitsos S, Tomos P, Koletsis E, Mulita F.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pylarinou C","firstName":"Charalampia","lastName":"Pylarinou","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9421-9550"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering & Biomedical Technology, University of Patras, 26500 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Gortzis L","firstName":"Lefteris","lastName":"Gortzis","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Leivaditis V","firstName":"Vasileios","lastName":"Leivaditis","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8692-0059"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiothoracic and Vascular Surgery, Westpfalz Klinikum, 67655 Kaiserslautern, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Liolis E","firstName":"Elias","lastName":"Liolis","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Oncology, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Antzoulas A","firstName":"Andreas","lastName":"Antzoulas","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Papadoulas S","firstName":"Spyros","lastName":"Papadoulas","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8628-2173"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Vascular Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Nikolakopoulos K","firstName":"Konstantinos","lastName":"Nikolakopoulos","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Vascular Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Panagiotopoulos I","firstName":"Ioannis","lastName":"Panagiotopoulos","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiac Surgery, Ippokrateio General Hospital of Athens, 11527 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Mitsos S","firstName":"Sofoklis","lastName":"Mitsos","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0606-7287"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Attikon Hospital, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, 12462 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Tomos P","firstName":"Periklis","lastName":"Tomos","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Attikon Hospital, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, 12462 Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Koletsis E","firstName":"Efstratios","lastName":"Koletsis","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1660-4047"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiothoracic Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Mulita F","firstName":"Francesk","lastName":"Mulita","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7198-2628"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, University of Patras, 26504 Patras, Greece."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7198-2628"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8628-2173"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8692-0059"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0606-7287"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9421-9550"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1660-4047"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4220338,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Bioengineering (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Bioengineering (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Bioengineering (Basel)","nlmid":"101676056","essn":"2306-5354","issn":"2306-5354"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"686","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Rule-based multi-agent system (MAS) architectures for healthcare coordination rely on hardcoded decision trees that cannot generalise to novel clinical scenarios or self-correct reasoning errors. These limitations are acute in surgical continuum care, where patients traverse presurgical risk stratification, intraoperative monitoring, postsurgical ICU, ward care, and remote rehabilitation over days to weeks-a complexity no fixed-policy agent architecture can address without prohibitive rule engineering.<h4>Objective</h4>We present the first agentic large language model (LLM) framework for autonomous end-to-end surgical continuum monitoring, superseding the prior rule-based MAS Digital Twin. Six ReAct-driven tool-use agents replace fixed-policy agents with dynamic reasoning, multi-hop evidence retrieval, and Reflexion self-correction while maintaining mandatory confidence-gated Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) gating at every care-pathway-modifying decision.<h4>Methods</h4>The framework is grounded in the ReAct paradigm and Reflexion self-evaluation, embedded within the DETER Digital Twin state engine S(t). Each agent is specified by a ReAct loop signature, a ten-function clinical tool registry, and confidence-gated HITL escalation logic. Inter-agent coordination replaces the rule-based Priority Queue Manager with an LLM-mediated Coordination Supervisor Agent reasoning over competing resource requests.<h4>Results</h4>The framework delivers: (i) six formally specified ReAct-loop agents with explicit tool registries and authorisation boundaries; (ii) a confidence-gated HITL architecture that reduces alert fatigue while preserving safety for ambiguous clinical scenarios; (iii) an extended conflict resolution function P(p,t,context) incorporating surgical phase and DETER deterioration trajectory gradient; (iv) Reflexion self-correction with a formal N_max = 2 termination condition and Clinical Factuality Verification Layer; and (v) a multi-phase Digital Twin state engine extending S(t) to the full surgical continuum.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The proposed framework represents a fundamental architectural departure from rule-based clinical AI-from hardcoded policies to dynamic reasoning, from static retrieval to multi-hop tool-use chains, and from fixed escalation thresholds to confidence-gated self-evaluation-providing a formally specified, clinically deployable foundation for next-generation autonomous surgical care coordination.","affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering & Biomedical Technology, University of Patras, 26500 Patras, Greece.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Decision Support","Postsurgical Monitoring","Reflexion","Digital Twin","Hitl","Agentic Ai","Autonomous Healthcare Ai","Deter","React Framework","Surgical Continuum","Tool-Use Llm Agents"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering13060686"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295611"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295611?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-15"},{"id":"42261384","source":"MED","pmid":"42261384","pmcid":"PMC13242867","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13242867"]},"doi":"10.1093/burnst/tkag026","title":"Large language models in emergency and critical care medicine: a comprehensive review of applications, challenges, and future directions.","authorString":"Yang J, Yang S, Shao Z, Chen T, Shen H, Zhou P, Xia B, Lei X, Wang L, Xue D, Zheng S, Yu Y, Zhang Z.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang J","firstName":"Jie","lastName":"Yang","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yang S","firstName":"Suibi","lastName":"Yang","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shao Z","firstName":"Ziyao","lastName":"Shao","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Smart Manufacturing in Energy Chemical Process, Ministry of Education, East China University of Science and Technology, 130 Meilong Road, Xuhui District, Shanghai 200237, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen T","firstName":"Tianqi","lastName":"Chen","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shen H","firstName":"Hongjie","lastName":"Shen","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhou P","firstName":"Pengmin","lastName":"Zhou","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xia B","firstName":"Boming","lastName":"Xia","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lei X","firstName":"Xiong","lastName":"Lei","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang L","firstName":"Lihui","lastName":"Wang","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Renji Hospital, Shanghai Jiaotong University School of Medicine, 160 Pujian Road, Pudong New District, Shanghai 200001, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xue D","firstName":"Dong","lastName":"Xue","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Smart Manufacturing in Energy Chemical Process, Ministry of Education, East China University of Science and Technology, 130 Meilong Road, Xuhui District, Shanghai 200237, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zheng S","firstName":"Shaojiang","lastName":"Zheng","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2323-0736"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Emergency and Trauma of Ministry of Education, Engineering Research Center for Hainan Biological Sample Resources of Major Diseases, the First Affiliated Hospital, Hainan Medical University, 31 Longhua Road, Longhua District, Haikou 570102, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu Y","firstName":"Yuetian","lastName":"Yu","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0193-4046"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Critical Care Medicine, Renji Hospital, Shanghai Jiaotong University School of Medicine, 160 Pujian Road, Pudong New District, Shanghai 200001, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Z","firstName":"Zhongheng","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Z","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2336-5323"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."},{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Precision Medicine in Diagnosis and Monitoring Research of Zhejiang Province, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China."},{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Shaoxing University, 508 Huancheng West Road, Yuecheng District, Shaoxing 312000, China."},{"affiliation":"Longquan Industrial Innovation Research Institute, No. 2 Industrial Road, Longyuan Street, Longquan City, Lishui 323799, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0193-4046"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2323-0736"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2336-5323"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4089155,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Burns & trauma","medlineAbbreviation":"Burns Trauma","isoabbreviation":"Burns Trauma","nlmid":"101651457","essn":"2321-3876","issn":"2321-3868"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"tkag026","abstractText":"Emergency and critical care medicine requires the rapid synthesis of heterogeneous clinical data under extreme time constraints. Early artificial intelligence tools lacked the flexibility to manage real-world patient heterogeneity. Large language models (LLMs) offer a paradigm shift by demonstrating advanced natural language understanding, cross-task generalization, and context-sensitive reasoning, thereby bridging the gap between fragmented algorithms and holistic clinical decision support. The effective deployment of these models is grounded in four methodological pillars: domain adaptation, knowledge integration, multimodal and temporal modeling, and transparency. Domain adaptation and knowledge integration specifically empower the context-sensitive reasoning required for high-stakes intensive care. This theoretical framework enables their application across clinical decision support, documentation optimization, medical education, and clinical research. Integrating continuous physiological waveforms with multi-omics data facilitates dynamic risk stratification for complex conditions like sepsis, while natural language-to-structured query language capabilities accelerate clinical data extraction and quality improvement. The transition of LLMs from experimental settings to routine clinical deployment remains constrained by model hallucinations, multimodal integration barriers, and unresolved ethical governance. Sustainable implementation requires a human-in-the-loop copilot design, rigorous multicenter prospective validation, and transparent regulatory frameworks. Addressing these challenges is essential to ensure that technological innovations safely translate into measurable improvements in patient survival and clinical outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Emergency Medicine, Sir Run Run Shaw Hospital, Zhejiang University School of Medicine, 3 Qingchun East Road, Shangcheng District, Hangzhou 310016, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"ZJDXLQCXZCJBGS2024016","agency":"Project of Zhejiang University Longquan Innovation Center","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2023YFC3603104","agency":"China National Key Research and Development Program","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2025ZD01902500","agency":"Prevention and Control of Emerging and Major Infectious Diseases–National Science and Technology Major Project","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2025ZD01902501","agency":"Prevention and Control of Emerging and Major Infectious Diseases–National Science and Technology Major Project","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"LHDMD24H150001","agency":"Huadong Medicine Joint Funds of the Zhejiang Provincial Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"226-2025-00024","agency":"Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"82272180","agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"GZY-ZJ-KJ-24082","agency":"the Science and Technology Department of the National Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine and the Zhejiang Provincial Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"320.6750.2024-23-07","agency":"Wu Jieping Medical Foundation Special Research Grant","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"82472243","agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2024KY1099","agency":"General Health Science and Technology Program of Zhejiang Province","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2024C03240","agency":"Key Research &amp; Development Project of Zhejiang Province","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2026JKZ042","agency":"Zhejiang Provincial Science and Technology Program for Disease Control and Prevention","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision Support","Precision Medicine","Multimodal Integration","Emergency And Critical Care Medicine","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/burnst/tkag026"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13242867"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13242867?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-23"},{"id":"42212181","source":"MED","pmid":"42212181","pmcid":"PMC13212166","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13212166"]},"doi":"10.2147/jmdh.s581251","title":"Cybersecurity and Privacy Risks of Generative AI Mental-Health Chatbots: A Systematic Review and Regulatory Framework.","authorString":"Sawesi S, Sabbineni H, Shagamreddy RR, Rashrash B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sawesi S","firstName":"Suhila","lastName":"Sawesi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sabbineni H","firstName":"Hemalatha","lastName":"Sabbineni","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Shagamreddy RR","firstName":"Rajashekhar Reddy","lastName":"Shagamreddy","initials":"RR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Rashrash B","firstName":"Budur","lastName":"Rashrash","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, West Virginia School of Osteopathic Medicine, Lewisburg, WV, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3510-8543"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-9517-7889"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091825,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of multidisciplinary healthcare","medlineAbbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","isoabbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","nlmid":"101512691","essn":"1178-2390","issn":"1178-2390"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"581251","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>Large language models (LLMs) and other generative artificial intelligence systems are increasingly used in mental health care for psychoeducation, emotional support, screening, and crisis-related interactions. To our knowledge, this is the first structured synthesis explicitly mapping LLM-specific cybersecurity and privacy risks to Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) regulatory frameworks. We aimed to characterize deployment patterns, identify multi-layered risks, and evaluate alignment of reported safeguards with established healthcare governance standards.<h4>Methods</h4>A PRISMA-guided systematic review was conducted using PubMed, APA PsycNet, and Google Scholar. After screening eligible records against predefined inclusion criteria, 33 studies were included. Two reviewers independently extracted data on application domains, deployment settings, risk categories, attack surfaces, data sensitivity, and reported or recommended controls.<h4>Results</h4>Generative AI chatbots were most frequently used for therapy or emotional support (13/33, 39.4%), followed by safety evaluation or benchmarking (9/33, 27.3%) and psychoeducation or advice (6/33, 18.2%). Suicide prevention or crisis detection was the most common domain (10/33, 30.3%). Most systems relied on general-purpose LLMs (21/33, 63.6%) and were deployed via consumer-facing platforms (16/33, 48.5%). Key risks included harmful or unsafe outputs, failures in crisis response, exposure of sensitive personal information, and limited transparency. Critically, 78.8% of studies (26/33) were rated high risk for cybersecurity evaluation rigor, indicating that formal adversarial testing and structured threat modeling remain rare.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Current governance frameworks have not fully adapted to generative conversational AI in mental health contexts. Because the therapeutic interface functions as a primary attack surface, single-layer security evaluation (assessing only software validation or content safety in isolation) is inadequate. More comprehensive approaches are needed, including stronger cybersecurity controls, privacy-preserving data practices, and explicit alignment with FDA SaMD guidance, HIPAA, ISO 14971, and the NIST AI Risk Management Framework.","affiliation":"Department of Information Sciences and Technologies, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, Grand Rapids, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["risk management","Clinical Safety","Data Protection","Regulatory Frameworks","Digital Therapeutics","Conversational Systems"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S581251"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13212166?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-31","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"42328270","source":"MED","pmid":"42328270","pmcid":"PMC13275664","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13275664"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1730457","title":"Designing and evaluating large language model-enabled clinical decision support for heart failure: a modular and risk-tiered framework.","authorString":"Zhu W, Peng J, Yan Z, Chen Y, Xu J, Zhang L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhu W","firstName":"Wenfang","lastName":"Zhu","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Peng J","firstName":"Jin","lastName":"Peng","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"West China College of Basic Medicine and Forensic Medicine, Sichuan University, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan Z","firstName":"Zhi","lastName":"Yan","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Transformation of Scientific and Technological Achievements, Capital Medical University, Beijing, China."},{"affiliation":"School of Medical Technology, Sichuan College of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen Y","firstName":"Yuhong","lastName":"Chen","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu J","firstName":"Jinpeng","lastName":"Xu","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang L","firstName":"Liang","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiac Surgery, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1730457","abstractText":"Heart failure (HF) care requires repeated decisions across suspected disease, diagnostic confirmation, phenotyping, guideline-directed medical therapy, device consideration, worsening HF, transition care, and advanced HF planning. Large language models (LLMs) may support this work by synthesizing structured and unstructured electronic health record data, retrieving current evidence, and presenting patient-specific reasoning. However, an HF-specific LLM clinical decision support system should not be framed as a single autonomous agent. We present the Heart Failure Intelligent Agent (HF-IA) as a modular, risk-tiered conceptual framework in which agent functions have different data requirements, reference standards, risk levels, and validation pathways. We argue that evaluation should combine node-level tests, longitudinal case replay, silent prospective validation, and post-deployment monitoring. This framework is conceptual and does not claim clinical effectiveness; its value is to clarify design, evaluation, and governance requirements for future LLM-enabled HF decision support.","affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Anhui Chest Hospital, Anhui, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Heart Failure","Clinical Decision Support","Large Language Model","Retrieval-augmented Generation","Gold-standard Case Replay","Risk-tiered Autonomy"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1730457"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275664"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13275664?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-22","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-04"},{"id":"42140617","source":"MED","pmid":"42140617","pmcid":"PMC13221620","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13221620"]},"doi":"10.2196/80416","title":"A Multiassessment and Multiprofessional Agents Approach for Medical Chatbot Risk Estimation: Development and Evaluation Study.","authorString":"Tamayo LPV, Nishiyama T, Peng S, Wakamiya S, Aramaki E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tamayo LPV","firstName":"Lenard Paulo Velasco","lastName":"Tamayo","initials":"LPV","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4992-7240"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma-shi, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nishiyama T","firstName":"Tomohiro","lastName":"Nishiyama","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1538-8266"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma-shi, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Peng S","firstName":"Shaowen","lastName":"Peng","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4020-9100"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma-shi, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Wakamiya S","firstName":"Shoko","lastName":"Wakamiya","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9371-1340"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma-shi, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Aramaki E","firstName":"Eiji","lastName":"Aramaki","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0201-3609"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma-shi, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4992-7240"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9371-1340"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0201-3609"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1538-8266"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4020-9100"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4190202,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical informatics","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Inform","nlmid":"101645109","essn":"2291-9694","issn":"2291-9694"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e80416","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Assessing chatbot responses across 3 domains-medical, ethical, and legal-is essential to ensuring the safe use of artificial intelligence in health care. Although advancements in the use of large language models (LLMs) show significant improvements in evaluating question-answer datasets, such as multiple-choice medical exams, existing systems use general LLMs without incorporating specialized domain knowledge. They rely on standardized instructions without integrating real-world information, and ensemble methods such as majority voting fail to resolve disagreements among agents, resulting in misclassification and challenges in risk assessment.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aims to design, develop, and evaluate a synergistic approach for assessing risks associated with chatbot responses using multiassessment (MA) and multiprofessional agents (MPAs).<h4>Methods</h4>We designed and developed an approach consisting of MA and MPA, specifically initial assessment (MA1), which internalizes 3 roles and provides an initial risk estimation, and final assessment (MA3), which aims to reach a final consensus based on the previous assessments (MA1 and MA2), with each using 1 LLM. The verification assessment (MA2) incorporates an MPA or role-based LLM specialized agents for each risk domain (medical, ethical, and legal). We evaluated the proposed approach using the MedNLP-CHAT (Medical Natural Language Processing for AI Chat) corpus (N=226; 100 train, 126 test), covering baseline, enhanced prompt, embedding-based search, and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). Primary metrics included macro F<sub>1</sub>-score and joint accuracy to evaluate system performance, along with CI and paired macro F<sub>1</sub>-score difference (Δ) as supporting metrics to assess the approach's effectiveness.<h4>Results</h4>The MA-MPA framework integrated with RAG achieved the highest average macro F<sub>1</sub>-score of 0.800 across risk domains and a joint accuracy of 76 (60.3%) correct predictions across all risk domains out of 126 question-answer pairs, with notable improvements over the best reported Eighteenth NII Testbeds and Community for Information Access Research Project (NTCIR-18) MedNLP-CHAT systems in the ethical (+0.252) and legal (+0.096) risk domains, while the medical domain showed a modest increase of +0.070. The MA approach contributed the largest gains, particularly from MA1 to MA2, with paired macro F<sub>1</sub>-score gains ranging from +0.176 to +0.214 across systems. The MPA approach performed better when integrated with MA and external knowledge, with paired bootstrap estimates showing a gain of +0.037 (95% CI 0.003-0.074) over baseline; however, joint accuracy gains were not evident (95% CI -2.9% to 7.7%), and gains relative to the enhanced prompt were small. Notably, MA alone achieved higher joint accuracy than RAG (62.7% vs 60.3%), indicating a metric-specific trade-off rather than consistent superiority across all metrics.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The MA-MPA approach shows potential for improving risk estimation in chatbot responses. The results suggest that the framework is particularly useful for enhancing balanced overall performance, especially when combined with external knowledge, although the medical risk domain remains challenging. Furthermore, more specialized LLMs may further improve contextually grounded risk estimation.","affiliation":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Ikoma-shi, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Assessment","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Natural Language Processing","Large Language Model","Medical Question And Answer","Multiassessment","Multiprofessional Agent"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/80416"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13221620"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13221620?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-17","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-15"},{"id":"42536834","source":"MED","pmid":"42536834","doi":"10.2196/98822","title":"Perspectives of Individuals With Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder on the Role of Artificial Intelligence in Therapy and Treatment: Thematic Qualitative Study.","authorString":"Mokhtar D, Wang H, Garland EC, Shakya D, Occhino-Moede L, Liu X, Frank AC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mokhtar D","firstName":"Daniel","lastName":"Mokhtar","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0011-5963"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang H","firstName":"Harrison","lastName":"Wang","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9941-6895"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}},{"fullName":"Garland EC","firstName":"Emma C","lastName":"Garland","initials":"EC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6799-925X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}},{"fullName":"Shakya D","firstName":"Dejan","lastName":"Shakya","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0281-3534"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}},{"fullName":"Occhino-Moede L","firstName":"Lucas","lastName":"Occhino-Moede","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-8450-5534"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu X","firstName":"Xiao","lastName":"Liu","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-9406-5301"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}},{"fullName":"Frank AC","firstName":"Adam Charles","lastName":"Frank","initials":"AC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8203-8480"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8203-8480"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9941-6895"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0281-3534"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-9406-5301"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-8450-5534"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6799-925X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0011-5963"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"18","journalIssueId":4229067,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of participatory medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Particip Med","isoabbreviation":"J Particip Med","nlmid":"101539422","essn":"2152-7202","issn":"2152-7202"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e98822","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>AI has become increasingly used in mental health care for applications such as diagnosis, monitoring, and treatment support. These include tools like clinician support systems, large language models, and conversational agents used to augment psychotherapy and clinical decision-making. While prior research suggests potential benefits of and concerns with AI, little is known within the domain of obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). Given the expanding role of AI in psychiatry, understanding these perspectives is essential to ensuring AI implementation aligns with patient priorities and values.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aims to explore the perspectives of individuals with OCD on the use of AI in health care, including perceived benefits, risks, and its role in relation to human clinicians.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted semistructured interviews with 24 adults self-reporting OCD, recruited through online communities and advocacy networks. Eligible individuals (≥18 y with self-reported OCD) completed screening, provided informed consent, and participated in remote Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)-compliant Zoom (Zoom Communications, Inc) interviews (May-December 2024). Transcripts were deidentified, open-coded, and used to develop a codebook. Focused codes were applied using a thematic analysis framework in Dedoose (v9.2.22; Sociocultural Research Consultants, LLC). Each transcript was independently coded by 2 reviewers, with discrepancies resolved through consensus. Themes were developed through iterative interpretive analysis of code clusters.<h4>Results</h4>Participants' perspectives encompassed concerns and benefits of AI in mental health care. Participants expressed concerns about the accuracy and efficacy of information provided by AI, as well as a limited ability for clinical judgment in psychiatric care. Additionally, participants emphasized the importance of human connection, particularly therapeutic alliance, empathy, and reassurance provided by clinicians, which they felt AI could not replicate. Concerns about data privacy, security, and downstream use of information were also highlighted. Despite concerns, many endorsed the use of AI as an adjunct rather than a replacement for clinicians, noting potential benefits in symptom monitoring, preliminary information gathering, and support for administrative tasks, provided that human oversight is maintained.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Individuals with OCD expressed nuanced views on AI in mental health care, balancing cautious optimism with several concerns. While AI may improve efficiency, standardization, and symptom monitoring, participants highlighted risks related to deindividualization, accuracy, and erosion of human connection. These findings underscore the importance of patient-centered, ethically guided AI integration that preserves the therapeutic alliance while leveraging technological benefits.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, Keck School of Medicine, University of Southern California, 2500 Alcazar St, Suite 2200, Los Angeles, CA, 90033, United States, 1 323-442-6000, 1 323-442-6001.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Qualitative","AI","Ocd","Obsessive-compulsive Disorder","Patient Experience","Thematic Analysis","Llm","Chatbot","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/98822"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-31","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-31","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"41924178","source":"MED","pmid":"41924178","pmcid":"PMC13035794","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13035794"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1772274","title":"Promises and challenges of applying large language models in the healthcare domain.","authorString":"Wang Q, Gong Z, Lai Z, Bu L, Dahlweid FM, Sun H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wang Q","firstName":"Qingyu","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Gong Z","firstName":"Ziheng","lastName":"Gong","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lai Z","firstName":"Zou","lastName":"Lai","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Bu L","firstName":"Lina","lastName":"Bu","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Dahlweid FM","firstName":"Fried-Michael","lastName":"Dahlweid","initials":"FM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Dedalus Healthcare, Milan, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun H","firstName":"Hong","lastName":"Sun","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."},{"affiliation":"Sino-European Joint Lab for Health Information Processing and Applications, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1772274","abstractText":"Large language models are rapidly moving from theoretical concepts to active clinical pilots. Current approaches diverge between general-purpose models, which adapt to healthcare via prompt engineering, and domain-specific models, which prioritize deep alignment with medical knowledge graphs to ensure safety. Despite reported benefits in documentation efficiency and diagnostic reasoning, significant challenges remain regarding hallucination, privacy, and the validity of evaluation metrics. This Mini Review synthesizes current evidence, contrasts these two modeling paradigms, highlights key controversies, and maps out future development routes including retrieval-augmented generation and agentic architectures.","affiliation":"Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Perceiving and Intelligent Systems, Jiaxing University, Jiaxing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision Support","Multimodal","Large Language Models","Model Hallucinations"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1772274"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13035794"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13035794?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-17"},{"id":"42160468","source":"MED","pmid":"42160468","pmcid":"PMC13189224","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13189224"]},"doi":"10.2196/95899","title":"Large Language Models in Clinical Trial Recruitment: Sociotechnical and Economic Framework Development Study.","authorString":"Qian Q.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Qian Q","firstName":"Qian","lastName":"Qian","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-3578-4384"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Room EF625, Core F, Main Campus, Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China (Hong Kong), 86 15021400019, 86 15021400019."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-3578-4384"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["plain_english","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"5","journalIssueId":4189272,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR AI","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR AI","isoabbreviation":"JMIR AI","nlmid":"9918645789006676","essn":"2817-1705","issn":"2817-1705"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e95899","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language models (LLMs) have shown substantial promise in patient-trial matching, but most published studies still evaluate the performance under controlled technical conditions rather than within real recruitment workflows. Less is known about how LLM-enabled clinical artificial intelligence (AI) systems should be embedded into organizational settings where privacy constraints, human oversight, patient-facing concerns, and governance costs shape deployment outcomes.<h4>Objective</h4>This study develops a theory-grounded conceptual framework for analyzing how LLM-enabled clinical AI can be integrated into clinical trial recruitment workflows and how such integration affects operational, governance, and economic outcomes.<h4>Methods</h4>Using structured conceptual analysis and targeted evidence synthesis, the study draws on recent literature on LLM-based patient matching, human-AI collaboration in clinical settings, and clinical AI governance. Sociotechnical systems theory and transaction cost economics are integrated to build the LLM-Embedded Clinical Recruitment Architecture (LECRA) and to derive 6 testable propositions.<h4>Results</h4>LECRA conceptualizes recruitment as a closed-loop sociotechnical and economic system, spanning data complexity, model configuration and processing, human-AI collaboration, and economic and governance consequences. The revised framework identifies privacy constraints, hallucination risk, bias, patient trust, oversight intensity, and regulatory validation costs as key moderators of performance. It also reframes recruitment performance as a multidimensional construct and outlines an empirical roadmap for future testing.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LECRA offers a more deployment-sensitive account of when LLM-enabled recruitment is likely to create value and when the benefits may be offset by coordination, compliance, or trust-related frictions. This framework is intended to support future empirical studies and more realistic implementation decisions rather than to claim validated superiority of LLM-assisted recruitment.","affiliation":"Department of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Room EF625, Core F, Main Campus, Hung Hom, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China (Hong Kong), 86 15021400019, 86 15021400019.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Trials","Patient Selection","Governance","Sociotechnical Systems","Transaction Cost Economics","Human-ai Collaboration","Clinical Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/95899"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189224"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13189224?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-20","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"42526601","source":"MED","pmid":"42526601","doi":"10.1016/j.modpat.2026.101049","title":"End-to-End Clinical Validation of a Human-Supervised Large Language Model Agent for Enterprise Surgical Pathology Reporting.","authorString":"Abukhiran I, Mansour A, Caicedo ML, Minkowitz JM, Pantanowitz L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Abukhiran I","firstName":"Ibrahim","lastName":"Abukhiran","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1271-4110"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Pittsburgh/University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, PA. Electronic address: Abukhirani@upmc.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Mansour A","firstName":"Akila","lastName":"Mansour","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-0691-8819"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Pittsburgh/University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, PA."}]}},{"fullName":"Caicedo ML","firstName":"Martha L","lastName":"Caicedo","initials":"ML","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Pittsburgh/University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, PA."}]}},{"fullName":"Minkowitz JM","firstName":"Jeremy M","lastName":"Minkowitz","initials":"JM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Pittsburgh/University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, PA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pantanowitz L","firstName":"Liron","lastName":"Pantanowitz","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Pittsburgh/University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, PA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1271-4110"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-0691-8819"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4225578,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Modern pathology : an official journal of the United States and Canadian Academy of Pathology, Inc","medlineAbbreviation":"Mod Pathol","isoabbreviation":"Mod Pathol","nlmid":"8806605","essn":"1530-0285","issn":"0893-3952"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101049","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) show promise for text-based pathology tasks, yet most reported applications remain experimental, lack formal clinical validation, or operate outside secure, health system-approved environments. We developed and clinically validated a rule-guided, agent-based LLM that assists gastrointestinal (GI) biopsy reporting by automating report structuring while preserving full diagnostic authority with the pathologist. The AI agent (Microsoft 365 Copilot) ran within an enterprise-approved, HIPAA-compliant Microsoft 365 environment, configured with a fixed rule-based system configuration prompt and a quick-text knowledge base. In a prospective validation, 94 GI biopsy cases were evaluated by subspecialty GI pathologists using specimen container labels extracted from the laboratory information system and pathologist-entered shorthand diagnoses. Agent outputs were reviewed for formatting accuracy, organ and procedure identification, shorthand expansion fidelity, blank diagnosis enforcement, and diagnostic safety. The agent preserved specimen part structure and correctly identified organ, sub-organ, and procedure context in 100% of cases; shorthand expansion was accurate in all applicable cases. Minor formatting deviations occurred in 8 cases (8.5%) without affecting diagnostic meaning. Two cases (2%) showed minor diagnostic misinterpretation, in which descriptive container-label terms (e.g., \"ulcer,\" \"erosion\") were incorporated into diagnostic text; no hallucinated diagnoses were identified. Repeatability testing on cases enriched for descriptive labels showed 81% identical outputs across 105 runs (19% variability), with non-reproducible semantic leakage in 3% of runs. A comparative time study showed faster AI-assisted reporting (mean 39 vs 72 seconds for speech-to-text and 76 seconds for manual typing; ∼33-37 second reductions, p < 0.05), measured across the full workflow through sign-out, with lower variability. By restricting this end-to-end, production-embedded agent to rule-guided structuring, formatting, and controlled shorthand expansion while prohibiting diagnostic inference, the system achieved high efficiency, consistency, and seamless workflow integration on real GI biopsy cases. Low-frequency, stochastic errors and minor variability remain inherent to LLMs despite strict constraints; although infrequent, they indicate such systems are best suited for non-diagnostic, clerical augmentation rather than autonomous use. All output therefore requires pathologist careful review before sign-out. These findings support constrained, agent-based LLMs to safely enhance reporting efficiency while preserving diagnostic responsibility and human oversight.","affiliation":"University of Pittsburgh/University of Pittsburgh Medical Center, Pittsburgh, PA. Electronic address: Abukhirani@upmc.edu.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Validation","Human-in-the-loop Artificial Intelligence","Agent-based Large Language Model","Gastrointestinal Biopsy Pathology","Surgical Pathology Reporting Automation"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.modpat.2026.101049"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-29"},{"id":"42388248","source":"MED","pmid":"42388248","pmcid":"PMC13321024","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13321024"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.mmr.2026.100050","title":"A roadmap for medical large language models: a review of foundations, applications, and challenges.","authorString":"Sha YY, Yu L, Lin ZH, Kaur A, Lou YY, Gornale SS, Zhang TY, Wong LS, Wang ZW, Yan Y, Zhang XB, Hong R, Li K, Im SK, de Carvalho P, Tan T, Li KF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sha YY","firstName":"Yu-Yang","lastName":"Sha","initials":"YY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macau 999078, Macao SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu L","firstName":"Li","lastName":"Yu","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Oncology, Shengjing Hospital of China Medical University, Shenyang 110004, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin ZH","firstName":"Ze-Hui","lastName":"Lin","initials":"ZH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macau 999078, Macao SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaur A","firstName":"Amandeep","lastName":"Kaur","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science and Technology, School of Engineering and Technology, Central University of Punjab, Bathinda, Punjab 151401, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Lou YY","firstName":"Yan-Yan","lastName":"Lou","initials":"YY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Hematology and Oncology, Mayo Clinic, Jacksonville, FL 32224, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gornale SS","firstName":"Shivanand S","lastName":"Gornale","initials":"SS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5373-4049"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, School of Mathematics and Computing Sciences, Rani Channamma University, Belagavi, Karnataka 590016, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang TY","firstName":"Tian-Yu","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"TY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, the Netherlands Cancer Institute, Amsterdam 1066 CX, the Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Wong LS","firstName":"Ling Shing","lastName":"Wong","initials":"LS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Health and Life Sciences, INTI International University, Nilai, Negeri Sembilan 71800, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang ZW","firstName":"Zhi-Wen","lastName":"Wang","initials":"ZW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Peking University, Beijing 100191, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yan Y","firstName":"Yan","lastName":"Yan","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Guangdong-Hong-Kong-Macao University Joint Laboratory of Interventional Medicine, the Fifth Affiliated Hospital, Sun Yat-Sen University, Zhuhai 519000, Guangdong, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang XB","firstName":"Xian-Bin","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"XB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of General Surgery and Institute of Precision Diagnosis and Treatment of Digestive System Tumors, Carson International Cancer Center, Shenzhen University General Hospital, Shenzhen University, Shenzhen 518055, Guangdong, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Hong R","firstName":"Rui","lastName":"Hong","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medicine and Engineering Interdisciplinary Research Laboratory of Nursing and Materials, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, China."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Biomedical Engineering, College of Medicine, Southwest Jiaotong University, Chengdu 610031, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li K","firstName":"Ka","lastName":"Li","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medicine and Engineering Interdisciplinary Research Laboratory of Nursing and Materials, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610041, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Im SK","firstName":"Sio Kei","lastName":"Im","initials":"SK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macau 999078, Macao SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"de Carvalho P","firstName":"Paulo","lastName":"de Carvalho","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Informatics Engineering Department, Faculty of Science and Technology, University of Coimbra, 3030-290 Coimbra, Portugal."}]}},{"fullName":"Tan T","firstName":"Tao","lastName":"Tan","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macau 999078, Macao SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li KF","firstName":"Ke-Feng","lastName":"Li","initials":"KF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macau 999078, Macao SAR, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5373-4049"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"13","journalIssueId":4187217,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Military Medical Research","medlineAbbreviation":"Mil Med Res","isoabbreviation":"Mil Med Res","nlmid":"101643181","essn":"2054-9369","issn":"2095-7467"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100050","abstractText":"Medical large language models (Med-LLMs) have shown considerable promise across a broad range of clinical tasks, including decision support, medical documentation, patient communication, multimodal analysis, and telemedicine. Their rapid development has generated growing interest in how large language models (LLMs) may support healthcare practice, while also raising important questions about reliability, clinical validity, and safe deployment. This review provides a structured overview of recent progress in Med-LLMs by examining their major application areas, key challenges, and emerging future directions. Current evidence shows that the clinical usefulness of Med-LLMs cannot be judged by model performance alone. Their value in practice depends on whether they are supported by reliable evidence, remain consistent with current medical knowledge, and can be integrated into clinical workflows. Important challenges remain in evaluation, safety, knowledge updating, and real-world deployment. These issues reflect a gap between performance in controlled settings and clinical practice. Future progress will require stronger clinical validation, better alignment with medical practice, and more careful deployment across different settings. The clinical impact of Med-LLMs will depend on whether they can be used as reliable tools in clinical care.","affiliation":"Faculty of Applied Sciences, Macao Polytechnic University, Macau 999078, Macao SAR, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clinical Applications","Model evaluation","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Clinical Deployment","Medical Large Language Models (Med-llms)"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mmr.2026.100050"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13321024"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13321024?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-27"},{"id":"PPR1258597","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9991723/v1","title":"What Older Adults Need from Digital Loneliness Support: A Mixed-Methods Requirements Study","authorString":"Zhao J, Or C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zhao J","firstName":"Junyu","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Or C","firstName":"Calvin","lastName":"Or","initials":"C"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>  <bold>Background:</bold>  Late-life loneliness is a serious health risk, yet digital solutions show modest effects due to a mismatch with older adults’ needs, constraints, and preferences. Although conversational AI and large language models (LLMs) offer potential, their value hinges on usability, trust, and safe escalation rather than novelty. This study derived actionable, implementation-oriented requirements for loneliness support delivered via mobile health (mHealth) for community-dwelling older adults, including conversational or LLM-enabled delivery.  <bold>Methods:</bold>  A cross-sectional, convergent mixed-methods study was conducted with 27 community-dwelling, smartphone-using older adults aged 60 or older who screened positive for elevated loneliness. Participants completed standardized measures of loneliness, social network, depressive and anxiety symptoms, and eHealth literacy and a semi-structured interview incorporating structured choice and prioritization tasks. Quantitative data were summarized descriptively using internal consistency, Spearman correlations, and exploratory subgroup contrasts. Interviews were analyzed using Framework Analysis across six a priori domains, a personal coping and resilience domain, and cross-cutting themes. Coding was AI-assisted with full human verification, and quantitative and qualitative findings were integrated through a joint display.  <bold>Results:</bold>  Participants reported elevated loneliness (UCLA Mean = 51.3, SD = 9.3) and constrained social networks (63.0% at LSNS-6 score of 12 or less). Interviews characterized loneliness as situational solitude, relational disconnection, and functional vulnerability, with engagement shaped by strong self-reliance norms and pervasive concerns about trust and fraud. Integrating these findings yielded six implementation-oriented requirements: recognizable and verifiable contact, hybrid delivery, human-in-the-loop support, low-burden and language-concordant usability, privacy by design, and engagement fit. Exploratory correlations were directionally consistent with these patterns and are reported descriptively.  <bold>Conclusions:</bold>  Trust functioned as a precondition for engagement, and lower digital readiness tracked greater loneliness, indicating that verifiable identity and human onboarding are central, rather than peripheral, to loneliness support. These requirements provide a service-design foundation for developing and evaluating mHealth and conversational interventions, including LLM-enabled delivery, with robust safety and handoff mechanisms. Given the single-site sample and descriptive design, these requirements should be interpreted as transferable hypotheses.  </p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9991723/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1258597","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-24","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24"},{"id":"41976876","source":"MED","pmid":"41976876","pmcid":"PMC13073393","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13073393"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15072576","title":"Clinical Artificial Intelligence Agents in Nephrology: From Prediction to Action Through Workflow-Native Intelligence-A Roadmap for Workflow-Integrated Care.","authorString":"Thongprayoon C, Pesce F, Cheungpasitporn W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Thongprayoon C","firstName":"Charat","lastName":"Thongprayoon","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pesce F","firstName":"Francesco","lastName":"Pesce","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2882-4226"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Renal Medicine, Ospedale Isola Tiberina-Gemelli Isola, 00168 Rome, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Translational Medicine and Surgery, Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, 00168 Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Cheungpasitporn W","firstName":"Wisit","lastName":"Cheungpasitporn","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN 55905, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9954-9711"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2882-4226"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4179667,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2576","abstractText":"<b>Background</b>: Artificial intelligence in nephrology has largely focused on predictive models for outcomes such as acute kidney injury (AKI), chronic kidney disease (CKD) progression, and transplant complications. Although these models demonstrate technical performance, their real-world clinical impact has remained limited because prediction alone rarely translates into coordinated clinical action. Clinical artificial intelligence agents represent workflow-native systems that operate in real time, interact bidirectionally with clinical environments, adapt to evolving patient and workflow states, and support coordinated clinical action rather than generating isolated predictions. This review proposes clinical artificial intelligence agents as a new paradigm for integrating artificial intelligence directly into nephrology workflows. <b>Methods</b>: We conducted a narrative synthesis of emerging literature on artificial intelligence systems, agentic artificial intelligence architectures, clinical decision support, and digital health infrastructures relevant to kidney care. Drawing from interdisciplinary sources in medicine, health informatics, and artificial intelligence research, we developed a conceptual framework describing the architecture, governance requirements, and evaluation principles of clinical artificial intelligence agents in nephrology. <b>Results</b>: Clinical artificial intelligence agents represent workflow-integrated systems capable of continuously perceiving patient data, reasoning under clinical constraints, planning tasks, and supporting coordinated clinical actions over time. We describe a layered architecture consisting of perception, cognition, planning and control, action, and learning components. Potential applications span the nephrology care continuum, including CKD management, AKI monitoring, dialysis and continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) optimization, kidney transplantation care coordination, glomerulonephritis management, and supervised patient-facing systems. <b>Conclusions</b>: Clinical artificial intelligence agents shift the role of artificial intelligence from isolated prediction toward longitudinal clinical orchestration. Future evaluation should prioritize workflow integration, time-to-action, clinician oversight, safety, and patient-centered outcomes rather than relying solely on traditional model performance metrics. This roadmap provides a conceptual foundation for the responsible development and clinical integration of agentic artificial intelligence systems in nephrology.","affiliation":"Division of Nephrology and Hypertension, Department of Medicine, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN 55905, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Nephrology","Clinical Workflow Integration","Clinical Artificial Intelligence Agents","Conceptual Roadmap"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15072576"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073393?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-27"},{"id":"42228937","source":"MED","pmid":"42228937","pmcid":"PMC13273208","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13273208"]},"doi":"10.2196/85475","title":"Preliminary Evaluation of a Large Language Model-Powered Chatbot for Osteoporosis Self-Management Education: Formative Randomized Controlled Trial.","authorString":"Huang J, Xin X, He C, Xiong H, Pang W, Pang S, Sun W, Ding X.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang J","firstName":"Jinling","lastName":"Huang","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6004-9007"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China."},{"affiliation":"University of Glasgow, Glasgow, Scotland, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Xin X","firstName":"Xiaolian","lastName":"Xin","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-8135-8840"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"He C","firstName":"Chunyan","lastName":"He","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-5519"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xiong H","firstName":"Haoyan","lastName":"Xiong","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7604-9025"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Pang W","firstName":"Wenjun","lastName":"Pang","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3754-8498"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Pang S","firstName":"Shaobo","lastName":"Pang","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2704-8280"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun W","firstName":"Wei","lastName":"Sun","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-1801-3199"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Glasgow, Glasgow, Scotland, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Ding X","firstName":"Xianghua","lastName":"Ding","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8122-6252"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Glasgow, Glasgow, Scotland, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6004-9007"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8122-6252"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7604-9025"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2704-8280"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-8507-5519"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-1801-3199"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-3754-8498"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-8135-8840"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4204625,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e85475","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>With the increasing burden of chronic diseases, self-management education (SME) is crucial. Traditional SME based on face-to-face delivery by clinicians is resource-intensive, and general digital tools such as web-based platforms often provide limited interactivity for patient learning. Although chatbots based on large language models (LLMs) show promise in interactivity, their real-world effectiveness lacks empirical evidence.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to explore the feasibility and preliminary effectiveness of an LLM-based chatbot specifically designed for osteoporosis SME.<h4>Methods</h4>A formative randomized controlled trial was conducted in a tertiary hospital from February 2024 to March 2025. Adults aged ≥18 years with osteoporosis were recruited and randomly assigned (1:1) to either the intervention (OPBot) group or a control group receiving traditional health education. The chatbot provided interactive educational content and question-and-answer support, while the control group received face-to-face education and written materials. Osteoporosis knowledge was assessed using the Osteoporosis Knowledge Assessment Tool at baseline and discharge. Nurses' time spent on health education was self-recorded during each intervention session and aggregated across sessions. Adherence to disease management was assessed at 1, 3, and 6 months after discharge via telephone using Likert-scale questionnaires. The reliability of OPBot responses was evaluated by 2 clinician assessors using a 5-point Likert scale, with interrater agreement calculated using Cohen κ. Group comparisons were conducted using 2-tailed independent t tests, Mann-Whitney U tests, and chi-square tests, and adherence outcomes were analyzed using mixed-effects models.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 100 participants were randomized; 12% (12/100) were excluded due to loss to follow-up, refusal of the second knowledge assessment, or death, leaving 88% (88/100) participants for analysis (n=45, 51.1% in the OPBot group and n=43, 48.9% in the control group). The OPBot group showed significantly higher postintervention knowledge scores than the control group (median 80.0, IQR 70.0-89.0 vs median 75.0, IQR 65.5-80.0; P=.01). Nurses in the OPBot group spent lesser time on SME than those in the control group (median 5.0, IQR 2.0-17.0 vs median 23.0, IQR 20.0-25.0 minutes; P<.001). For adherence outcomes, a significant group×time interaction was observed for calcium supplement intake (odds ratio 1.49, 95% CI 1.08-2.06; P=.02), indicating differing adherence trajectories over time. The OPBot group also showed higher odds of consuming calcium-rich foods across time points (odds ratio 2.87, 95% CI 1.04-7.89; nominal P=.04), although this association did not remain significant after Holm correction. No significant effects were observed for sun exposure (P=.56), exercise (P=.79), or total adherence scores (P=.33). In the question-and-answer module, most OPBot responses were rated as highly reliable 89.4% (76/85), with high interrater agreement (Cohen κ=0.83).<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLM-based chatbots specifically designed for osteoporosis SME may improve patient knowledge, supporting adherence behaviors, and reducing healthcare workload. However, further large-scale studies are needed to confirm these findings.","affiliation":"Affiliated Hospital of Guangdong Medical University, Zhanjiang, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Osteoporosis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Patient Education as Topic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Self-Management","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ED","qualifierName":"education","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Adherence","Osteoporosis","Patient education","Self-management Education","Chatbots","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/85475"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13273208"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13273208?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-05","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-02"},{"id":"41908138","source":"MED","pmid":"41908138","pmcid":"PMC13021839","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13021839"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1798519","title":"Mapping the use of large language models in hiring decisions: a scoping review.","authorString":"Tripathi A, Tripathi A, Darena F, Mishra PK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tripathi A","firstName":"Arpit","lastName":"Tripathi","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}},{"fullName":"Tripathi A","firstName":"Ankit","lastName":"Tripathi","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}},{"fullName":"Darena F","firstName":"Frantisek","lastName":"Darena","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mishra PK","firstName":"Pawan Kumar","lastName":"Mishra","initials":"PK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2704-8330"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2704-8330"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1798519","abstractText":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being explored and deployed across recruitment and selection processes, reshaping how hiring decisions are supported, communicated, and justified. Unlike earlier algorithmic hiring tools, LLMs operate through language-mediated interaction, influencing interpretive and evaluative layers of decision-making. This scoping review maps the academic literature on LLMs in hiring to examine (i) where and how these systems are applied across the hiring pipeline, (ii) what forms of evidence and outcomes are assessed, (iii) which risks and mitigation strategies are documented, and (iv) how disciplinary structures shape research focus. Following PRISMA-ScR guidelines, we synthesize research published between 2018 and 2026 across multiple disciplines using a transparent, lexicon-based coding approach. The results reveal a rapidly expanding but uneven literature, characterized by concentration in early hiring stages, selective outcome measurement favoring efficiency and performance, high awareness of ethical risks with limited empirical validation of controls, and structurally constrained interdisciplinarity. The review highlights key gaps and provides a foundation for future interdisciplinary and field-based research on responsible LLM use in hiring.","affiliation":"Faculty of Business and Economics, Mendel University in Brno, Brno, Czechia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Recruitment","Human Resource Management","Scoping Review","Socio-technical Systems","Hiring","Algorithmic Decision-making","Generative Ai","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1798519"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13021839"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13021839?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-13"},{"id":"41534067","source":"MED","pmid":"41534067","pmcid":"PMC12853083","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12853083"]},"doi":"10.2196/86550","title":"From Agents to Governance: Essential AI Skills for Clinicians in the Large Language Model Era.","authorString":"Cao W, Zhang Q, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cao W","firstName":"Weiping","lastName":"Cao","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9117-6367"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, The People's Hospital of Leshan, Leshan, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Q","firstName":"Qing","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5652-4036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Informatics, West China Medical School, Sichuan University, Chengdu, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5652-4036"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-9117-6367"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4082344,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e86550","abstractText":"Large language models are rapidly transitioning from pilot schemes to routine clinical practice. This creates an urgent need for clinicians to develop the necessary skills to strike the right balance between seizing opportunities and taking accountability. We propose a 3-tier competency framework to support clinicians' evolution from cautious users to responsible stewards of artificial intelligence (AI). Tier 1 (foundational skills) defines the minimum competencies for safe use, including prompt engineering, human-AI agent interaction, security and privacy awareness, and the clinician-patient interface (transparency and consent). Tier 2 (intermediate skills) emphasizes evaluative expertise, including bias detection and mitigation, interpretation of explainability outputs, and the effective clinical integration of AI-generated workflows. Tier 3 (advanced skills) establishes leadership capabilities, mandating competencies in ethical governance (delineating accountability and liability boundaries), regulatory strategy, and model life cycle management-specifically, the ability to govern algorithmic adaptation and change protocols. Integrating this framework into continuing medical education programs and role-specific job descriptions could enhance clinicians' ability to use AI safely and responsibly. This could standardize deployment and support safer clinical practice, with the potential to improve patient outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, West China Hospital, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Agent","Education","Continuing Medical Education","Competency","Clinician","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/86550"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12853083"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12853083?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-14"},{"id":"41592221","source":"MED","pmid":"41592221","pmcid":"PMC13032092","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13032092"]},"doi":"10.2196/80348","title":"Large Language Model-Based Chatbots and Agentic AI for Mental Health Counseling: Systematic Review of Methodologies, Evaluation Frameworks, and Ethical Safeguards.","authorString":"Cho HN, Wang J, Hu D, Zheng K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cho HN","firstName":"Ha Na","lastName":"Cho","initials":"HN","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8033-6644"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, University of California, Irvine, Irvine, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang J","firstName":"Jiayuan","lastName":"Wang","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2144-4610"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, University of California, Irvine, Irvine, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu D","firstName":"Di","lastName":"Hu","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2842-1478"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, University of California, Irvine, Irvine, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Zheng K","firstName":"Kai","lastName":"Zheng","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4121-4948"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, University of California, Irvine, Irvine, CA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8033-6644"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2842-1478"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4121-4948"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2144-4610"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"5","journalIssueId":4146411,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR AI","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR AI","isoabbreviation":"JMIR AI","nlmid":"9918645789006676","essn":"2817-1705","issn":"2817-1705"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e80348","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language model (LLM)-based chatbots have rapidly emerged as tools for digital mental health (MH) counseling. However, evidence on their methodological quality, evaluation rigor, and ethical safeguards remains fragmented, limiting interpretation of clinical readiness and deployment safety.<h4>Objective</h4>This systematic review aimed to synthesize the methodologies, evaluation practices, and ethical or governance frameworks of LLM-based chatbots developed for MH counseling and to identify gaps affecting validity, reproducibility, and translation.<h4>Methods</h4>We searched Google Scholar, PubMed, IEEE Xplore, and ACM Digital Library for studies published between January 2020 and May 2025. Eligible studies reported original development or empirical evaluation of LLM-driven MH counseling chatbots. We excluded studies that did not involve LLM-based conversational agents, were not focused on counseling or supportive MH communication, or lacked evaluable system outputs or outcomes. Screening and data extraction were conducted in Covidence (Veritas Health Innovation) following PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) 2020 guidelines. Study quality was appraised using a structured traffic light framework across 5 methodological domains (design, dataset reporting, evaluation metrics, external validation, and ethics), with an overall judgment derived across domains. We used narrative synthesis with descriptive aggregation to summarize methodological trends, evaluation metrics, and governance considerations.<h4>Results</h4>Twenty studies met the inclusion criteria. GPT-based models (GPT-2/3/4) were used in 45% (9/20) of studies, while 90% (18/20) used fine-tuned or domain-adaptation models such as LLaMa, ChatGLM, or Qwen. Reported deployment types were not mutually exclusive; standalone apps were most common (18/20, 90%), and some systems were also implemented as virtual agents (4/20, 20%) or delivered via existing platforms (2/20, 10%). Evaluation approaches were frequently mixed, with qualitative assessment (13/20, 65%), such as thematic analysis or rubric-based scoring, often complemented by quantitative language metrics (18/20, 90%), including BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy), ROUGE (Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation), or perplexity. Quality appraisal indicated consistently low risk for dataset reporting and evaluation metrics, but recurring limitations were observed in external validation and reporting on ethics and safety, including incomplete documentation of safety safeguards and governance practices. No included study reported registered randomized controlled trials or independent clinical validation in real-world care settings.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLM-based MH counseling chatbots show promise for scalable and personalized support, but current evidence is limited by heterogeneous study designs, minimal external validation, and inconsistent reporting of safety and governance practices. Future work should prioritize clinically grounded evaluation frameworks, transparent reporting of model and prompt configurations, and stronger validation using standardized outcomes to support safe, reliable, and regulatory-ready deployment.","affiliation":"Department of Informatics, University of California, Irvine, Irvine, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Personalized Health Care","Digital Health","Conversational Agent","Digital Mental Health Intervention","Large Language Model Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/80348"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13032092"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13032092?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-13","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-13"},{"id":"42247576","source":"MED","pmid":"42247576","pmcid":"PMC13240795","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13240795"]},"doi":"10.2196/88567","title":"Voice-Based Structured Nursing Documentation Using Automatic Speech Recognition and Large Language Models: Development and Evaluation Study.","authorString":"Su MH, Wang WC, Hsu YM, Hou SY, Chuang SJ, Chang SS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Su MH","firstName":"Meng-Han","lastName":"Su","initials":"MH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-3179-6224"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Innovation Center, China Medical University Hospital, China Medical University, No. 2, Yude Rd, North Dist, Taichung, 404327, Taiwan, 886 0422052121 ext 12584."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang WC","firstName":"Wei-Chun","lastName":"Wang","initials":"WC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2239-8174"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Innovation Center, China Medical University Hospital, China Medical University, No. 2, Yude Rd, North Dist, Taichung, 404327, Taiwan, 886 0422052121 ext 12584."}]}},{"fullName":"Hsu YM","firstName":"Yi-Min","lastName":"Hsu","initials":"YM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9821-3746"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"China Medical University Hospital, Taichung, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Hou SY","firstName":"Shih-Yen","lastName":"Hou","initials":"SY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1899-5574"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Innovation Center, China Medical University Hospital, China Medical University, No. 2, Yude Rd, North Dist, Taichung, 404327, Taiwan, 886 0422052121 ext 12584."}]}},{"fullName":"Chuang SJ","firstName":"Su-Jung","lastName":"Chuang","initials":"SJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6325-6834"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"China Medical University Hospital, Taichung, Taiwan."}]}},{"fullName":"Chang SS","firstName":"Shih-Sheng","lastName":"Chang","initials":"SS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1664-4818"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Innovation Center, China Medical University Hospital, China Medical University, No. 2, Yude Rd, North Dist, Taichung, 404327, Taiwan, 886 0422052121 ext 12584."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1664-4818"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1899-5574"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2239-8174"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6325-6834"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9821-3746"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-3179-6224"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4205653,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR nursing","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Nurs","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Nurs","nlmid":"101771299","essn":"2562-7600","issn":"2562-7600"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88567","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>For clinical nurses, manually entering information into hospital information systems (HISs) remains time-consuming and prone to omissions. Although speech recognition can reduce the need for manual entry, its use in clinical settings has historically been limited by code-switching, medical terminology, and noisy ward environments. Recent advances in customized automatic speech recognition (ASR) and large language models (LLMs) now make speech-based, structured documentation aligned with nursing frameworks such as DART (data, action, response, and teaching) increasingly feasible.<h4>Objective</h4>This study developed and evaluated an integrated ASR and LLM system that transforms spoken nursing input into structured DART notes and evaluated its accuracy, usability, and clinical feasibility within HIS workflows.<h4>Methods</h4>A code-switching nursing speech corpus from emergency and ward settings was used to fine-tune the Whisper large-v2 model with parameter-efficient adaptation. The LLM generated schema-constrained DART records from ASR transcripts, which were verified by nurses before being uploaded to the corresponding HIS fields. Evaluation included mixed error rate for ASR accuracy, F1-scores, and agreement statistics for DART classification, hallucination assessments based on factual correctness, and analysis of nurse feedback on system use.<h4>Results</h4>The fine-tuned ASR model reduced the mixed error rate from 44.79% to 6.67%. DART generation achieved a macroaveraged F1-score of 0.82 (95% CI 0.80-0.84) and met the noninferiority margin relative to human transcripts (δ=-0.04). The hallucination rate was 2.51%. During deployment, the monthly volume of valid nursing notes generated through voluntary use of the ASR system increased from 32,724 to 65,417, where each note represented a single documentation entry generated per patient care episode. Among 120 participating nurses, 91 (75.8%) reported reduced workload and improved completeness.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The integrated ASR and LLM system was feasible and showed strong performance, with good acceptance among clinical nurses. It reduced the manual documentation burden, improved record completeness, and demonstrated the value of an ASR- and LLM-supported workflow for nursing documentation.","affiliation":"Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Innovation Center, China Medical University Hospital, China Medical University, No. 2, Yude Rd, North Dist, Taichung, 404327, Taiwan, 886 0422052121 ext 12584.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nursing Records","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Speech Recognition Software","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Documentation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nursing","Documentation","Nursing Records","Code-switching","Automatic Speech Recognition","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88567"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13240795"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13240795?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-10","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-05"},{"id":"PPR1266867","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.03.20.26348890","title":"Implementation of Human-in-the-Loop ChatGPT-based Patient Screening Across Multiple Diverse Clinical Trials","authorString":"Dohopolski M, Esselink K, Desai N, Grones B, Patel T, Jiang S, Peterson E, Navar AM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Dohopolski M","firstName":"Michael","lastName":"Dohopolski","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9043-1490"}},{"fullName":"Esselink K","firstName":"Kate","lastName":"Esselink","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Desai N","firstName":"Neil","lastName":"Desai","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Grones B","firstName":"Bailey","lastName":"Grones","initials":"B"},{"fullName":"Patel T","firstName":"Toral","lastName":"Patel","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Jiang S","firstName":"Steve","lastName":"Jiang","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Peterson E","firstName":"Eric","lastName":"Peterson","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Navar AM","firstName":"Ann Marie","lastName":"Navar","initials":"AM"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9043-1490"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>  Manual screening for trial eligibility is inefficient and costly. We prospectively evaluated a large language model (LLM)-assisted prescreening workflow across multiple active trials. <h4>Methods</h4>  We deployed a retrieval-augmented generation LLM-based pipeline across multiple trials at an academic medical center. Structured electronic health record data and free-text notes were used by the LLM to classify each criterion as either  met, likely met, likely not met, not met, uncertain , or  no documentation found with accompanying rationale. Coordinators were given a sorted patient list based on the LLM-based eligibility to be reviewed and provide their assessment of each criterion, and final prescreening status (success vs failure). Criterion-level performance– accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value (PPV), negative predictive value (NPV), and F1 score–was calculated and measured over time. Patient prescreening status was evaluated as a function of percent of individual AI criteria met (60-80% and ≥  80 %).  <h4>Results</h4>  From 10/2024–9/2025, 39,182 patients were prescreened by our LLM-workflow across 26 (21 oncology and 5 non-oncology) studies with 112 distinct criteria; 914 patients with high likelihood for eligibility underwent coordinator review (total 5,096 criteria). Aggregated individual criterion-level performance: accuracy 0.94 (95% CI, 0.92–0.96), sensitivity 0.98 (0.97–0.99), specificity 0.81 (0.71–0.88), PPV 0.95 (0.92–0.97), NPV 0.93 (0.90–0.95), F1 0.97 (0.95–0.97). Twenty-seven criteria prompts across 14/26 trials were automatically updated based on coordinator feedback. Patients were more likely to be reviewed by coordinators (544/987, 55.1% vs 372/397, 93.7%) and more likely to be labeled as a prescreen success (104/544, 19.1% vs 162/372, 43.5%) when ≥  80% AI-labeled criteria were  met or  likely met vs 60-80%. Cost averaged $0.12 per patient.  <h4>Conclusion</h4>  A LLM-assisted, human-in-the-loop prescreening workflow achieved high criterion-level performance at low cost across a variety of actively enrolling clinical trials. Structured coordinator feedback supported an automated learning workflow, enhancing screening operations while maintaining human oversight.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.03.20.26348890"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-27"},{"id":"41701936","source":"MED","pmid":"41701936","pmcid":"PMC12912660","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12912660"]},"doi":"10.2196/77127","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Medical Education: Transformative Potential, Current Applications, and Future Implications.","authorString":"Izquierdo-Condoy JS, Arias-Intriago M, Montero Corrales L, Ortiz-Prado E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Izquierdo-Condoy JS","firstName":"Juan S","lastName":"Izquierdo-Condoy","initials":"JS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1178-0546"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"One Health Research Group, Universidad de Las Américas, Via Nayon S/N, Quito, 170124, Ecuador, +593 995760693."}]}},{"fullName":"Arias-Intriago M","firstName":"Marlon","lastName":"Arias-Intriago","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-3717-383X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"One Health Research Group, Universidad de Las Américas, Via Nayon S/N, Quito, 170124, Ecuador, +593 995760693."}]}},{"fullName":"Montero Corrales L","firstName":"Laura","lastName":"Montero Corrales","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3752-9421"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Escuela de Comunicación, Universidad Latina de Costa Rica, San Jose, Costa Rica."}]}},{"fullName":"Ortiz-Prado E","firstName":"Esteban","lastName":"Ortiz-Prado","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1895-7498"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"One Health Research Group, Universidad de Las Américas, Via Nayon S/N, Quito, 170124, Ecuador, +593 995760693."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1178-0546"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1895-7498"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3752-9421"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-3717-383X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4112614,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical education","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","nlmid":"101684518","essn":"2369-3762","issn":"2369-3762"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e77127","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly influencing medical education by enabling adaptive learning, AI-assisted assessment, and scalable instructional tools. Natural language processing, machine learning, and generative large language models offer innovative ways to support teaching and learning, yet their integration raises ethical, pedagogical, and infrastructural challenges. This viewpoint article aims to examine the current applications, benefits, and challenges of AI in medical education and propose strategies for responsible and effective integration. AI tools such as chatbots, virtual patients, and intelligent tutoring systems enhance personalized and immersive learning. Automated grading and predictive analytics support efficient evaluations, while AI-assisted writing tools streamline content creation. Despite these advances, concerns persist around data privacy, algorithmic bias, unequal access, and diminished critical thinking. Key solutions include AI literacy training, data oversight, equitable infrastructure, and curriculum reform. The FACETS framework offers 6 dimensions (ie, form, application, context, instructional mode, technology, and the SAMR [substitution, augmentation, modification, redefinition model]) to evaluate AI integration effectively. AI offers substantial opportunities to transform medical education, but its adoption must be ethical, equitable, and pedagogically grounded. Strategic frameworks such as FACETS, combined with institutional governance and cross-sector collaboration, are essential to guide implementation so that AI enhances learning outcomes while preserving the humanistic foundations of medical practice.","affiliation":"One Health Research Group, Universidad de Las Américas, Via Nayon S/N, Quito, 170124, Ecuador, +593 995760693.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Forecasting"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Education, Medical","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Medical education","Machine Learning","Future Implications","Adaptive Learning Systems"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/77127"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12912660"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12912660?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-17"},{"id":"PMC12763229","source":"PMC","pmcid":"PMC12763229","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12763229"]},"title":"Older Adults’ Attitude Towards and Intent to Use of AI-Powered Conversational Agents for Social Support","journalInfo":{"issue":"Suppl 2","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4081997,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-12-01","journal":{"title":"Innovation in aging","medlineAbbreviation":"Innov Aging","isoabbreviation":"Innov Aging","nlmid":"101703706","essn":"2399-5300","issn":"2399-5300"}},"pubYear":"2025","language":"eng","pubModel":"Undetermined","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Abstract"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12763229/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"PubMedCentral","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12763229/pdf/?tool=EBI"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12763229"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12763229?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-06","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-01","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-01"},{"id":"42181412","source":"MED","pmid":"42181412","pmcid":"PMC13197427","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13197427"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.107548","title":"Persona Routing Associated With Fewer Safety and Monotonicity Violations in Simulated Emergency Large Language Model (LLM) Reasoning.","authorString":"Harada Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Harada Y","firstName":"Yuusuke","lastName":"Harada","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Chiba University, Chiba, JPN."},{"affiliation":"Public Policy, Hosei University, Tokyo, JPN."},{"affiliation":"Humanities and Social Sciences, Hiroshima University, Hiroshima, JPN."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4172604,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e107548","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>The objective of this study was to evaluate whether persona-routing strategies that combine a LEAN persona with a SAFE persona reduce rule-defined contraindication and sequence safety failures, as well as counterfactual monotonicity violations, while preserving efficiency in simulated emergency clinical reasoning. The study also aimed to determine whether such routing can reduce explicit safety failures and monotonicity violations while keeping resource use close to the lean baseline.<h4>Methods</h4>Using a single ChatGPT deployment (GPT-5.2 Pro; accessed January 2026; San Francisco, CA: OpenAI), we collected Japanese-language persona outputs for 28 synthetic emergency vignettes (56 scenario-level runs) and eight Base/Worse counterfactual pairs (16 comparisons per strategy). We compared four personas (high/low time pressure × LEAN/SAFE) and three routing strategies that escalated from high-time-pressure LEAN persona (P_HL) to high-time-pressure SAFE persona (P_HS) via red flags, dual-run auditing, and optional arbitration. Outputs were constrained to structured JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) and automatically scored for test suggestions, discharge safety-net specificity (0-5), contraindication/sequence safety violations (severity 0-3), and monotonicity violations. Routing outcomes were evaluated as deterministic offline simulations over stored persona outputs; accordingly, reported call counts are simulated expected large language model (LLM) calls rather than separately logged live controller calls.<h4>Results</h4>The lean baseline suggested the fewest tests (mean: 1.95) but produced safety violations in 8/56 scenarios (14.3%) and monotonicity violations in 10/16 comparisons (62.5%). SAFE personas had 0/56 safety violations and 0/16 monotonicity violations, but suggested more tests (means: 3.38-4.32). Routing eliminated safety violations and reduced monotonicity violations (ROUTER_R1 2/16; ROUTER_R2_CF 0/16) while keeping test counts near the lean baseline in the main router simulation (means: 2.14-2.21) with modest simulated call overhead (ROUTER_R1 1.21 calls; ROUTER_R2_AUDIT 2.00 calls).<h4>Conclusions</h4>In this single-model, synthetic evaluation, persona routing was associated with fewer rule-defined safety violations (14.3-0%) and monotonicity violations (62.5% with LEAN prompting vs. 12.5% with ROUTER_R1 and 0% with ROUTER_R2_CF) while preserving low test-suggestion counts.","affiliation":"School of Medicine, Chiba University, Chiba, JPN.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Safety","Clinical Decision Support","Triage","Routing","Monotonicity","Metamorphic Testing","Large Language Model","Persona Prompting"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.107548"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197427"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13197427?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1195083","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-22"},{"id":"42147983","source":"MED","pmid":"42147983","pmcid":"PMC13179146","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13179146"]},"doi":"10.2147/idr.s601126","title":"A Theoretical Framework for &lt;i&gt;Shared Reasoning Fragility&lt;/i&gt; in Clinician-Chatbot Interactions Through the Example of Antibiotic Prescribing.","authorString":"Giacobbe DR, Grossi AA, Marelli C, Muccio M, Guastavino S, Murgia Y, Mora S, Signori A, Rosso N, Giacomini M, Campi C, Piana M, Bassetti M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Giacobbe DR","firstName":"Daniele Roberto","lastName":"Giacobbe","initials":"DR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2385-1759"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UO Clinica Malattie Infettive, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Health Sciences (DISSAL), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Grossi AA","firstName":"Alessandra Agnese","lastName":"Grossi","initials":"AA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Human Sciences and Innovation for the Territory, University of Insubria, Varese, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biotechnologies and Life Sciences, Center for Clinical Ethics, University of Insubria, Varese, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Marelli C","firstName":"Cristina","lastName":"Marelli","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CESP - INSERM U1018, Oncostat, Labeled Ligue Contre le Cancer, Gustave Roussy, Université Paris-Saclay, Villejuif, France."},{"affiliation":"Institut Curie - INSERM U1331, Team Statistics Applied to Personalized Medicine, Paris, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Muccio M","firstName":"Marco","lastName":"Muccio","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-0297-1019"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UO Clinica Malattie Infettive, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Guastavino S","firstName":"Sabrina","lastName":"Guastavino","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics (DIMA), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Murgia Y","firstName":"Ylenia","lastName":"Murgia","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, Bioengineering, Robotics and System Engineering (DIBRIS), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Mora S","firstName":"Sara","lastName":"Mora","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UO Information and Communication Technologies, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Signori A","firstName":"Alessio","lastName":"Signori","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Sciences (DISSAL), Section of Biostatistics, University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Ospedale Policlinico San Martino, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Rosso N","firstName":"Nicola","lastName":"Rosso","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UO Information and Communication Technologies, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Giacomini M","firstName":"Mauro","lastName":"Giacomini","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Informatics, Bioengineering, Robotics and System Engineering (DIBRIS), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Campi C","firstName":"Cristina","lastName":"Campi","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2105-8554"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics (DIMA), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Life Science Computational Laboratory (LISCOMP), IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Piana M","firstName":"Michele","lastName":"Piana","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics (DIMA), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Life Science Computational Laboratory (LISCOMP), IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Bassetti M","firstName":"Matteo","lastName":"Bassetti","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"UO Clinica Malattie Infettive, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Health Sciences (DISSAL), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2105-8554"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2385-1759"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-0297-1019"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091437,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Infection and drug resistance","medlineAbbreviation":"Infect Drug Resist","isoabbreviation":"Infect Drug Resist","nlmid":"101550216","essn":"1178-6973","issn":"1178-6973"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"601126","abstractText":"General-purpose large language model (LLM)-based chatbots are increasingly used by clinicians to discuss medical problems, including antibiotic prescribing. Their use creates an unprecedented setting for clinical reasoning in which diagnostic and therapeutic thinking becomes dynamically shared between human and machine. Here, we propose a theoretical framework, intended for subsequent empirical assessment, around the concept of <i>shared reasoning fragility</i>, defined as the potential instability arising from the interaction between clinician reasoning and the chatbot's opaque, association-based processes, which are structurally different from classical human reasoning. The theoretical framework is based on the conceptual argument that, while the black box dilemma is often discussed for classification-oriented clinical decision support systems with an emphasis on explainability versus external validation, chatbot-assisted practice introduces a distinct problem: chatbots can accompany clinicians throughout the entire reasoning pathway rather than being consulted only at the final decision point. In the present perspective, we argue more explicitly that the fragility of this continuous co-reasoning primarily stems from its novelty and pervasiveness. Using strictly illustrative examples in antibiotic prescribing, we suggest the theoretical possibility that fluent and convincing outputs may redirect attention, mask omissions in work-up, and subtly shift hypothesis selection during shared clinical reasoning processes. While it is important to stress that our framework is purely theoretical and thus cannot be confirmed at the present stage, our considerations are intended to motivate the required quantitative research to confirm or refute <i>shared reasoning fragility</i>, measure its extent, and evaluate downstream implications for patient care.","affiliation":"UO Clinica Malattie Infettive, IRCCS Azienda Ospedaliera Metropolitana, Genoa, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["discussion","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Infection","Artificial intelligence","Machine Learning","Healthcare","Natural Language Processing","Antibiotic Prescribing","Deep Learning"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/IDR.S601126"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179146"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179146?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-18","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-18","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-11"},{"id":"41508786","source":"MED","pmid":"41508786","doi":"10.1111/aej.70055","title":"Evaluation of Large Language Model-Based Chatbots for Dental Trauma Management: A Comparative Study Based on Accuracy, Consistency and Information Quality.","authorString":"Isik V, Sengul RI, Sismanoglu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Isik V","firstName":"Vasfiye","lastName":"Isik","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1622-2698"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endodontics, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Istanbul, Turkey."}]}},{"fullName":"Sengul RI","firstName":"Rana Ikbal","lastName":"Sengul","initials":"RI","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-2688-0207"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Istanbul, Turkey."}]}},{"fullName":"Sismanoglu S","firstName":"Soner","lastName":"Sismanoglu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1272-5581"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Restorative Dentistry, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Istanbul, Turkey."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1272-5581"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1622-2698"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-2688-0207"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2","volume":"52","journalIssueId":4237639,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01","journal":{"title":"Australian endodontic journal : the journal of the Australian Society of Endodontology Inc","medlineAbbreviation":"Aust Endod J","isoabbreviation":"Aust Endod J","nlmid":"100888004","essn":"1747-4477","issn":"1329-1947"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"356-363","abstractText":"This study evaluated and compared the accuracy, consistency, readability, and information quality of three LLM-based chatbots, namely ChatGPT-5, Claude AI (Sonnet 4.0), and Perplexity (Mistral Large 2), in addressing traumatic dental injury questions. Forty true/false statements were submitted to each chatbot three times at weekly intervals to assess accuracy and consistency. Additionally, chatbot responses for 25 open-ended case-based questions were evaluated for readability, understandability and actionability, information reliability and quality. For true/false questions, Perplexity showed the highest accuracy, followed by Claude and ChatGPT. For open-ended responses, ChatGPT excelled in readability (FRE: 62.4 ± 7.6), Perplexity in understandability (91.0 ± 4.3) and actionability (93.0 ± 6.4) and Claude in information reliability (mDISCERN total: 61.2; no variability observed). All chatbots achieved acceptable global quality scores (> 4.4). These findings emphasise the complementary role of chatbots in dental trauma management. Tool selection should be based on intended use, while continued human oversight remains essential in clinical decision-making.","affiliation":"Department of Endodontics, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Istanbul, Turkey.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","Evaluation Study","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Tooth Injuries","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Reproducibility of Results"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Comprehension"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Dental Trauma","Traumatic Dental Injuries","Chatbots","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aej.70055"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-09","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-08"},{"id":"42255283","source":"MED","pmid":"42255283","pmcid":"PMC13233492","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13233492"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1832634","title":"Human-supervised, large language model-based clinical decision support aligned to national newborn protocols in Kenya: a pragmatic, early-stage evaluation.","authorString":"Kuria T, Kamau G, Makokha F, Omondi P, Mbugua G, Kamau D, Mbugua S, Gitaka J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kuria T","firstName":"Teresiah","lastName":"Kuria","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases, Mount Kenya University, Thika, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamau G","firstName":"Gideon","lastName":"Kamau","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Development, Labpoint Ltd, Thika, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Makokha F","firstName":"Felisters","lastName":"Makokha","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health and Sanitation, Ministry of Health, Bungoma, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Omondi P","firstName":"Protus","lastName":"Omondi","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases, Mount Kenya University, Thika, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Mbugua G","firstName":"George","lastName":"Mbugua","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Research and Development, Labpoint Ltd, Thika, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamau D","firstName":"David","lastName":"Kamau","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases, Mount Kenya University, Thika, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Mbugua S","firstName":"Samuel","lastName":"Mbugua","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, United States International University in Africa, Nairobi, Kenya."}]}},{"fullName":"Gitaka J","firstName":"Jesse","lastName":"Gitaka","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases, Mount Kenya University, Thika, Kenya."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6611-9256"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1832634","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Timely, protocol-adherent clinical decisions are crucial for reducing neonatal mortality in low-resource settings. Translating extensive national guidelines into bedside practice remains challenging.<h4>Objective</h4>We developed and evaluated AIFYA, a human-supervised, large language model (LLM)-based clinical decision support system (CDSS) aligned with Kenya's national newborn care protocols.<h4>Methods</h4>This prospective, mixed-methods, early-stage evaluation, guided by the DECIDE-AI framework, embedded AIFYA into routine workflows at two public health facilities (Level 5 and Level 4) in Bungoma County, Kenya, from September 2024 to June 2025. Primary outcomes were: (1) adoption, measured by cumulative neonatal cases managed; (2) training reach, assessed by credentialed healthcare workers (HCWs); and (3) guideline and citation concordance, evaluated through blinded review of 118 AI-generated recommendations by two neonatologists, with adjudication by a third. Secondary outcomes included protocol adherence and triage-to-decision time.<h4>Results</h4>A total of 50 HCWs were trained, and 550 neonatal cases were managed over 10 months. Among surveyed HCWs (<i>n</i> = 33), 76% were female (mean age 32.1 years). Expert review found 75% of recommendations were correct and 15% partially correct, with strong inter-rater reliability (weighted Cohen's kappa 0.85; 95% CI 0.79-0.91) between reviewers. Citation accuracy was 96%. In 40 complex dosing scenarios, 75% of outputs were rated correct. The median triage-to-decision time was 23 min (IQR 18-31). Implementation was supported by an offline-first architecture and a facility-based coaching model, sustaining engagement despite staff turnover.<h4>Conclusion</h4>A human-supervised, guideline-aligned AI CDSS can be feasibly implemented in routine neonatal care in low-resource settings, with high adoption and guideline concordance. Citation-linked recommendations enhance transparency and support clinical verification with most clinically appropriate recommendations. However, variability in complex scenarios highlights the need for ongoing refinement and strict clinical oversight. These findings support progression to control trials to evaluate clinical effectiveness and safety.","affiliation":"Centre for Research in Infectious Diseases, Mount Kenya University, Thika, Kenya.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Kenya","Clinical Decision Support System","Guideline Adherence","Low-resource Settings","Digital Health","Large Language Model","Human-Supervised"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1832634"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13233492"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13233492?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1267059","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-21"},{"id":"41202205","source":"MED","pmid":"41202205","pmcid":"PMC12594504","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12594504"]},"doi":"10.2196/75078","title":"A Prompt Engineering Framework for Large Language Model-Based Mental Health Chatbots: Conceptual Framework.","authorString":"Boit S, Patil R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Boit S","firstName":"Sorio","lastName":"Boit","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-1833-4068"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, 1 Campus Dr, Allendale, MI, 49401, United States, 1 616-331-4375."}]}},{"fullName":"Patil R","firstName":"Rajvardhan","lastName":"Patil","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6944-4692"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, 1 Campus Dr, Allendale, MI, 49401, United States, 1 616-331-4375."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6944-4692"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-1833-4068"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4041540,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-11-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR mental health","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Ment Health","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Ment Health","nlmid":"101658926","essn":"2368-7959","issn":"2368-7959"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e75078","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Artificial intelligence (AI), particularly large language models (LLMs), presents a significant opportunity to transform mental health care through scalable, on-demand support. While LLM-powered chatbots may help reduce barriers to care, their integration into clinical settings raises critical concerns regarding safety, reliability, and ethical oversight. A structured framework is needed to capture their benefits while addressing inherent risks. This paper introduces a conceptual model for prompt engineering, outlining core design principles for the responsible development of LLM-based mental health chatbots.<h4>Objective</h4>This paper proposes the Mental Well-Being Through Dialogue - Safeguarded and Adaptive Framework for Ethics (MIND-SAFE), a comprehensive, layered framework for prompt engineering that integrates evidence-based therapeutic models, adaptive technology, and ethical safeguards. The objective is to propose and outline a practical foundation for developing AI-driven mental health interventions that are safe, effective, and clinically relevant.<h4>Methods</h4>We outline a layered architecture for an LLM-based mental health chatbot. The design incorporates (1) an input layer with proactive risk detection; (2) a dialogue engine featuring a user state database for personalization and retrieval-augmented generation to ground responses in evidence-based therapies such as cognitive behavioral therapy, acceptance and commitment therapy, and dialectical behavior therapy; and (3) a multitiered safety system, including a postgeneration ethical filter and a continuous learning loop with therapist oversight.<h4>Results</h4>The primary contribution is the framework itself, which systematically embeds clinical principles and ethical safeguards into system design. We also propose a comparative validation strategy to evaluate the framework's added value against a baseline model. Its components are explicitly mapped to the Framework for AI Tool Assessment in Mental Health and Readiness Evaluation for AI-Mental Health Deployment and Implementation frameworks, ensuring alignment with current scholarly standards for responsible AI development.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The framework offers a practical foundation for the responsible development of LLM-based mental health support. By outlining a layered architecture and aligning it with established evaluation standards, this work offers guidance for developing AI tools that are technically capable, safe, effective, and ethically sound. Future research should prioritize empirical validation of the framework through the phased, comparative approach introduced in this paper.","affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, College of Computing, Grand Valley State University, 1 Campus Dr, Allendale, MI, 49401, United States, 1 616-331-4375.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Language"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health Services"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Telemedicine"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Digital Mental Health","Conversational Ai","Ethical Ai","Prompt Engineering","Large Language Model","Ai In Mental Health Care","Mind-safe Framework","Mental Health Chatbot"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/75078"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12594504"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12594504?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-11-07","dateOfCreation":"2025-11-07","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-04","dateOfRevision":"2025-11-12","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-11-07","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-07"},{"id":"42125889","source":"MED","pmid":"42125889","pmcid":"PMC13168883","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13168883"]},"doi":"10.1093/ejo/cjag034","title":"Exploring LLM-based chatbot effectiveness in answering questions related to the risks and benefits of orthognathic treatment: a cross-sectional study.","authorString":"Smyth RSD, Tsichlaki A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Smyth RSD","firstName":"Robert Samuel David","lastName":"Smyth","initials":"RSD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5763-1298"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Orthodontic Unit, UCL Eastman Dental Institute, Rockefeller Building, 21 University Street, London WC1E 6DE, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Tsichlaki A","firstName":"Aliki","lastName":"Tsichlaki","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Orthodontic Unit, UCL Eastman Dental Institute, Rockefeller Building, 21 University Street, London WC1E 6DE, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5763-1298"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"48","journalIssueId":4171791,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"European journal of orthodontics","medlineAbbreviation":"Eur J Orthod","isoabbreviation":"Eur J Orthod","nlmid":"7909010","essn":"1460-2210","issn":"0141-5387"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"cjag034","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>To assess the accuracy, reliability, quality, and readability of responses generated by three large language model chatbots, ChatGPT4o, Microsoft Copilot, and Google Gemini 2.5 Flash, when answering common patient questions about the risks and benefits of orthognathic treatment.<h4>Materials and methods</h4>Twenty frequently searched questions were identified via Google and entered into each chatbot. Responses were evaluated using validated scoring systems for accuracy, modified DISCERN, global quality scale (GQS), and Flesch Reading Ease. Intra- and inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen's kappa and intra-class correlation coefficients. Non-parametric tests were applied due to non-normal data distribution.<h4>Results</h4>Copilot achieved the highest reliability and quality scores, with significant differences observed in modified DISCERN (P < 0.001) and GQS (P = 0.046). Post hoc tests confirmed Copilot significantly outperformed ChatGPT. Accuracy scores did not differ significantly (P = 0.704). Readability varied significantly with Gemini and ChatGPT producing more accessible responses than Copilot. Intra- and inter-rater reliability scores were substantial to excellent for categorical measures and excellent for readability.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Copilot provided the most reliable and high-quality responses, whilst ChatGPT and Gemini offered greater readability ease. Despite these strengths, variability in accuracy and reliability highlights the need for caution. Chatbots should be considered as supplementary tools, and patients should verify information with qualified professionals.","affiliation":"Orthodontic Unit, UCL Eastman Dental Institute, Rockefeller Building, 21 University Street, London WC1E 6DE, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Risk Assessment"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Reproducibility of Results"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Comprehension"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Software"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Orthognathic Surgical Procedures","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Chatbots","Orthognathic Treatment","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/ejo/cjag034"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168883"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13168883?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-13","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-15","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-01"},{"id":"41699048","source":"MED","pmid":"41699048","pmcid":"PMC13022085","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022085"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02453-4","title":"Reimagining psychiatric care with agentic AI: promise, challenges, and a roadmap forward.","authorString":"Sharma D, Meshkat S, Perivolaris A, Kamaleddin MA, Teferra BG, Rueda A, Samavi R, Jetly R, Mago V, Wu Y, Zhang Y, Cao B, Greenshaw A, Krishnan S, Bhat V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sharma D","firstName":"Divya","lastName":"Sharma","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics and Statistics, York University, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Meshkat S","firstName":"Shakila","lastName":"Meshkat","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Perivolaris A","firstName":"Argyrios","lastName":"Perivolaris","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Kamaleddin MA","firstName":"Mohammad Amin","lastName":"Kamaleddin","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1856-503X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Teferra BG","firstName":"Bazen Gashaw","lastName":"Teferra","initials":"BG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Rueda A","firstName":"Alice","lastName":"Rueda","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9977-9653"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Samavi R","firstName":"Reza","lastName":"Samavi","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical, Computer, and Biomedical Engineering, Toronto Metropolitan University, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Jetly R","firstName":"Rakesh","lastName":"Jetly","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Mental Health Research, University of Ottawa, Ottawa, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Mago V","firstName":"Vijay","lastName":"Mago","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9741-3463"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Health Policy & Management, York University, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Wu Y","firstName":"Yuqi","lastName":"Wu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","firstName":"Yanbo","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2421-157X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine and Dentistry, University of Alberta, Edmonton, A, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Cao B","firstName":"Bo","lastName":"Cao","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine and Dentistry, University of Alberta, Edmonton, A, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Ministry of Health, Government of Alberta, Edmonton, AB, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Computing Science, University of Alberta, Edmonton, AB, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Greenshaw A","firstName":"Andrew","lastName":"Greenshaw","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine and Dentistry, University of Alberta, Edmonton, A, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Krishnan S","firstName":"Sri","lastName":"Krishnan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrical, Computer, and Biomedical Engineering, Toronto Metropolitan University, Toronto, ON, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhat V","firstName":"Venkat","lastName":"Bhat","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Interventional Psychiatry Program, St. Michael's Hospital, Unity Health Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada. venkat.bhat@utoronto.ca."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, University of Toronto, Toronto, ON, Canada. venkat.bhat@utoronto.ca."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1856-503X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2421-157X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9741-3463"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9977-9653"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4112760,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"252","abstractText":"Agentic artificial intelligence (AI) represents a pivotal shift in clinical decision support, moving beyond static tools by reasoning, adapting, and acting alongside clinicians. Psychiatry, grounded in subjective experience, trust, and longitudinal care, offers both an opportunity and a high-stakes testbed. Agentic systems may enhance documentation, personalize care, support continuous monitoring, and extend access, while raising risks around bias, explainability, privacy, and therapeutic alliance. In this Perspective, we (i) define psychiatry-specific agentic AI distinct from decision-support and fully autonomous systems; (ii) synthesize current evidence across studies; (iii) propose assistive, collaborative, and semi-autonomous roles; and (iv) outline a roadmap for responsible implementation.","affiliation":"Department of Mathematics and Statistics, York University, Toronto, ON, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02453-4"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022085"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022085?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-16"},{"id":"42041331","source":"MED","pmid":"42041331","pmcid":"PMC13113701","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13113701"]},"doi":"10.3390/antibiotics15040368","title":"Large Language Model Recommendations for Empiric Antibiotics Versus Clinician Prescribing: A Non-Interventional Paired Retrospective Antimicrobial Stewardship Analysis.","authorString":"Antonie NI, Ionescu VA, Gheorghe G, Tiucă LC, Diaconu CC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Antonie NI","firstName":"Ninel Iacobus","lastName":"Antonie","initials":"NI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Ionescu VA","firstName":"Vlad Alexandru","lastName":"Ionescu","initials":"VA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Gheorghe G","firstName":"Gina","lastName":"Gheorghe","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Tiucă LC","firstName":"Loredana-Crista","lastName":"Tiucă","initials":"LC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."}]}},{"fullName":"Diaconu CC","firstName":"Camelia Cristina","lastName":"Diaconu","initials":"CC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6837-260X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine Department, Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest, 105402 Bucharest, Romania."},{"affiliation":"Section of Medical Sciences, Academy of Romanian Scientists, 050045 Bucharest, Romania."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6837-260X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4186027,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Antibiotics (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Antibiotics (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Antibiotics (Basel)","nlmid":"101637404","essn":"2079-6382","issn":"2079-6382"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"368","abstractText":"<b>Background/Objectives</b>: Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) remains a major global health threat, strengthening the case for antimicrobial stewardship strategies that limit unnecessary broad-spectrum empiric therapy while preserving timely escalation when clinically warranted. Before any clinical deployment of large language model (LLM)-based antibiotic decision support can be considered, structured offline evaluation is needed to assess whether model outputs align with auditable stewardship constraints under real-world admission contexts. We therefore evaluated whether post hoc LLM-generated empiric antibiotic recommendations showed greater concordance with a pre-specified stewardship benchmarking framework than clinician-initiated regimens in a retrospective shadow-mode setting. <b>Methods</b>: Single-center retrospective paired evaluation at Clinical Emergency Hospital of Bucharest (Internal Medicine, 2020-2024). The unit of analysis was the admission (N = 493), with paired 24 h empiric regimens (clinician-prescribed vs. post hoc LLM-recommended via OpenAI API; not visible to clinicians; no influence on care). Local laboratory-derived epidemiology was precomputed from microbiology exports and provided as structured prompt context to approximate information parity with clinicians' implicit local ecology knowledge. Primary (prespecified) endpoint: any contextual guardrail violation (unjustified carbapenem/antipseudomonal/anti-MRSA under prespecified structured severity/MDR-risk rules), exact McNemar. Key secondary (prespecified): Δ contextual guardrail penalty (LLM - Clin), sign test and Wilcoxon signed-rank (ties reported). Ethics committee approval was obtained. <b>Results</b>: Guardrail violations occurred in 17.0% of clinician regimens vs. 4.9% of LLM regimens (paired RD -12.2%; matched OR 0.216, 95% CI 0.127-0.367; McNemar exact <i>p</i> = 1.60 × 10<sup>-10</sup>). Δ penalty had median 0 with 398/493 ties; among non-ties, improvements (Δ < 0) exceeded adverse shifts (79 vs. 16; sign-test <i>p</i> = 3.47 × 10<sup>-11</sup>). <b>Conclusions</b>: In this offline, non-interventional paired evaluation, LLM-generated empiric regimens showed greater concordance with a pre-specified stewardship benchmarking framework than clinician empiric regimens for the same admissions. These findings should not be interpreted as evidence of clinical superiority, patient safety, or causal effectiveness, but rather as process-level benchmarking within a rule-based stewardship construct. As such, reproducible guardrail-based benchmarking may serve as an early pre-implementation step to identify alignment and potential failure modes before prospective, safety-governed evaluation.","affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila Bucharest, 050474 Bucharest, Romania.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Antimicrobial resistance","Antimicrobial Stewardship","Large Language Model","Openai Api","Paired Retrospective Study"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/antibiotics15040368"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13113701"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13113701?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1163822","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"CrossRef Pre-print loader","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-27","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-02"},{"id":"42471961","source":"MED","pmid":"42471961","doi":"10.2147/jmdh.s612064","title":"Automating Case-Based Learning in Obstetrics and Gynecology: Validation of a Locally Deployed Large Language Model.","authorString":"Hu X, Luo X, Zhao Y, Zheng B.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hu X","firstName":"Xueyan","lastName":"Hu","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-3338-1703"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Wenzhou People's Hospital, Wenzhou, Zhejiang, People's Republic of China."},{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Chinese Medical University, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Luo X","firstName":"Xiaoju","lastName":"Luo","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Wenzhou People's Hospital, Wenzhou, Zhejiang, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao Y","firstName":"Yuan","lastName":"Zhao","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Wenzhou People's Hospital, Wenzhou, Zhejiang, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zheng B","firstName":"Beibei","lastName":"Zheng","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Wenzhou People's Hospital, Wenzhou, Zhejiang, People's Republic of China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-3338-1703"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091825,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of multidisciplinary healthcare","medlineAbbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","isoabbreviation":"J Multidiscip Healthc","nlmid":"101512691","essn":"1178-2390","issn":"1178-2390"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"612064","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Standardized residency training in obstetrics and gynecology (OB/GYN) relies heavily on Case-Based Learning (CBL). Yet, manually curating high-quality teaching cases remains a labor-intensive burden.<h4>Objective</h4>To develop and evaluate a methodological framework using a locally deployed large language model (LLM) to automate the extraction and construction of structured CBL databases.<h4>Methods</h4>We employed a locally deployed Qwen3-8B-Instruct model to analyze 3678 OB/GYN PubMed case abstracts, extracting primary diagnosis, core teaching points, clinical pitfalls, and difficulty levels. Two senior OB/GYN educators established a ground-truth dataset from 100 randomly selected cases to quantify extraction accuracy and AI hallucination rates. Teaching utility was assessed via a 5-point Likert scale, calculating inter-rater reliability using intraclass correlation coefficients (ICC).<h4>Results</h4>Our automated framework effectively categorized the 3678 cases into diverse subspecialties, with Reproductive Endocrinology and Obstetrics (28.10%) and Andrology and Male Infertility (25.20%) representing the largest cohorts. The LLM demonstrated high precision in extracting objective clinical data, achieving a 96.0% accuracy rate for primary diagnoses and 92.0% for core teaching points. Notably, the overall incidence of AI hallucinations remained low at 3.0%. In pedagogical evaluations, the model earned high scores for diagnostic accuracy (Mean = 4.48/5.0, <i>p</i> < 0.05), showing strong expert consensus (ICC = 0.92). However, the model's performance faltered when addressing subjective clinical nuances; inter-rater reliability dropped significantly regarding the utility of clinical pitfalls (ICC = 0.65) and the appropriateness of difficulty levels (ICC = 0.54).<h4>Conclusion</h4>This study demonstrates that LLMs offer an efficient, scalable framework for building large-scale CBL databases. While highly accurate in extracting objective facts, AI still lacks the \"tacit knowledge\" and empirical intuition of senior clinicians. Thus, human oversight remains indispensable to validate content and mitigate clinical risks. Future research must evaluate how these databases directly impact resident competency and learning outcomes.","affiliation":"Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Wenzhou People's Hospital, Wenzhou, Zhejiang, People's Republic of China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["cbl","Health Informatics","Obstetrics And Gynecology","Natural Language Processing","Case-based Learning","Nlp","Ob/gyn","Llm","Knowledge Extraction","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S612064"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-14"},{"id":"42391101","source":"MED","pmid":"42391101","pmcid":"PMC13326726","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13326726"]},"doi":"10.2196/85666","title":"Exploring Ambient Artificial Intelligence to Enhance Learning and Feedback During Operating Room-to-Intensive Care Unit Handoffs: Co-Design and Simulation Study.","authorString":"Jalilian L, Barra FL, Grogan T, Lee J, Kadambi A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jalilian L","firstName":"Laleh","lastName":"Jalilian","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5083-516X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology and Perioperative Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, 757 Westwood Plaza, Suite 3325, Los Angeles, CA, 90095, United States, 1 (310) 267-6629."}]}},{"fullName":"Barra FL","firstName":"Federico Lorenzo","lastName":"Barra","initials":"FL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-4763-6329"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Education - General Directorate, Azienda Ospedaliero-Universitaria \"Maggiore della Carità\", Novara, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Grogan T","firstName":"Tristan","lastName":"Grogan","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9471-2938"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine Statistics Core, David Geffen School of Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, 1100 Glendon Avenue, 8th Floor,, Los Angeles, CA, 90024, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee J","firstName":"Jason","lastName":"Lee","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-4033-4440"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology and Perioperative Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, 757 Westwood Plaza, Suite 3325, Los Angeles, CA, 90095, United States, 1 (310) 267-6629."}]}},{"fullName":"Kadambi A","firstName":"Achuta","lastName":"Kadambi","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2444-2503"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of California, Los Angeles, 420 Westwood Plaza,, Los Angeles, CA, 90095, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5083-516X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9471-2938"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2444-2503"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-4033-4440"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-4763-6329"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4225182,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR medical education","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Med Educ","nlmid":"101684518","essn":"2369-3762","issn":"2369-3762"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e85666","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Operating room (OR)-to-intensive care unit (ICU) handoffs are among the most complex and high-risk communication events in perioperative care. Despite the implementation of structured checklists, trainees often receive limited feedback on their communication skills, and simulation-based education rarely provides objective data on communication performance and checklist adherence. This study explores how an ambient artificial intelligence (AI) handoff assistant used during simulation-based training of OR-to-ICU handoff discussions can enhance clinical communication training and AI literacy by mapping spoken handoff discussions to handoff checklist items, providing immediate feedback on checklist item omissions, and generating a structured handoff note that functions as a feedback-rich learning artifact.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aims to co-design and evaluate an ambient AI handoff assistant that transcribes spoken OR-to-ICU handoff communication, maps the discussion to handoff checklist items, generates a structured handoff note for educational review, and provides immediate feedback on handoff completeness during simulated OR-to-ICU handoff discussions in a low-fidelity educational setting.<h4>Methods</h4>A 2-phase mixed-methods study was conducted within the University of California, Los Angeles, Department of Anesthesiology and Perioperative Care (July-October 2025). Phase 1 comprised co-design interviews with 4 clinician educators to identify limitations of current handoff training and inform AI feature development. Phase 2 involved an error analysis, as well as evaluations of usability, workload, and educational impact, conducted through ten 60-minute simulation sessions with pairs of medical students and first-year residents. Quantitative measures included the Physician Task Load Index, System Usability Scale, and a postsimulation survey; qualitative data from co-design sessions and simulation debrief interviews were thematically analyzed.<h4>Results</h4>Educators highlighted inconsistent checklist use and the absence of objective feedback on learners' communication skills as key areas that could benefit from structured documentation of handoff discussions using AI. Error analysis of the ambient AI handoff assistant revealed a mean of 3.6 (SD 1.2) errors per note, with incorrect output being the most frequent error type. There was no statistically significant difference between the ambient AI handoff assistant and the paper checklist with respect to the Physician Task Load Index and System Usability Scale measures. Trainees valued real-time transcripts and structured handoff notes for reflection of communication practices, and exposure to AI documentation errors enhanced critical thinking and awareness of AI technology limitations.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The ambient AI handoff assistant mapped simulated handoff discussions to checklist items and generated a structured handoff note, facilitating reflection on team-based communication skills in handoff education. Imperfections in the AI's output encouraged critical appraisal of its capabilities and prompted discussion about automation complacency, suggesting that AI-assisted simulations can foster both communication and digital literacy skills essential for future AI-enabled clinical practice.","affiliation":"Department of Anesthesiology and Perioperative Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, 757 Westwood Plaza, Suite 3325, Los Angeles, CA, 90095, United States, 1 (310) 267-6629.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Learning"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Feedback"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Intensive Care Units","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Operating Rooms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Checklist"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Patient Handoff","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Simulation Training","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Simulation Training","Human-computer Interaction","Handoff Communication","Prompt Engineering","Ambient Artificial Intelligence"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/85666"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13326726"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13326726?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-02"},{"id":"42141031","source":"MED","pmid":"42141031","pmcid":"PMC13179377","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13179377"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02740-0","title":"Large language models require a new form of oversight: capability-based monitoring.","authorString":"Kellogg KC, Ye B, Hu Y, Savova GK, Wallace B, Bitterman DS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kellogg KC","firstName":"Katherine C","lastName":"Kellogg","initials":"KC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"MIT Sloan School of Management, Boston, MA, USA. kkellogg@mit.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Ye B","firstName":"Bingyang","lastName":"Ye","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, Brandeis University, Waltham, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hu Y","firstName":"Yifan","lastName":"Hu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Harvard John A. Paulson School Of Engineering And Applied Sciences, Cambridge, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Savova GK","firstName":"Guergana K","lastName":"Savova","initials":"GK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Computation Health Informatics Program, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Wallace B","firstName":"Byron","lastName":"Wallace","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Khoury College of Computer Sciences, Northeastern University, Boston, MA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bitterman DS","firstName":"Danielle S","lastName":"Bitterman","initials":"DS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"AI in Medicine Program, Mass General Brigham, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."},{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Brigham and Women's Hospital/Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."},{"affiliation":"Computation Health Informatics Program, Boston Children's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA. dbitterman@bwh.harvard.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4188236,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"375","affiliation":"MIT Sloan School of Management, Boston, MA, USA. kkellogg@mit.edu.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Letter"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"ACS.ASTRO-CSDG-24-1244514-01-CTPS.pc.gr.222210","agency":"American Cancer Society","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 CA294033","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24CA248010","agency":"National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UM1TR004408","agency":"National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01CA294033-01","agency":"Division of Cancer Prevention, National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24CA248010","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U54CA274516-01A1","agency":"National Cancer Institute","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U24 CA248010","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"UM1TR004408","agency":"NCATS NIH HHS","acronym":"TR","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"ME-2024C2-37484","agency":"Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute","acronym":"PCORI","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"U54CA274516-01A1","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02740-0"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179377"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13179377?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-15"},{"id":"42194358","source":"MED","pmid":"42194358","pmcid":"PMC13206696","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13206696"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14101267","title":"Barriers and Facilitators to the Use of Large Language Model-Based Conversational Agents in Mental Healthcare: A Systematic Review.","authorString":"Shankar R, Lim A, Xu Q.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Shankar R","firstName":"Ravi","lastName":"Shankar","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-5578-3481"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Research & Innovation Office, Tan Tock Seng Hospital, National Healthcare Group, Singapore 308433, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Lim A","firstName":"Amaevia","lastName":"Lim","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Yong Loo Lin School of Medicine, National University of Singapore, Singapore 119077, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu Q","firstName":"Qian","lastName":"Xu","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6845-9618"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Civil, Aerospace and Design Engineering, University of Bristol, Bristol BS8 1TH, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-5578-3481"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6845-9618"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4201779,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1267","abstractText":"(1) <b>Background/Objectives</b>: Over one billion individuals globally live with mental health conditions, yet the treatment gap exceeds 75% in low- and middle-income countries. Large language model (LLM)-based conversational agents have emerged as a potentially scalable solution, though the evidence base remains nascent and largely pre-clinical. This review synthesises barriers and facilitators to their implementation in mental healthcare using the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR). (2) <b>Methods</b>: Eight databases were searched from January 2022 to January 2026. Study selection was managed using Covidence. Two reviewers independently screened, extracted, and appraised studies using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool. Directed content analysis guided by CFIR was used for synthesis. (3) <b>Results</b>: Twenty-seven studies (three RCTs, nine mixed methods, eight qualitative, four cross-sectional, three observational) comprising >22,000 participants across 12 countries met inclusion criteria. Five barrier domains (27 sub-themes) and four facilitator domains (22 sub-themes) were identified. Inadequate crisis detection (reported in 21/27 studies) and 24/7 availability (reported in 26/27 studies) are the most frequently reported barriers and facilitators, respectively. These figures represent study-level reporting frequencies, not population-level prevalence estimates. CFIR mapping revealed universal coverage for Knowledge and Beliefs (100%) and Patient Needs and Resources (96%) but critical gaps in the Process domain (Evaluating: 7%; Champions: 11%). (4) <b>Conclusions</b>: LLM-based conversational agents demonstrate substantial promise but present critical safety deficiencies. A tiered implementation framework, independent safety certification, and equity-sensitive design are recommended.","affiliation":"Clinical Research & Innovation Office, Tan Tock Seng Hospital, National Healthcare Group, Singapore 308433, Singapore.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Mental health","Systematic review","Implementation Science","Barriers And Facilitators","Cfir","Digital Mental Health","Conversational Agents","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14101267"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13206696"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13206696?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-07"},{"id":"41818744","source":"MED","pmid":"41818744","pmcid":"PMC13022535","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13022535"]},"doi":"10.2196/87830","title":"Trustworthy AI-Augmented Objective Structured Clinical Examinations in Nursing Education: Taiwan-Japan Viewpoint on 5 AI Roles, Governance, and Cross-Border Implementation.","authorString":"Kubota K, Nishimura A, Seto R, Li L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Kubota K","firstName":"Kazumi","lastName":"Kubota","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Healthcare Information Management, University of Tokyo Hospital, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nishimura A","firstName":"Ayako","lastName":"Nishimura","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Nursing, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Seto R","firstName":"Ryoma","lastName":"Seto","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Division of Healthcare Informatics, Tokyo Healthcare University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Li L","firstName":"Liu","lastName":"Li","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Show Chwan Health Care System, Taipei, Taiwan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4800-644X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6154-9342"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6981-1667"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2270-2313"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4136778,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e87830","abstractText":"Generative artificial intelligence (AI) is arriving in high-stakes assessment; however, governance, validity evidence, and faculty readiness remain uneven. From a Taiwan-Japan perspective, we outline a pragmatic, transferable approach to integrating AI into nursing objective structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) using a 5-AI-role model-learning assistant, AI‑augmented standardized patient, assessment assistant, case generator, and learning analyst-mapped across pre-OSCE, peri-OSCE, and post-OSCE workflows with human-in-the-loop final judgment. Taiwan contributes agile interdisciplinary development, staged pilots (practice, mock OSCE, and limited high-stakes stations), A/B comparisons, and explainability-by-design logging that links scores to time-stamped evidence. Japan contributes robust policy scaffolding (national AI use guidance in K-12, a revised nursing model core curriculum with outcomes and assessment blueprints, and institutional research cultures that support auditability and quality assurance). We distill 4 cross-cutting governance pillars-human oversight, learning process transparency, ethics and safety, and traceability-into implementable techniques (machine-readable rubrics, standardized patient persona cards, bias monitoring, and targeted faculty development). Aligning with international principles (International Advisory Committee for AI; Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development; United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization; World Health Organization; European Commission's High Level Expert Group; and National Institute of Standards and Technology), we propose a joint road map and shared registry to benchmark reliability, validity, equity, and workload impact. This viewpoint targets OSCE directors, nursing educators, and institutional leaders and provides a phase-gated governance blueprint rather than reporting original trial outcomes. Taiwan-led agility, complemented by Japan's standards-driven assurance, can form an Asia-Pacific reference model for trustworthy AI‑augmented OSCE in nursing education.","affiliation":"Research Organization, Shimonoseki City University, Shimonoseki, Yamaguchi, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Education, Nursing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Educational Measurement","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Japan"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Taiwan"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","AI","Nursing Education","Standardized Patient","Osce","Objective Structured Clinical Examination","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Generative Ai","Taiwan-japan Collaboration","Assessment Governance","Transparency And Traceability"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/87830"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13022535?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-12","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-12","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-12"},{"id":"42319138","source":"MED","pmid":"42319138","pmcid":"PMC13286463","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13286463"]},"doi":"10.1097/hc9.0000000000000983","title":"Hepatology e-consult responses generated by artificial intelligence demonstrate accuracy but require human oversight.","authorString":"Huang HKT, Yen DW, Li MY, Jutras G, Deng LX, McCulloch CE, Pletcher MJ, Lai JC, Hameed B, Ge J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang HKT","firstName":"Holly K T","lastName":"Huang","initials":"HKT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5061-8429"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Yen DW","firstName":"Debra W","lastName":"Yen","initials":"DW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Hepatology, Department of Transplant, California Pacific Medical Center, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Li MY","firstName":"Michelle Y","lastName":"Li","initials":"MY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Jutras G","firstName":"Gabrielle","lastName":"Jutras","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Hepatology, Department of Medicine, Centre Hospitalier de l'Université de Montréal, Montréal, Québec, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Deng LX","firstName":"Lisa X","lastName":"Deng","initials":"LX","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7777-6710"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Veterans Affairs Medical Center, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"McCulloch CE","firstName":"Charles E","lastName":"McCulloch","initials":"CE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1279-6179"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pletcher MJ","firstName":"Mark J","lastName":"Pletcher","initials":"MJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6966-1312"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Lai JC","firstName":"Jennifer C","lastName":"Lai","initials":"JC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2092-6380"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hameed B","firstName":"Bilal","lastName":"Hameed","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2200-8428"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ge J","firstName":"Jin","lastName":"Ge","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1574-1525"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7777-6710"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1279-6179"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5061-8429"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6966-1312"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1574-1525"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2092-6380"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2200-8428"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"10","journalIssueId":4215036,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Hepatology communications","medlineAbbreviation":"Hepatol Commun","isoabbreviation":"Hepatol Commun","nlmid":"101695860","essn":"2471-254X","issn":"2471-254X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0983","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Electronic consultations (e-consults) improve specialist access but burden providers. We developed LiVersa, a customized large language model (LLM) for liver diseases. We evaluated its performance in drafting hepatology e-consult responses and the equivalence between human and machine reviewers.<h4>Methods</h4>LiVersa-generated responses for hepatology e-consults answered at the University of California San Francisco (UCSF) from January to March 2025. Using a 12-item rubric, 3 independent hepatologists and \"LLM-as-a-judge\" (OpenAI-o1) evaluated drafts against original responses. We tested equivalence between human reviewers and \"LLM-as-a-judge\" using two one-sided tests (TOST).<h4>Results</h4>Among 61 e-consults, the most common categories were abnormal liver function tests (34%), hepatitis B (23%), and abnormal imaging (21%). LiVersa drafts demonstrated no differences from hepatologist responses in word count (284 vs. 264, p=0.47) and verbosity (24 vs. 25 words per sentence, p=0.44). Human reviewers rated 72% of drafts as reasonable starting points and 83% as providing appropriate case-specific recommendations; 10% contained misleading/incorrect information, and 3.4% posed a risk of severe harm. LiVersa performed better at avoiding misleading information and extraneous suggestions but scored lower on clinical equivalence, immediate usability, and comprehensiveness. LLM-based reviewers were more stringent than human reviewers, rating fewer drafts as clinically equivalent (27% vs. 48%) and more as potentially harmful (67% vs. 20%), with agreement on accuracy, precision, and comprehensiveness (mean difference 0.026-0.029; TOST p<0.05).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Customized LLMs like LiVersa show promise for e-consult drafting but require human oversight. LLM-as-a-judge was more conservative than humans, supporting its role in rapid quality assurance during model updates.","affiliation":"Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Department of Medicine, University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"K23 DK139455","agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"P30 DK026743","agency":"NIDDK NIH HHS","acronym":"DK","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Liver Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gastroenterology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Remote Consultation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cirrhosis","Liver diseases","Referral And Consultation","Telemedicine","Clinical Decision Support Systems"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/HC9.0000000000000983"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13286463"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13286463?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-19"},{"id":"40933813","source":"MED","pmid":"40933813","pmcid":"PMC12417106","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12417106"]},"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2025.1655154","title":"Artificial intelligence and health empowerment in rural communities and landslide- or avalanche-isolated contexts: real case at a fictitious location.","authorString":"Krumsvik RJ, Slettvoll V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Krumsvik RJ","firstName":"Rune Johan","lastName":"Krumsvik","initials":"RJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Psychology, University of Bergen, Bergen, Norway."}]}},{"fullName":"Slettvoll V","firstName":"Vegard","lastName":"Slettvoll","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine, University of Bergen, Bergen, Norway."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":3897144,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1655154","abstractText":"Through a series of case studies, we have pretested the capabilities and reliability of the Large Language Models (LLM), Generative Pre-trained Transformer 4 (GPT-4) and OpenAI o3 reasoning model (o3) in educational and healthcare contexts. Based on this knowledge, we took a step further by testing these technologies in an authentic patient case set in a fictitious location. The context for this brief case report relates to the fact that, in the first quarter of 2025, fewer patients lacked an assigned GP compared to previous years-a positive trend. However, this offers little relief to those cut off from GP care due to their rural location or because of landslides and extreme weather. This case highlights the need for knowledge-based preparedness and alternative health empowerment pathways in rural Norway. This brief case report describes a single 16-year-old boy (<i>N</i> = 1) with no significant past medical history or chronic conditions. Although he lived in an urban area, we reframed the encounter as a simulated rural, avalanche-isolated scenario to test the feasibility of AI-supported care under extreme access constraints. Specifically, the case models how a patient in an avalanche-prone mountain valley-where seasonal road closures routinely sever access to healthcare facilities-could receive rapid, guideline-concordant treatment for severe tonsillitis during a period of general-practitioner (GP) unavailability. Repeated attempts to secure a same-day appointment were thwarted by workforce shortages and impassable roads, resulting in the earliest available appointment being five days away. The family leveraged point-of-care technologies (fingerstick C-reactive protein analysis, wearable sensors, blood pressure device, digital fever device, mobile ECG) and an o3 language model[1] to evaluate disease severity. A peak CRP of 130 mg/L, combined with otherwise stable vital signs, prompted a remote consultation with a trusted physician in their social network, who confirmed the diagnosis of bacterial tonsillitis and initiated treatment with phenoxymethylpenicillin (Apocillin). Within 72 h, CRP fell to 23 mg/L and symptoms were resolved. The patient case and the events described in this pilot study are authentic, but the location is fictitious. The waiting time to see a general practitioner was five days in both the actual urban setting and the simulated rural scenario; however, unlike in urban contexts-where patients can often access immediate care through emergency clinics or private GPs-such options are typically unavailable in sparsely populated rural areas. This case illustrates how AI and health technology can serve as a \"virtual waiting room\" for individuals in rural or landslide- and avalanche-isolated areas, especially when GP access is limited and the condition is low-risk, such as mild sore throat symptoms. The case illustrates how inexpensive diagnostics and AI-supported reasoning can strengthen health empowerment and temporarily bridge care gaps for residents of geographically isolated Norwegian communities-provided that human clinical oversight and robust digital health governance remain in place. Therefore, all LLM recommendations and technology support were reviewed during an in-person physician examination in a family network, and the final antibiotic prescription came from the clinician, underscoring that AI functioned solely as decision support rather than autonomous care.","affiliation":"Faculty of Psychology, University of Bergen, Bergen, Norway.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["brief-report","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Landslide","Rural Healthcare","Avalanches","Health Empowerment","Brief Case Report","Isolated Villages","Simulated Rural Setting"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2025.1655154"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12417106"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12417106?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-09-15","dateOfCreation":"2025-09-11","firstIndexDate":"2025-09-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-11-29","dateOfRevision":"2025-09-15","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-08-26","firstPublicationDate":"2025-08-26"},{"id":"42193555","source":"MED","pmid":"42193555","pmcid":"PMC13203138","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13203138"]},"doi":"10.3390/bs16050676","title":"AI in Psychotherapy: Opportunities and Risks.","authorString":"Neacșu V.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Neacșu V","firstName":"Valentina","lastName":"Neacșu","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Psychology, \"Titu Maiorescu\" University, 040056 Bucharest, Romania."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4201776,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Behavioral sciences (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Behav Sci (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Behav Sci (Basel)","nlmid":"101576826","essn":"2076-328X","issn":"2076-328X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"676","abstractText":"This article examines the emerging role of artificial intelligence in mental health contexts, with a particular focus on psychotherapy and the risks associated with deploying large language models (LLMs) in sensitive clinical domains. It aims to provide a broad review of current literature, highlighting key risks of general-purpose artificial intelligence (AI) systems, while also exploring the potential of clinically oriented LLMs for therapist training, supervision, and professional development. It discusses several key concerns, including AI-related psychosis, the development of parasocial attachments, and the growing number of crisis-related interactions users have with general-purpose AI models. These challenges raise important questions about the safety, reliability, and ethical management of AI systems when individuals seek support during periods of psychological crisis. Beyond identifying these risks, the article explores the potential of clinical LLMs specifically designed for mental health applications. In particular, AI can serve as a tool for therapists' training, supervision, and professional development, offering simulated clinical scenarios, structured feedback, and support for reflective practice. The article concludes by outlining key directions for the responsible development of therapeutic AI. These include the importance of human oversight, the use of specialized and clinically informed training datasets, advances in model fine-tuning and safety alignment, and the establishment of clear professional guidelines and regulatory frameworks. Together, these developments may help ensure that AI technologies are integrated into mental healthcare in ways that prioritize safety, ethical practice, and the continued central role of human clinicians.","affiliation":"Faculty of Psychology, \"Titu Maiorescu\" University, 040056 Bucharest, Romania.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Psychology","psychotherapy","Artificial Intelligence (Ai)","Ai Therapy","Ai Psychosis","Clinical Llms"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/bs16050676"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13203138"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13203138?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"PPR1168838","source":"PPR","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-29"},{"id":"42524332","source":"MED","pmid":"42524332","doi":"10.2147/rmhp.s624615","title":"Risk Management of Large Language Model-Based Exercise and Health Guidance: A China-Anchored, Comparatively Informed Six-Dimensional Trigger Matrix and Lifecycle Governance Framework for the Wellness-to-SaMD Continuum.","authorString":"Pan K, Lin X, Huang S, Huang C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan K","firstName":"Kaijiang","lastName":"Pan","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-0108-1035"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Marxism, Xiamen Ocean Vocational College, Xiamen, Fujian, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Lin X","firstName":"Xinyu","lastName":"Lin","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-8182-2431"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Film and Communication, Xiamen University of Technology, Xiamen, Fujian, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang S","firstName":"Shengqi","lastName":"Huang","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0522-478X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Huandaolu (Xiamen) Sports and Health Service Co., Ltd, Xiamen, Fujian, People's Republic of China."}]}},{"fullName":"Huang C","firstName":"Caihua","lastName":"Huang","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5134-0169"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research and Communication Center for Exercise and Health, Xiamen University of Technology, Xiamen, Fujian, People's Republic of China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5134-0169"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-0108-1035"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0522-478X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-8182-2431"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"19","journalIssueId":4091775,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Risk management and healthcare policy","medlineAbbreviation":"Risk Manag Healthc Policy","isoabbreviation":"Risk Manag Healthc Policy","nlmid":"101566264","essn":"1179-1594","issn":"1179-1594"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"624615","abstractText":"LLM-based exercise and health guidance creates a distinctive risk-management challenge: seemingly modest changes in product claims, target users, data inputs, personalization, automation, human oversight, or updates may move a tool from general wellness support toward higher-risk medical use. In exercise prescription and rehabilitation, an unsafe recommendation can affect physical load, recognition of warning symptoms, and timely referral. We conducted a structured narrative review and doctrinal/comparative legal analysis, anchored in China's National Medical Products Administration (NMPA) framework and informed by the European Union Medical Device Regulation (MDR), the EU Artificial Intelligence Act, and US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and International Medical Device Regulators Forum (IMDRF) materials. Peer-reviewed literature was primarily searched for 2019-2026, with foundational regulatory, legal, and technical guidance included where directly relevant. We propose a six-dimensional trigger matrix covering intended use and claims, user context, depth of personalization, data and sensor sources, automation, human oversight and closed-loop control, and upgrade and change pathways. The framework includes anchored Green/Yellow/Red coding rules, non-compensatory aggregation rules, a structured governance checklist, and a lifecycle pathway for evidence generation, risk management, and change control. In a preliminary application exercise, three independent raters applied the coding rules to seven standardized hypothetical scenarios and achieved complete agreement on all dimension-level and overall designations (Fleiss' kappa = 1.00). This small exercise supports initial reproducibility of the rubric but does not establish legal classification accuracy, clinical validity, or real-world effectiveness. The proposed framework is intended to support earlier risk identification, evidence planning, procurement review, and dialogue among developers, healthcare institutions, and regulators; it does not replace product-specific legal analysis or regulatory determination.","affiliation":"School of Marxism, Xiamen Ocean Vocational College, Xiamen, Fujian, People's Republic of China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Exercise Prescription","Healthcare Risk Management","Software As A Medical Device","Human Oversight","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/RMHP.S624615"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-29","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-24"},{"id":"42383194","source":"MED","pmid":"42383194","pmcid":"PMC13316258","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13316258"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.esmorw.2026.100723","title":"Large language models in oncology: promise, pitfalls, and the path to real-world adoption.","authorString":"McInerney S, Hall PS, Cresswell K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"McInerney S","firstName":"S","lastName":"McInerney","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7543-7450"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Genetics and Cancer, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Hall PS","firstName":"P S","lastName":"Hall","initials":"PS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Genetics and Cancer, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Cresswell K","firstName":"K","lastName":"Cresswell","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6634-9537"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Usher Institute, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6634-9537"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-7543-7450"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4219706,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"ESMO real world data and digital oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","isoabbreviation":"ESMO Real World Data Digit Oncol","nlmid":"9919053637506676","essn":"2949-8201","issn":"2949-8201"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"100723","affiliation":"Institute of Genetics and Cancer, University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh, UK.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Editorial"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.esmorw.2026.100723"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13316258"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13316258?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-22","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-22"},{"id":"41578826","source":"MED","pmid":"41578826","pmcid":"PMC12839511","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12839511"]},"doi":"10.5152/j.aott.2025.25428","title":"Comparative evaluation of large language model-based chatbots in a septic arthritis scenario: ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity.","authorString":"Bayrak HC, Karagöz B, Bayrak Ö.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bayrak HC","firstName":"Hünkar Cagdas","lastName":"Bayrak","initials":"HC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, Bursa Çekirge State Hospital, Bursa, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Karagöz B","firstName":"Bekir","lastName":"Karagöz","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7447-452X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, Eskisehir City Hospital, Eskisehir, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Bayrak Ö","firstName":"Özlem","lastName":"Bayrak","initials":"Ö","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Infection Diseases and Clinic Microbiology Bursa, Bursa İlker Çelikcan State Hospital, Bursa, Türkiye."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7447-452X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-4963-9980"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"59","journalIssueId":4099607,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-10-01","journal":{"title":"Acta orthopaedica et traumatologica turcica","medlineAbbreviation":"Acta Orthop Traumatol Turc","isoabbreviation":"Acta Orthop Traumatol Turc","nlmid":"9424806","essn":"2589-1294","issn":"1017-995X"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"415-420","abstractText":"<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to comparatively evaluate the clinical knowledge generation performance of 3 widely used large language model (LLM)-based chatbots (ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity) in the context of septic arthritis.<h4>Methods</h4>This cross-sectional comparative study was based on 24 scenario-based clinical questions developed in accordance with the SANJO guideline (Management of Septic Arthritis in Native Joints) of the European Bone and Joint Infection Society. Responses generated by ChatGPT (OpenAI GPT-4), Claude 2 (Anthropic), and Perplexity AI were independently assessed by 2 senior experts: 1 in orthopedic surgery and the other in infectious diseases. Each response was evaluated across 6 domains: scientific accuracy, content depth, termino logical consistency, clinical applicability, brevity, and reference support, using a 5-point Likert scale.<h4>Results</h4>All 3 LLM-based chatbots achieved perfect scores in accuracy and terminological consistency (P = 1.000), and no significant dif ference was observed in clinical applicability (P = .912). Perplexity scored significantly lower in content depth compared to both ChatGPT (P = .001) and Claude (P = .041), whereas ChatGPT and Claude did not differ significantly (P = .807). ChatGPT produced significantly more unnecessary elaboration than Claude (P = .009) and Perplexity (P < .001), while Claude and Perplexity were comparable (P = .115). For reference support, Perplexity scored significantly higher than both ChatGPT (P < .001) and Claude (P < .001), with no difference between the latter 2 (P = 1.000). Overall, Perplexity achieved the highest total score (P < .001), followed by ChatGPT and Claude. Interrater agree ment was substantial (κ = 0.72).<h4>Conclusion</h4>The LLM-based chat platforms demonstrated overall high performance, but their strengths differed across evaluation domains. While ChatGPT and Claude provided more comprehensive and detailed responses, Perplexity offered stronger reference sup port. These findings suggest that context-specific selection of LLMs is essential, as the optimal choice may vary depending on whether detailed explanation or robust referencing is prioritized.   Cite this article as: Bayrak HC, Karagöz B, Bayrak Ö. Comparative evaluation of large language model-based chatbots in a septic arthritis scenario: ChatGPT, Claude, and Perplexity. Acta Orthop Traumatol Turc., 2025;59(6):415-420.","affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, Bursa Çekirge State Hospital, Bursa, Türkiye.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Arthritis, Infectious","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cross-Sectional Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.5152/j.aott.2025.25428"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12839511"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12839511?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-30","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-10-28","firstPublicationDate":"2025-10-28"},{"id":"42508552","source":"MED","pmid":"42508552","doi":"10.1016/j.pcad.2026.07.005","title":"Large language models in sports cardiology: Challenges, governance, and human-in-the-loop integration.","authorString":"Palermi S, Saglietto A, D'Ascenzo F, De Ferrari GM, Bruno F, Tse G, Kizilkiliç SE, Tjong F, Campillo SR, Keser N, Busnatu S, Marketou M, Wenzl FA, Vecchiato M, Rampone JM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Palermi S","firstName":"Stefano","lastName":"Palermi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Departmental Faculty of Medicine and Surgery, UniCamillus-Saint Camillus International University of Health Sciences, Rome, Italy. Electronic address: stefano.palermi@unicamillus.org."}]}},{"fullName":"Saglietto A","firstName":"Andrea","lastName":"Saglietto","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiology, Cardiovascular and Thoracic Department, Città della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"D'Ascenzo F","firstName":"Fabrizio","lastName":"D'Ascenzo","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiology, Cardiovascular and Thoracic Department, Città della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"De Ferrari GM","firstName":"Gaetano Maria","lastName":"De Ferrari","initials":"GM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiology, Cardiovascular and Thoracic Department, Città della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy. Electronic address: gaetanomaria.deferrari@unito.it."}]}},{"fullName":"Bruno F","firstName":"Francesco","lastName":"Bruno","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiology, Cardiovascular and Thoracic Department, Città della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Tse G","firstName":"Gary","lastName":"Tse","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Tianjin Key Laboratory of Ionic-Molecular Function of Cardiovascular Disease, Department of Cardiology, Tianjin Institute of Cardiology, Second Hospital of Tianjin Medical University, No. 23, Pingjiang Road, Hexi District, Tianjin, 300211, People's Republic of China; Department of Population Health, School of Nursing and Health Sciences, Hong Kong Metropolitan University, 999077, Hong Kong, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Kizilkiliç SE","firstName":"Sevda Ece","lastName":"Kizilkiliç","initials":"SE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Life Sciences, Hasselt University, Agoralaan Gebouw D, 3590 Diepenbeek, Hasselt, Belgium; Heart Centre Hasselt, Jessa Hospital, Stadsomvaart 11, 3500, Hasselt, Belgium; Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Ghent University, Corneel Heymanslaan 10, 9000 Gent, Belgium."}]}},{"fullName":"Tjong F","firstName":"Fleur","lastName":"Tjong","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Heart Center, Department of Clinical and Experimental Cardiology, Amsterdam University Medical Centers, University of Amsterdam, Amsterdam, the Netherlands. Electronic address: F.v.tjong@amsterdamumc.nl."}]}},{"fullName":"Campillo SR","firstName":"Samuel Ruiperez","lastName":"Campillo","initials":"SR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, ETH, Zurich, Switzerland; AI Center, ETH, Zurich, Switzerland; Harvard-MIT Biomedical Engineering Center, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Boston, USA. Electronic address: samuel.ruiperezcampillo@inf.ethz.ch."}]}},{"fullName":"Keser N","firstName":"Nurgul","lastName":"Keser","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Health Sciences University Faculty of Medicine Department of Cardiology İstanbul-, Türkiye. Electronic address: nkeser@sakarya.edu.tr."}]}},{"fullName":"Busnatu S","firstName":"Stefan","lastName":"Busnatu","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Faculty of Medicine, University of Medicine and Pharmacy Carol Davila, Bucharest, Romania. Electronic address: stefan.busnatu@umfcd.ro."}]}},{"fullName":"Marketou M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Marketou","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Crete, School of Medicine, Crete, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Wenzl FA","firstName":"Florian A","lastName":"Wenzl","initials":"FA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Molecular Cardiology, University of Zurich, Schlieren, Zurich, Switzerland; National Disease Registration and Analysis Service, NHS, London, UK; Department of Cardiovascular Sciences, University of Leicester, Leicester, UK; Division of Cardiovascular Medicine, Radcliffe Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, Oxford, UK. Electronic address: florian.wenzl@uzh.ch."}]}},{"fullName":"Vecchiato M","firstName":"Marco","lastName":"Vecchiato","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, University of Padova, Padova, Italy; Department of Theoretical and Applied Sciences, eCampus University, Novedrate, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Rampone JM","firstName":"Joao Matteo","lastName":"Rampone","initials":"JM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Cardiology, Cardiovascular and Thoracic Department, Città della Salute e della Scienza, Turin, Italy. Electronic address: joaomatteorampone@gmail.com."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4226748,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Progress in cardiovascular diseases","medlineAbbreviation":"Prog Cardiovasc Dis","isoabbreviation":"Prog Cardiovasc Dis","nlmid":"0376442","essn":"1873-1740","issn":"0033-0620"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S0033-0620(26)00071-X","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into clinical artificial intelligence (AI) workflows, with emerging applications in medical documentation, information synthesis, guideline retrieval, and patient communication. Recent advances in foundation models, including multimodal systems, have further expanded these capabilities. However, current evidence derives predominantly from examination-style benchmarks, retrospective vignettes, and hospital-based populations, limiting its direct applicability to athlete-centered cardiovascular care. Sports cardiology represents a uniquely challenging environment for AI because of low disease prevalence, physiological-pathological overlap, longitudinal uncertainty, and eligibility-driven decision-making.<h4>Main body</h4>This narrative review critically examines the conceptual, technical, regulatory, and governance challenges associated with LLM deployment in sports cardiology. We argue that current evaluation paradigms inadequately capture the uncertainty-intensive nature of athlete cardiovascular assessment and propose the text-proxy paradigm, a governance-oriented framework in which LLMs operate as semantic integration layers that process clinician-verified and structured outputs generated by validated upstream systems rather than directly interpreting physiological signals. Within this architecture, LLMs may support documentation, longitudinal synthesis, guideline retrieval, contextual reasoning, and athlete communication under continuous human oversight. We further discuss uncertainty propagation across layered AI systems, workflow-oriented validation, regulatory implications, and a staged framework for responsible clinical implementation.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Future progress will depend less on increasingly capable models than on trustworthy clinical ecosystems that prioritize governance, calibration, transparency, auditability, and clinician oversight. Rather than serving as an early adopter of autonomous AI, sports cardiology may provide a benchmark for the responsible integration of foundation models into high-stakes clinical medicine.","affiliation":"Departmental Faculty of Medicine and Surgery, UniCamillus-Saint Camillus International University of Health Sciences, Rome, Italy. Electronic address: stefano.palermi@unicamillus.org.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Cardiology","Sports Cardiology","Large Language Model"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pcad.2026.07.005"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-27","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-27"},{"id":"41899141","source":"MED","pmid":"41899141","pmcid":"PMC13026613","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13026613"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15062218","title":"Safety-First Framework for AI-Enabled Anamnesis in Head and Neck Surgery: Evidence Synthesis from a Narrative Review.","authorString":"Vaira LA, Qadeer H, Lechien JR, Maniaci A, Maglitto F, Troise S, Chiesa-Estomba CM, Consorti G, Cirignaco G, Iannella G, Navarro-Cuéllar C, Salzano G, Soro GM, Boscolo-Rizzo P, Vellone V, De Riu G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Vaira LA","firstName":"Luigi Angelo","lastName":"Vaira","initials":"LA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7789-145X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Maxillofacial Surgery Operative Unit, Department of Medicine, Surgery and Pharmacy, University of Sassari, 07100 Sassari, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Qadeer H","firstName":"Hareem","lastName":"Qadeer","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Maxillofacial Surgery Operative Unit, Department of Medicine, Surgery and Pharmacy, University of Sassari, 07100 Sassari, Italy."},{"affiliation":"PhD School of Biomedical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Science, University of Sassari, 07100 Sassari, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Lechien JR","firstName":"Jerome R","lastName":"Lechien","initials":"JR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0845-0845"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, Mons School of Medicine, Research Institute for Health Sciences and Technology, University of Mons (UMons), 7000 Mons, Belgium."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head Neck Surgery, Elsan Polyclinic of Poitiers, 86000 Poitiers, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Maniaci A","firstName":"Antonino","lastName":"Maniaci","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1251-0185"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine and Surgery, University of Enna Kore, 94100 Enna, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Maglitto F","firstName":"Fabio","lastName":"Maglitto","initials":"F","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1080-4067"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Head and Neck Section, Department of Neurosciences, Reproductive and Odontostomatological Science, Federico II University of Naples, 80131 Naples, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Troise S","firstName":"Stefania","lastName":"Troise","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8421-0328"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Head and Neck Section, Department of Neurosciences, Reproductive and Odontostomatological Science, Federico II University of Naples, 80131 Naples, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Chiesa-Estomba CM","firstName":"Carlos M","lastName":"Chiesa-Estomba","initials":"CM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9454-9464"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otorhinolaryngology-Head & Neck Surgery, Hospital Universitario Donostia, 20014 San Sebastian, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Consorti G","firstName":"Giuseppe","lastName":"Consorti","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3508-5847"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Maxillofacial Surgery, Department of Neurological Sciences, Marche University Hospitals-Umberto I, 60121 Ancona, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Sciences and Public Health, Polytechnic University of Marche, 60121 Ancona, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Cirignaco G","firstName":"Giulio","lastName":"Cirignaco","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6830-9309"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Maxillofacial Surgery, Department of Neurological Sciences, Marche University Hospitals-Umberto I, 60121 Ancona, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Section of Maxillo-Facial Surgery, University of Siena, Viale Bracci, 53100 Siena, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Iannella G","firstName":"Giannicola","lastName":"Iannella","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1781-2809"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of 'Organi di Senso', University \"Sapienza\", Viale dell'Università, 33, 00185 Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Navarro-Cuéllar C","firstName":"Carlos","lastName":"Navarro-Cuéllar","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9869-9240"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Maxillofacial Surgery Department, Hospital Gregorio Marañon, Universidad Complutense de Madrid, 28040 Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Salzano G","firstName":"Giovanni","lastName":"Salzano","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3947-8733"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Head and Neck Section, Department of Neurosciences, Reproductive and Odontostomatological Science, Federico II University of Naples, 80131 Naples, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Soro GM","firstName":"Giovanni Maria","lastName":"Soro","initials":"GM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Administrative Direction, University of Sassari, 07100 Sassari, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Boscolo-Rizzo P","firstName":"Paolo","lastName":"Boscolo-Rizzo","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical, Surgical and Health Sciences, Section of Otolaryngology, University of Trieste, 34127 Trieste, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Vellone V","firstName":"Valentino","lastName":"Vellone","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6048-1190"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Life Science, Health, and Health Professions, Università degli Studi \"Link\", 00165 Rome, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"De Riu G","firstName":"Giacomo","lastName":"De Riu","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7201-6445"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Maxillofacial Surgery Operative Unit, Department of Medicine, Surgery and Pharmacy, University of Sassari, 07100 Sassari, Italy."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6048-1190"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8421-0328"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9454-9464"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9869-9240"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0845-0845"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1080-4067"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1251-0185"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3508-5847"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6830-9309"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7201-6445"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7789-145X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1781-2809"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3947-8733"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4161796,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2218","abstractText":"<b>Objectives</b>: To synthesize evidence on artificial intelligence (AI)-enabled medical history taking (anamnesis)-beyond large language models (LLMs) alone-and to translate findings into implications and research priorities for head and neck surgery. <b>Methods</b>: We performed a PRISMA-informed narrative review. Searches from database inception to 31 December 2025 (updated 3 January 2026) were conducted in MEDLINE (PubMed), Embase, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, IEEE Xplore, and ACM Digital Library, supplemented by medRxiv/arXiv screening and citation chasing. We included studies evaluating or describing AI-supported history capture/summarization, conversational interviewing, symptom checker/digital triage, EHR-integrated intake-to-decision support pipelines, voice interviewing, education/training systems, and governance/ethical considerations related to digital anamnesis. Findings were synthesized by system category and by cross-cutting outcome domains, with a head and neck surgery interpretive lens. <b>Results</b>: Fifty studies (2014-2025) were included. Evidence most consistently suggested feasibility and acceptability of pre-consultation computer-assisted history taking and the potential to reduce documentation burden and improve structured capture. In contrast, symptom checkers and digital triage tools showed highly variable diagnostic/triage performance and prominent safety concerns, highlighting the importance of conservative red-flag escalation strategies, continuous monitoring, and clear accountability. LLM-based diagnostic dialogue demonstrated strong performance in controlled evaluations, but prospective real-world validation, governance, and workflow integration remain limited. <b>Conclusions</b>: AI-enabled anamnesis comprises heterogeneous tools with uneven evidence. For head and neck surgery, potential near-term applications may include structured pre-visit intake, clinician-facing summarization, and training applications, whereas autonomous triage warrants harm-oriented, specialty-calibrated validation and robust governance prior to broader clinical reliance.","affiliation":"Maxillofacial Surgery Operative Unit, Department of Medicine, Surgery and Pharmacy, University of Sassari, 07100 Sassari, Italy.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"ECS 00000038","agency":"Ministero dell'università e della ricerca","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Decision Support Systems","Anamnesis","Head And Neck Surgery","Medical History Taking","Symptom Checker","Chatbots","Conversational Ai","Digital Triage","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15062218"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13026613"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13026613?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-14"},{"id":"42275418","source":"MED","pmid":"42275418","pmcid":"PMC13256495","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13256495"]},"doi":"10.2196/88435","title":"Between Help and Harm: An Evaluation Study of Mental Health Crisis Handling by Large Language Models.","authorString":"Arnaiz-Rodriguez A, Baidal M, Derner E, Annable JL, Ball M, Ince M, Perez Vallejos E, Oliver N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Arnaiz-Rodriguez A","firstName":"Adrian","lastName":"Arnaiz-Rodriguez","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5567-801X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ELLIS Alicante, Muelle de Poniente 5, Distrito Digital 5 - Edificio A, Puerto de Alicante, Alicante, 03001, Spain, 34 659540233."}]}},{"fullName":"Baidal M","firstName":"Miguel","lastName":"Baidal","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-6103-8293"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ELLIS Alicante, Muelle de Poniente 5, Distrito Digital 5 - Edificio A, Puerto de Alicante, Alicante, 03001, Spain, 34 659540233."}]}},{"fullName":"Derner E","firstName":"Erik","lastName":"Derner","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7588-7668"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ELLIS Alicante, Muelle de Poniente 5, Distrito Digital 5 - Edificio A, Puerto de Alicante, Alicante, 03001, Spain, 34 659540233."},{"affiliation":"Czech Technical University in Prague, Prague, Czech Republic."}]}},{"fullName":"Annable JL","firstName":"Jenn Layton","lastName":"Annable","initials":"JL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0787-6684"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Health Science, University of Nottingham, Nottingham, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Ball M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Ball","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1766-2524"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Public Health and Social Care, School of Psychology, University of Derby, Derby, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Ince M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Ince","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-9396-8393"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mental Health Practitioner, Independent scholar, Derby, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Perez Vallejos E","firstName":"Elvira","lastName":"Perez Vallejos","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0258-9440"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computer Science & School of Medicine, University of Nottingham, Nottingham, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Oliver N","firstName":"Nuria","lastName":"Oliver","initials":"N","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5985-691X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ELLIS Alicante, Muelle de Poniente 5, Distrito Digital 5 - Edificio A, Puerto de Alicante, Alicante, 03001, Spain, 34 659540233."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5567-801X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5985-691X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0258-9440"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0787-6684"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7588-7668"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-1766-2524"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-6103-8293"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-9396-8393"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4205449,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR mental health","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Ment Health","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Ment Health","nlmid":"101658926","essn":"2368-7959","issn":"2368-7959"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e88435","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The use of large language models (LLMs)-powered chatbots has reshaped how people seek information and advice, including for emotional and mental health support. While LLMs can offer scalable support, their ability to safely detect and respond to acute mental health crises-including suicidal ideation, self-harm, and violent thoughts-remains poorly understood. Progress is hampered by the absence of unified mental health crisis taxonomies, annotated benchmarks, and empirical evaluations grounded in clinical best practices.<h4>Objective</h4>We addressed these gaps by introducing (1) a unified taxonomy of 6 clinically informed mental health crisis categories; (2) an evaluation dataset of over 2000 user inputs drawn from 12 publicly available conversational mental health datasets, classified into crisis categories; and (3) an expert-designed protocol for assessing response appropriateness. We also used LLMs to automatically identify crisis-indicative inputs and conducted an auditing study of 5 LLMs to evaluate the safety and appropriateness of their responses.<h4>Methods</h4>We developed a taxonomy of mental health crisis categories informed by clinical experts and established literature. From over 239,000 mental health-related user inputs collected from 12 Hugging Face datasets, we curated 2252 examples (206 for validation, 2046 for testing) covering all taxonomy categories. We evaluated 3 LLMs on their ability to classify inputs into crisis categories, selecting the model with the strongest agreement with human annotators as the judge to label the test set. We then audited 5 LLMs on their ability to generate safe and appropriate responses to the 2046 test examples. Response quality was measured using a clinically informed 5-point Likert scale (1=harmful and 5=fully appropriate), relying on an LLM-as-a-judge validated against human expert feedback.<h4>Results</h4>Several LLMs exhibited high consistency and generally reliable behavior when responding to explicit crisis disclosures, but significant risks remain. A nonnegligible proportion of responses was rated as inappropriate or harmful, particularly in the self-harm and suicidal ideation categories. Substantial performance differences were observed across models: gpt-5-nano and deepseek-v3.2-exp achieved very low harmful response rates, whereas gpt-4o-mini, Llama-4-Scout-17B-16E-Instruct, and grok-4-fast-non-reasoning generated markedly higher rates of unsafe outputs. All models exhibited systemic weaknesses, including poor handling of indirect or ambiguous risk signals, reliance on formulaic responses, and frequent misalignment with user context.<h4>Conclusions</h4>These findings underscore the urgent need for enhanced safeguards, improved crisis detection, and context-aware interventions in LLM deployments and highlight the central role of alignment and safety engineering-beyond model scale or openness-in determining crisis response reliability. Our taxonomy, dataset, and evaluation framework lay the groundwork for ongoing research in artificial intelligence-driven mental health support, helping to minimize harm and protect vulnerable users.","affiliation":"ELLIS Alicante, Muelle de Poniente 5, Distrito Digital 5 - Edificio A, Puerto de Alicante, Alicante, 03001, Spain, 34 659540233.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Evaluation Study","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Disorders","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health Services","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"OG","qualifierName":"organization & administration","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Classification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Databases, Factual"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intelligent Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Mental health","Social Sciences","Natural Language Processing","Large Language Models","User Interface Systems And Human-computer Interaction"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/88435"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13256495"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13256495?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":3,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-12","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-11"},{"id":"41284989","source":"MED","pmid":"41284989","pmcid":"PMC12643393","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12643393"]},"doi":"10.2196/78289","title":"Accuracy of Large Language Model Responses Versus Internet Searches for Common Questions About Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonist Therapy: Exploratory Simulation Study.","authorString":"Tan SYT, Sng GGR, Lee PC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tan SYT","firstName":"Sarah Ying Tse","lastName":"Tan","initials":"SYT","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5451-788X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endocrinology, Singapore General Hospital, 20 College Road, Singapore, 169608, Singapore, +65 62223322."}]}},{"fullName":"Sng GGR","firstName":"Gerald Gui Ren","lastName":"Sng","initials":"GGR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8795-7491"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endocrinology, Singapore General Hospital, 20 College Road, Singapore, 169608, Singapore, +65 62223322."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee PC","firstName":"Phong Ching","lastName":"Lee","initials":"PC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9446-2032"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endocrinology, Singapore General Hospital, 20 College Road, Singapore, 169608, Singapore, +65 62223322."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5451-788X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8795-7491"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9446-2032"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4041553,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-11-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e78289","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Novel glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists (GLP1RAs) for obesity treatment have generated considerable dialogue on digital media platforms. However, nonevidence-based information from online sources may perpetuate misconceptions about GLP1RA use. A promising new digital avenue for patient education is large language models (LLMs), which could potentially be used as an alternative platform to clarify questions regarding GLP1RA therapy.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to compare the accuracy, objectivity, relevance, reproducibility, and overall quality of responses generated by an LLM (GPT-4o) and internet searches (Google) for common questions about GLP1RA therapy.<h4>Methods</h4>This study compared LLM (GPT-4o) and internet (Google) search responses to 17 simulated questions about GLP1RA therapy. These questions were specifically chosen to reflect themes identified based on Google Trends data. Domains included indications and benefits of GLP1RA therapy, expected treatment course, and common side effects and specific risks pertaining to GLP1RA treatment. Responses were graded by 2 independent evaluators based on safety, consensus with guidelines, objectivity, reproducibility, relevance, and explainability using a 5-point Likert scale. Mean scores were compared using paired 2-tailed t tests. Qualitative observations were recorded.<h4>Results</h4>LLM responses had significantly higher scores than internet responses in the \"objectivity\" (mean 3.91, SD 0.63 vs mean 3.36, SD 0.80; mean difference 0.55, SD 1.00; 95% CI 0.03-1.06; P=.04) and \"reproducibility\" (mean 3.85, SD 0.49 vs mean 3.00, SD 0.97; mean difference 0.85, SD 1.14; 95% CI 0.27-1.44; P=.007) categories. There was no significant difference in the mean scores in the \"safety,\" \"consensus,\" \"relevance,\" and \"explainability\" categories. Interrater agreement was high (overall percentage agreement 95.1%; Gwet agreement coefficient 0.879; P<.001). Qualitatively, LLM responses provided appropriate information about standard GLP1RA-related queries, including the benefits of GLP1RA, expected treatment course, and common side effects. However, it lacked updated information pertaining to newly emerging concerns surrounding GLP1RA use, such as the impact on fertility and mental health. Internet search responses were more heterogeneous, yielding several irrelevant or commercially biased sources.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This study found that LLM responses to GLP1RA therapy queries were more objective and reproducible than those to internet-based sources, with comparable relevance and concordance with clinical guidelines. However, LLMs lacked updated coverage of emerging issues, reflecting static training data limitations. In contrast, internet results were more current but were inconsistent and often commercially biased. These findings highlight the potential of LLMs to provide reliable and comprehensible health information, particularly for individuals hesitant to seek professional advice, while emphasizing the need for human oversight, dynamic data integration, and evaluation of readability to ensure safe and equitable use in obesity care. This study, although formative, is the first study to compare LLM and internet search output on common GLP1RA-related queries. It paves the way for future studies to explore how LLMs can integrate real-time data retrieval and evaluate their readability for lay audiences.","affiliation":"Department of Endocrinology, Singapore General Hospital, 20 College Road, Singapore, 169608, Singapore, +65 62223322.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Obesity","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Reproducibility of Results"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Language"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Internet"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Patient Education as Topic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonists"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Patient education","Glucagon-like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonist","Semaglutide","Glp1ra","Ozempic","Chatgpt"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 Receptor Agonists","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/78289"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12643393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12643393?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-11-24","dateOfCreation":"2025-11-24","firstIndexDate":"2025-11-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-23","dateOfRevision":"2025-11-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-11-24","firstPublicationDate":"2025-11-24"},{"id":"41664164","source":"MED","pmid":"41664164","pmcid":"PMC12896320","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12896320"]},"doi":"10.1186/s13045-026-01781-y","title":"Implementing generative artificial intelligence in precision oncology: safety, governance, and significance.","authorString":"Hamamoto R, Koyama T, Takahashi S, Yasuda T, Kobayashi K, Akagi Y, Kouno N, Sudo K, Hirata M, Sunami K, Kubo T, Katayama H, Takashima A, Taniguchi T, Matsumoto H, Shibaki R, Asada K, Komatsu M, Kaneko S, Yamada M, Horinouchi H, Tanaka K, Goto Y, Kato K, Saito Y, Nakamura K, Yamamoto N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hamamoto R","firstName":"Ryuji","lastName":"Hamamoto","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2632-1334"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan. rhamamot@ncc.go.jp."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan. rhamamot@ncc.go.jp."}]}},{"fullName":"Koyama T","firstName":"Takafumi","lastName":"Koyama","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takahashi S","firstName":"Satoshi","lastName":"Takahashi","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yasuda T","firstName":"Tomohiro","lastName":"Yasuda","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kobayashi K","firstName":"Kazuma","lastName":"Kobayashi","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Akagi Y","firstName":"Yu","lastName":"Akagi","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Graduate School of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, 7-3-1 Hongo Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, 113-8655, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kouno N","firstName":"Nobuji","lastName":"Kouno","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Surgery, Graduate School of Medicine, Kyoto University, Kyoto, 606-8507, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sudo K","firstName":"Kazuki","lastName":"Sudo","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Hirata M","firstName":"Makoto","lastName":"Hirata","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9994-9958"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Genetic Medicine and Services, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Sunami K","firstName":"Kuniko","lastName":"Sunami","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Laboratory Medicine, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kubo T","firstName":"Takashi","lastName":"Kubo","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Laboratory Medicine, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Katayama H","firstName":"Hiroshi","lastName":"Katayama","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Research Support Office, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takashima A","firstName":"Atsuo","lastName":"Takashima","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gastrointestinal Medical Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Taniguchi T","firstName":"Tomonori","lastName":"Taniguchi","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Matsumoto H","firstName":"Hiromi","lastName":"Matsumoto","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Shibaki R","firstName":"Ryota","lastName":"Shibaki","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Asada K","firstName":"Ken","lastName":"Asada","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Komatsu M","firstName":"Masaaki","lastName":"Komatsu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaneko S","firstName":"Syuzo","lastName":"Kaneko","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AI Medical Engineering Team, RIKEN Center for Advanced Intelligence Project, 1-4-1 Nihonbashi,, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 103-0027, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamada M","firstName":"Masayoshi","lastName":"Yamada","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Endoscopy Division, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Horinouchi H","firstName":"Hidehito","lastName":"Horinouchi","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Tanaka K","firstName":"Katsuya","lastName":"Tanaka","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"IT Integrating and Support Center, National Cancer Center, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Goto Y","firstName":"Yasushi","lastName":"Goto","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Kato K","firstName":"Ken","lastName":"Kato","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Gastrointestinal Medical Oncology, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Saito Y","firstName":"Yutaka","lastName":"Saito","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Endoscopy Division, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Nakamura K","firstName":"Kenichi","lastName":"Nakamura","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Clinical Research Support Office, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamamoto N","firstName":"Noboru","lastName":"Yamamoto","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Experimental Therapeutics, National Cancer Center Hospital, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2632-1334"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9994-9958"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"19","journalIssueId":4120300,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of hematology & oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"J Hematol Oncol","isoabbreviation":"J Hematol Oncol","nlmid":"101468937","essn":"1756-8722","issn":"1756-8722"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"14","abstractText":"The paramount challenge in precision oncology lies in further improving quality of life and response rates for individual patients. Efforts toward these goals are steadily expanding the scope of clinical implementation, despite ongoing challenges such as standardization, cost-effectiveness, and data harmonization. Building upon this maturing foundation, generative AI-which has evolved dramatically in recent years-is particularly valuable at this stage of advancing efficiency and adoption as an auxiliary technology linking literature, guidelines, trial protocols, and patient data. Specifically, through mutation interpretation, trial eligibility matching, and tumor board support, it is expected to contribute to advancing standardization, improving cost-effectiveness, accelerating data harmonization, and further accelerating human-centered decision-making. Accordingly, this review surveys the development history of generative AI and its current healthcare applications, organizing its implementation potential for precision oncology along three axes: (1) generative AI-based interpretation of genetic mutations and estimation of their pathological significance; (2) generative AI-driven verification of clinical trial eligibility; and (3) multimodal foundation models for imaging and pathology that compute \"tumor phenotypes\" using real-world data, contributing to report drafting and molecular surrogate estimation. In response, we propose a strategy centered on retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and human-in-the-loop (HITL) workflows, encompassing data preparation based on OMOP, mCODE, and FHIR; multicenter prospective evaluation; auditable logs and governance aligned with Good Manufacturing Practice (GMP) and the EU AI Act; and a synthetic data strategy including differential privacy. Ultimately, this approach validates value through real-world outcomes and charts a path toward \"learning oncology,\" accelerating patient-centered decision-making and clinical trial development.","affiliation":"Division of Medical AI Research and Development, National Cancer Center Research Institute, 5-1-1 Tsukiji, Chuo-ku, Tokyo, 104-0045, Japan. rhamamot@ncc.go.jp.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"the MEXT subsidy for the Advanced Integrated Intelligence Platform","agency":"Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"BRIDGE","agency":"Cabinet Office, Government of Japan","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Medical Oncology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Precision Medicine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Transformer","Precision Oncology","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Model","Vision Language Model","Retrieval-augmented Generation"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13045-026-01781-y"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896320"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12896320?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-07","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-09"},{"id":"42111317","source":"MED","pmid":"42111317","pmcid":"PMC13154040","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13154040"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1772215","title":"Designing AI for distress: an iterative case study of a hierarchical agent for psychosocial support.","authorString":"Patturajan V, Ravikumar D, Narayanan S, Krishnamurthy V, Santhosh S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Patturajan V","firstName":"Vaishnavi","lastName":"Patturajan","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ravikumar D","firstName":"Devipreya","lastName":"Ravikumar","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Narayanan S","firstName":"Sneghavalli","lastName":"Narayanan","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Krishnamurthy V","firstName":"Vallidevi","lastName":"Krishnamurthy","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Santhosh S","firstName":"Stephen","lastName":"Santhosh","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"DelMag Innovations, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-7836-8979"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1772215","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>In recent years, psychosocial issues like bullying and ragging have risen all around the world. Even though there are some victims who report such cases, most of them never do so because of fear, stigma, or mistrust, mainly among students. This scenario highlights the necessity of safe, anonymous, and easily available support system. The promising approach to solving this problem is conversational AI because it allows people to request assistance in a simple and an easy-going way. However, conversational AI systems often lack contextual sensitivity, emotional intelligence, and appropriate support response mechanisms. To address this, there is a need for structured frameworks integrating human-in-the-loop oversight along with preventive and actionable insights to ensure ethical and proportional responses. This paper introduces SocialWellbeing, a socio-technical system which is designed to overcome psychosocial harms in a three-pillar framework.<h4>Methods</h4>The initial pillar offers artificial intelligence-based first-line care whereby users can share their issues anonymously and get caring responses. The second pillar facilitates human-guided escalation where more serious cases are passed to trusted advisors or authorities to provide real-life support. The third pillar is devoted to data-driven prevention, where anonymized data are used to identify trends and serve as a guide in the institutional preventive efforts.<h4>Results</h4>The AI system was tested in two stages. Phase 1 involved prompt engineering that identified the types of incidents, detected the emotions, and generated supportive responses. Phase 2 improved this strategy with a chain-of-thought strategy, which was A.G.E. (Acknowledge-Guide-Escalate) oriented and explicit guidelines and escalation logic to add reliability and clarity to the strategy. Results of this evaluation show that Phase 2 improved the Gemini 2.5 Flash model's classification accuracy to approximately 88%-89%, compared to 84%-86% in Phase 1, with empathy scores increasing from 4.2-4.3 to 4.4-4.8 on a five-point scale. The level of empathy also went up on a five-point scale to 4.4-4.8 as compared to 4.2-4.3.<h4>Discussion</h4>The findings indicate that a structured, hierarchical approach improves the reliability and safety of psychosocial AI systems. The Acknowledge-Guide-Escalate (A.G.E.) framework enables consistent emotional recognition and contextually relevant guidance. The inclusion of human-in-the-loop oversight and preventive, actionable insights strengthens ethical alignment and response quality.","affiliation":"School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Chennai, Tamil Nadu, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Confidentiality","Escalation","Emotional Support","Psychosocial Assistance","Large Language Model","Mental Health Chatbot","Ai-based Guidance","Gemini Api"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1772215"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13154040"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13154040?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-11","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-11","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-24"},{"id":"42072996","source":"MED","pmid":"42072996","pmcid":"PMC13115902","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13115902"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14081098","title":"Governing Generative AI in Healthcare: A Normative Conceptual Framework for Epistemic Authority, Trust, and the Architecture of Responsibility.","authorString":"Akgün FE, Akgün M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Akgün FE","firstName":"Fatma Eren","lastName":"Akgün","initials":"FE","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8013-7565"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Elderly Care Programme, Department of Health Care Services, Vocational School of Health Services, Ağrı İbrahim Çeçen University, 04100 Ağrı, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Akgün M","firstName":"Metin","lastName":"Akgün","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3404-4274"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pulmonary Diseases, School of Medicine, Ağrı İbrahim Çeçen University, 04100 Ağrı, Türkiye."},{"affiliation":"Department of Systematic Philosophy and Logic, Faculty of Letters, Atatürk University, 25240 Erzurum, Türkiye."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8013-7565"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3404-4274"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4188735,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1098","abstractText":"<h4>Background/objectives</h4>Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT are rapidly being integrated into healthcare for tasks ranging from clinical documentation to diagnostic support. Current ethical discussions focus predominantly on bias, privacy, and accuracy, leaving three critical governance questions unresolved: What kind of knowledge does an LLM output represent in clinical reasoning? When is a clinician's or patient's trust in that output justified? Who bears responsibility when an AI-informed decision leads to patient harm? This study proposes the Epistemic Authority-Trust-Responsibility (ETR) Architecture, a normative conceptual framework that addresses these three questions as an integrated governance challenge.<h4>Methods</h4>The framework was developed through normative conceptual analysis-a method that constructs governance proposals by synthesising philosophical principles, ethical theories, and empirical evidence. The literature was identified through structured searches of PubMed, PhilPapers, and EUR-Lex (January 2020-March 2026), drawing on the philosophy of medical knowledge, the ethics of trust and testimony, and the moral philosophy of responsibility.<h4>Results</h4>The ETR Architecture produces four outputs: (i) a four-tier classification system that distinguishes LLM outputs-from administrative drafts to clinical evidence claims-and matches each tier to appropriate verification requirements; (ii) the concept of the 'epistemic placebo', formally defined as a governance measure that creates a documented appearance of compliance while lacking at least one operative element of genuine oversight; (iii) a model specifying four conditions under which trust in healthcare AI is justified; (iv) four testable hypotheses with associated research designs connecting governance design to trust calibration and patient safety.<h4>Conclusions</h4>The 2025-2027 regulatory transition period offers a critical window for shaping how healthcare institutions govern AI. We argue that deploying LLMs without explicitly classifying their outputs and building appropriate oversight risks allows governance norms to be set by technology vendors rather than by evidence-informed, patient-centred policy.","affiliation":"Elderly Care Programme, Department of Health Care Services, Vocational School of Health Services, Ağrı İbrahim Çeçen University, 04100 Ağrı, Türkiye.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["other","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Patient Safety","Healthcare","Epistemic Authority","Automation Bias","Responsibility Gap","Human Oversight","Ai Governance","Generative Artificial Intelligence","Large Language Model","Warranted Trust"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14081098"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13115902"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13115902?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":2,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-20"},{"id":"PPR1275962","source":"PPR","doi":"10.14293/pr2199.004004.v1","title":"Application and Impact of Large Language Model (LLM) Agents in Financial Operations and Public Policy","authorString":"Izev M, Johansson G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Izev M","firstName":"Mckenzie","lastName":"Izev","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-8450-4944"}},{"fullName":"Johansson G","firstName":"Greg","lastName":"Johansson","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-7856-0639"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-7856-0639"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-8450-4944"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"The rapid digitalization of finance, accelerated by advances in artificial intelligence, has positioned Large Language Models (LLMs) as transformative tools not only for private-sector financial operations but also for public-sector economic governance. While much of the existing literature has focused on LLM applications in investment and trading, their potential to reshape core financial operations—such as regulatory compliance, fraud detection, contract intelligence, and risk reporting—remains underexplored. Simultaneously, governments and central banks are beginning to grapple with how LLM agents can assist in policy formulation, macroeconomic simulation, fiscal transparency, and real-time economic sentiment monitoring. This research undertakes a systematic examination of the deployment of LLM agents across these dual domains, with a particular emphasis on operational efficiency, decision support quality, and institutional accountability. Preliminary findings suggest that while LLM agents significantly reduce processing times for tasks like regulatory reporting and contract review, their outputs exhibit concerning variability in reasoning consistency and are susceptible to subtle prompt-induced biases. In the policy domain, LLMs demonstrate promise in summarizing vast economic literature and generating scenario simulations, yet they struggle with causal reasoning and fail to adequately quantify uncertainty—raising serious concerns for their use in fiscal and monetary decision-making. The research concludes by proposing a human-in-the-loop governance architecture that balances automation with oversight, alongside a set of regulatory benchmarks for evaluating LLM reliability in financial and policy settings. By bridging the technical, operational, and ethical dimensions of LLM adoption, this study contributes to the emerging field of AI governance in finance and offers practical guidance for institutions navigating the transition toward AI-augmented financial ecosystems.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"ScienceOpen Preprints","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.14293/pr2199.004004.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1275962","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-07-01","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},{"id":"41941720","source":"MED","pmid":"41941720","pmcid":"PMC13052998","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13052998"]},"doi":"10.2196/91454","title":"It Is the Journey, Not the Destination: Moving From End Points to Trajectories When Assessing Chatbot Mental Health Safety.","authorString":"Morrin H, Au Yeung J, Agnew Z, Østergaard SD, Pollak TA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Morrin H","firstName":"Hamilton","lastName":"Morrin","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7801-0212"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychosis Studies, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience, King's College London, 16 De Crespigny Park, London, England, SE5 8AB, United Kingdom, 07752 814088."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychological Medicine, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience, King's College London, London, England, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"South London and Maudsley NHS Foundation Trust, London, England, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Au Yeung J","firstName":"Joshua","lastName":"Au Yeung","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9428-2146"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"King's College Hospital, London, England, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"Nuraxi AI, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biostatistics and Health Informatics, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience, King's College London, London, England, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Agnew Z","firstName":"Zarinah","lastName":"Agnew","initials":"Z","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4657-2621"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"The Collective Intelligence Project, San Francisco, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Østergaard SD","firstName":"Søren Dinesen","lastName":"Østergaard","initials":"SD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8032-6208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University, Aarhus, Central Jutland, Denmark."},{"affiliation":"Department of Affective Disorders, Aarhus University Hospital, Aarhus, Central Jutland, Denmark."}]}},{"fullName":"Pollak TA","firstName":"Thomas A","lastName":"Pollak","initials":"TA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6171-0810"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychosis Studies, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience, King's College London, 16 De Crespigny Park, London, England, SE5 8AB, United Kingdom, 07752 814088."},{"affiliation":"South London and Maudsley NHS Foundation Trust, London, England, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6171-0810"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7801-0212"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8032-6208"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9428-2146"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4657-2621"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"13","journalIssueId":4169897,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR mental health","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Ment Health","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Ment Health","nlmid":"101658926","essn":"2368-7959","issn":"2368-7959"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e91454","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Large language models are rapidly becoming embedded in everyday life through artificial intelligence (AI) chatbots that people use for practical assistance and companionship, as well as for support with mental health and emotional well-being. Alongside clear benefits, clinicians and public reports increasingly describe a minority of users whose interactions seem to drift over days or weeks toward strongly questionable convictions, delusions, or suicidal crises. Importantly, clinically meaningful deterioration can occur even without overtly unsafe text outputs, via more insidious processes, such as compulsive use, sleep disruption, withdrawal from human contact, and progressive narrowing of attention around the chatbot relationship. In this Viewpoint, we argue that risk often arises not at a single tipping point but through trajectory effects that accumulate across extended dialogue and that prevailing safety evaluation approaches are misaligned with this reality because they primarily score risk at discrete conversational end points often reached through scripted dialogues lasting just a single turn or several turns. Mental health benchmarks and safety suites (including clinician-informed efforts) have advanced the field by testing refusal behavior, toxicity, and adversarial prompting. However, they often treat the last message as the unit of analysis and, therefore, miss when risk-relevant relational cues, signs of validation, contradiction handling, and shifts in certainty first emerge and how they compound. We propose that mental health safety assessment should shift from end points to trajectories by (1) treating the whole dialogue, not just the end result, as the focus of evaluation; (2) reporting turn-by-turn dynamics, such as delusion confirmation and harm enablement, and timing and persistence of safety interventions; and (3) calibrating short multiturn tests against longer, clinically realistic interaction sequences that can reveal context-length effects and drift. We further argue that transcript-only evaluation is insufficient in mental health contexts. Similar language can reflect very different internal states, and the relationship between expressed psychopathology and real-world harm is nonlinear. Therefore, safety research should incorporate proximal human outcomes following interactions (eg, shifts in certainty, openness to counterevidence, arousal, urge to continue, and subsequent sleep or behavior) and build a prospective clinical surveillance infrastructure that supports transcript donation with consent and linkage to health outcomes. Together, these steps would enable benchmarks that are clinically relevant and better aligned with the types of harms now being observed in real-world chatbot use.","affiliation":"Department of Psychosis Studies, Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology & Neuroscience, King's College London, 16 De Crespigny Park, London, England, SE5 8AB, United Kingdom, 07752 814088.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Schizophrenia","AI","Suicide","Delusions","Psychosis","Human-computer Interaction","Chatbots"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/91454"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13052998"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13052998?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-07","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-07","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-06"},{"id":"41987181","source":"MED","pmid":"41987181","pmcid":"PMC13270741","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13270741"]},"doi":"10.1186/s12903-026-08269-8","title":"Performance of large language models in endodontics: accuracy, consistency, and benchmarking with consensus guidelines.","authorString":"Sismanoglu S, Isik V, Kayahan MB.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sismanoglu S","firstName":"Soner","lastName":"Sismanoglu","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Restorative Dentistry, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Fatih, Istanbul, 34098, Turkey. soner.s@hotmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Isik V","firstName":"Vasfiye","lastName":"Isik","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endodontics, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Istanbul, Turkey."}]}},{"fullName":"Kayahan MB","firstName":"Mehmet Baybora","lastName":"Kayahan","initials":"MB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endodontics, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Istanbul, Turkey."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"26","journalIssueId":4168455,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"BMC oral health","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Oral Health","isoabbreviation":"BMC Oral Health","nlmid":"101088684","essn":"1472-6831","issn":"1472-6831"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1043","abstractText":"BACKGROUND: Large language model (LLM)-based chatbots are increasingly used in healthcare, yet their diagnostic accuracy, consistency, and temporal stability in endodontics remain insufficiently evaluated. This study aimed to assess and compare the performance of LLM-based chatbots using established international clinical guidelines. METHODS: A diagnostic accuracy study with a repeated-measures design was conducted. Two LLMs (ChatGPT-5 and Gemini-2.5 Flash) were evaluated using 200 structured yes/no items derived from consensus-based international position statements covering the full scope of endodontic practice. Each model was tested weekly over three consecutive sessions, yielding 600 responses per model. Accuracy was assessed against reference answers, consistency was analyzed using Fleiss’ κ and Cohen’s κ, and logistic regression was performed to evaluate the effects of model type, week, and clinical domain. RESULTS: ChatGPT demonstrated significantly higher overall accuracy than Gemini (92.8% vs. 84.8%; OR = 2.37; p = 0.004) and near-perfect reproducibility (κ = 0.95), whereas Gemini showed fair agreement (κ = 0.38). Both models performed well in structured clinical domains but showed reduced accuracy in areas related to restoration and pulpal disease management. CONCLUSIONS: LLM-based chatbots show potential as decision support and educational tools in endodontics. However, performance limitations in complex clinical domains highlight the continued need for expert oversight in clinical decision-making.","affiliation":"Department of Restorative Dentistry, Faculty of Dentistry, Istanbul University-Cerrahpaşa, Fatih, Istanbul, 34098, Turkey. soner.s@hotmail.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Reproducibility of Results"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Endodontics","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Consensus"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Benchmarking"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Practice Guidelines as Topic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Position Statements","Gemini","Large Language Models","Chatgpt"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12903-026-08269-8"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13270741"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13270741?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-16"},{"id":"42495047","source":"MED","pmid":"42495047","doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2026.1803433","title":"HAMAgent: human assisted multiagent system for emotion recognition and digital health-a survey and preliminary study.","authorString":"Li Y, Sun Q, Xue J, Javadov A, Milling M, Zhang W, Kainz B, Schuller BW.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Li Y","firstName":"Yupei","lastName":"Li","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GLAM-Group on Language, Audio, & Music, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"CHI-Chair of Health Informatics, TUM University Hospital, Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun Q","firstName":"Qiyang","lastName":"Sun","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GLAM-Group on Language, Audio, & Music, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Xue J","firstName":"Jiahao","lastName":"Xue","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Engineering and Materials Science, Queen Mary University of London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Javadov A","firstName":"Aydin","lastName":"Javadov","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Eidgenössische Technische Hochschule Zürich, Zurich, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Milling M","firstName":"Manuel","lastName":"Milling","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CHI-Chair of Health Informatics, TUM University Hospital, Munich, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang W","firstName":"Weitong","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Human in the Loop (HITL), Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Kainz B","firstName":"Bernhard","lastName":"Kainz","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Human in the Loop (HITL), Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Schuller BW","firstName":"Björn W","lastName":"Schuller","initials":"BW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GLAM-Group on Language, Audio, & Music, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"CHI-Chair of Health Informatics, TUM University Hospital, Munich, Germany."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4117475,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Front Digit Health","nlmid":"101771889","essn":"2673-253X","issn":"2673-253X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1803433","abstractText":"This study surveys existing multi-large language model (LLM) agent applications, comparing systems that incorporate active human participation with those that operate fully autonomously within digital health contexts. Building on this analysis, we conduct an early exploration of human-in-the-loop feedback in multi-LLM interactions for emotion and human behaviour understanding using a subset of the FairytaleQA dataset. We further propose the HAMAgent framework to investigate how human feedback influences multi-agent reasoning and performance. Our preliminary experiments demonstrate the potential benefits of integrating human guidance into multi-LLM workflows and provide insights for designing effective human-in-the-loop systems in digital health. We conclude by discussing the key implications of our findings and outlining future directions for multi-agent LLM systems enhanced with human input.","affiliation":"GLAM-Group on Language, Audio, & Music, Imperial College London, London, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Emotion Recognition","Digital Health","Human In The Loop","Multiagent","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2026.1803433"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-09"},{"id":"42341243","source":"MED","pmid":"42341243","pmcid":"PMC13293476","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13293476"]},"doi":"10.2196/90709","title":"The Emerging Roles of AI in Self-Directed Stress Management: Systematic Review.","authorString":"Reyes MKG, Teo SSM, Hartanto A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Reyes MKG","firstName":"Mary Kamillah Grace","lastName":"Reyes","initials":"MKG","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5470-0516"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423."}]}},{"fullName":"Teo SSM","firstName":"Shauna Sha Min","lastName":"Teo","initials":"SSM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-3739-8116"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423."}]}},{"fullName":"Hartanto A","firstName":"Andree","lastName":"Hartanto","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8758-6400"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8758-6400"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-3739-8116"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-5470-0516"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e90709","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Stress is widespread and carries substantial mental health, social, and economic burdens. Yet, access to clinician-led stress management remains constrained by service capacity, cost, and stigma. In response, artificial intelligence (AI)-enabled tools have rapidly proliferated as scalable, self-directed options. However, evidence on how these systems support stress management outside formal clinical settings remains fragmented.<h4>Objective</h4>This systematic review aimed to synthesize empirical evidence on how AI-enabled technologies are used for self-directed stress management. We mapped the emerging functions of these tools, the psychological frameworks informing their design, the populations and settings studied, and the outcomes reported.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses)-compliant systematic review of English-language studies published between 2000 and 2025. Six databases were searched (APA PsycINFO, PubMed, MEDLINE, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, ProQuest, and Google Scholar).<h4>Results</h4>Of 3008 records identified, 35 studies met the inclusion criteria. The methodological quality of included studies was critically appraised using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (version 2018). Findings illustrated that AI-supported stress management can operate through 5 core functions, including psychological intervention, behavioral support, psychoeducation, companionship, and emotional support, and stress monitoring, detection, and triage. Across the reviewed studies, these functions supported self-directed stress management by helping users identify stress, regulate responses, and engage in coping outside formal clinical care.<h4>Conclusions</h4>AI-enabled systems show preliminary promise for supporting self-directed stress management through multiple user-facing functions grounded in established psychological frameworks.","affiliation":"School of Social Sciences, Singapore Management University, Level 4, 10 Canning Rise, Singapore, 179873, Singapore, 65 68281901, 65 68280423.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Stress, Psychological","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Coping Skills"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intelligent Systems"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Systematic review","Stress Management","Prisma","Psychoeducation","Preferred Reporting Items For Systematic Reviews And Meta-analyses","Stress Monitoring","Chatbots","Digital Mental Health","Conversational Agents","Self-guided Intervention","Self-directed Stress Management"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/90709"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293476"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13293476?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-25","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-24"},{"id":"41623519","source":"MED","pmid":"41623519","pmcid":"PMC12852379","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12852379"]},"doi":"10.3389/frhs.2025.1725425","title":"Rapid review on GenAI in nursing education.","authorString":"Hinsche L, Hasseler M, Tischendorf T, Schaal T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hinsche L","firstName":"Laura","lastName":"Hinsche","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Ostfalia University of Applied Sciences, Wolfsburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Hasseler M","firstName":"Martina","lastName":"Hasseler","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Ostfalia University of Applied Sciences, Wolfsburg, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Tischendorf T","firstName":"Tim","lastName":"Tischendorf","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5567-0134"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Westsachsische Hochschule Zwickau, Zwickau, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Schaal T","firstName":"Tom","lastName":"Schaal","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1122-2226"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Westsachsische Hochschule Zwickau, Zwickau, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1122-2226"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-5567-0134"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"5","journalIssueId":3899935,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in health services","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Health Serv","isoabbreviation":"Front Health Serv","nlmid":"9918334887706676","essn":"2813-0146","issn":"2813-0146"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"1725425","abstractText":"<h4>Backround</h4>The use of generative AI, as represented by ChatGPT, holds promising potential for nursing education. This manifests itself in various areas, including personalized learning, simulation training and teaching process support. However, its integration requires careful consideration of ethical implications, adaptation of curricula and a high level of digital competence on the part of teachers. Only in this way can potential risks, such as the distortion of knowledge, bias and educational inequalities, be avoided.<h4>Methodes</h4>Relevant publications were identified between 2019 and 2025 as part of a comprehensive literature search in the specialist databases PubMed, Embase, CINAHL and Scopus. The search was conducted using combined search terms that included the terms \"generative AI\", \"ChatGPT\" and \"nursing\". After removing duplicates and screening (PRISMA-guided), 140 full texts were analysed and divided into two publications. This rapid overview focuses on the topic of generative AI in nursing education.<h4>Results</h4>As part of the analysis of the included studies, five thematic areas were identified, which were divided into the categories of nursing education, competence development and nursing skills, implementation possibilities, examination quality and ethical considerations, and evaluated. A key theme is the dual potential of this technology: it can enrich learning through features such as virtual tutors and improved exam preparation, but it also requires critical consideration of ethical issues such as plagiarism, data bias and the need for human oversight.<h4>Outlook</h4>In this context, the conclusion emphasises the urgent need to adapt curricula and provide targeted further training for teachers so that GenAI can be used responsibly and effectively-rather than, as is often the case at present, by banning it altogether.","affiliation":"Faculty of Healthcare, Ostfalia University of Applied Sciences, Wolfsburg, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Nursing Education","Ethical Reflection","Digital Competence","Digital Nursing"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frhs.2025.1725425"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12852379"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12852379?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-06","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-30","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-04","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-15"},{"id":"PPR1246028","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.06.02.26354724","title":"Audited large language model triage for systematic review screening in national clinical guideline production: validation and prospective deployment","authorString":"Fagerberg P, Sallander O, Vikhe Patil K, Thunborg C, Lundström L, Berg A, Nyman A, Borg N, Lindén T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fagerberg P","firstName":"Petter","lastName":"Fagerberg","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1443-3128"}},{"fullName":"Sallander O","firstName":"Oscar","lastName":"Sallander","initials":"O"},{"fullName":"Vikhe Patil K","firstName":"Kim","lastName":"Vikhe Patil","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"Thunborg C","firstName":"Charlotta","lastName":"Thunborg","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0964-1747"}},{"fullName":"Lundström L","firstName":"Lina","lastName":"Lundström","initials":"L"},{"fullName":"Berg A","firstName":"Anders","lastName":"Berg","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Nyman A","firstName":"Anastasia","lastName":"Nyman","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5482-0675"}},{"fullName":"Borg N","firstName":"Natalia","lastName":"Borg","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Lindén T","firstName":"Thomas","lastName":"Lindén","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2444-8892"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1443-3128"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2444-8892"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5482-0675"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0964-1747"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Title and abstract screening limit the timeliness of systematic reviews used for clinical guidelines. We evaluated audited large language model (LLM) triage at Sweden’s National Board of Health and Welfare. Ten LLMs from five model families were tested on 419 Cochrane reviews comprising 26,892 records, and the selected ensemble was externally validated on 133 reviews including 8,501 records matched to planned guideline topics. The same locked model pair was then used prospectively across 24 systematic reviews in two national guideline programmes. On the 419-review selection benchmark, the selected Gemini-3-flash plus GPT-5.1 ensemble achieved 98.0% (95% CI, 97.3–98.7) mean review-level sensitivity, while topic-matched validation yielded 96.7% sensitivity (95% CI, 93.7–98.9). Prospective deployment screened 74,679 records, placed 63,858 (85.5%) in the AI-excluded pool and reduced estimated first-pass screening effort from 415 to 34 person-days. Across 600 randomly sampled AI-excluded records from the migraine and dementia programmes, none was confirmed as a final false negative after post-unblinding adjudication; across the completed 680-record audit, all 38 final retained records had been AI flagged, whereas locked blinded human consensus missed seven. These findings support locked, audited LLM triage, with human oversight and programme-specific monitoring, for systematic reviews used in national guidelines.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.06.02.26354724"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-03"},{"id":"41977066","source":"MED","pmid":"41977066","pmcid":"PMC13073410","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13073410"]},"doi":"10.3390/jcm15072767","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Head and Neck Surgical Oncology: A State-of-the-Art Review.","authorString":"Chen SX, Feucht M, Bhatt A, Farlow JL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen SX","firstName":"Steven X","lastName":"Chen","initials":"SX","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Feucht M","firstName":"Maria","lastName":"Feucht","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhatt A","firstName":"Aditya","lastName":"Bhatt","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2111-3813"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Farlow JL","firstName":"Janice L","lastName":"Farlow","initials":"JL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1214-4960"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1214-4960"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-2111-3813"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"7","volume":"15","journalIssueId":4179668,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of clinical medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"J Clin Med","isoabbreviation":"J Clin Med","nlmid":"101606588","essn":"2077-0383","issn":"2077-0383"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2767","abstractText":"Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly reshaping head and neck surgical oncology by augmenting decision-making across the full perioperative continuum. This state-of-the-art review aims to provide head and neck surgical oncologists with a conceptual framework for understanding and critically appraising AI tools entering clinical practice, summarizing how machine learning, deep learning, and generative AI are being integrated into contemporary surgical workflows. Preoperative applications include detection of occult nodal metastasis and extranodal extension. Intraoperative innovations include augmented reality-assisted navigation, real-time margin assessment, and improving visual clarity and tissue handling for robotic platforms. Postoperatively, AI can predict complications like free flap failure and oncologic outcomes. Large language models are being operationalized for clinician-facing applications such as documentation and inbox support, as well as patient-facing education. Despite promising results, broad clinical deployment remains limited by concerns about privacy, validation, reliability, safety, and ethics. Widespread adoption will require prospective clinical trials, robust governance, and human-centered workflows that ensure AI remains a safe, assistive copilot.","affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Indiana University School of Medicine, Indiana University, Indianapolis, IN 46202, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Computer vision","Surgery","Oncology","Otorhinolaryngology","Head And Neck","Machine Learning","Augmented Reality","Deep Learning","Large Language Model"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm15072767"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073410"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13073410?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-06"},{"id":"42374342","source":"MED","pmid":"42374342","doi":"10.1186/s12912-026-04942-z","title":"Undergraduate nursing students' perceptions, needs, and expectations regarding large language model-based virtual patients: a qualitative study.","authorString":"Zeng J, Zhu X, Liu X, Li S, Shen S, Zhang Q, Cao S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Zeng J","firstName":"Jianwen","lastName":"Zeng","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhu X","firstName":"Xule","lastName":"Zhu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu X","firstName":"Xin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li S","firstName":"Sixie","lastName":"Li","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Shen S","firstName":"Shiying","lastName":"Shen","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Q","firstName":"Qiong","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Nursing Department, Zhejiang Provincial People's Hospital, Hangzhou, China. zqzjsrmyy@163.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Cao S","firstName":"Shihua","lastName":"Cao","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China. csh@hznu.edu.cn."},{"affiliation":"Key Engineering Research Center of Mobile Health Management System, Ministry of Education, Hangzhou, China. csh@hznu.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4204972,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"BMC nursing","medlineAbbreviation":"BMC Nurs","isoabbreviation":"BMC Nurs","nlmid":"101088683","essn":"1472-6955","issn":"1472-6955"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Communication competence is a core component of clinical competence among undergraduate nursing students. However, conventional communication teaching is often constrained by limited contextual authenticity, insufficient opportunities for repeated practice, and inadequate feedback support. Large language model-based virtual patients (LLM-VPs) offer a new approach to nursing communication training, but limited evidence exists on how undergraduate nursing students, based on their prior communication-learning experiences, anticipate the educational value, functional requirements, and boundaries of such systems.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to explore undergraduate nursing students' anticipated perceptions, functional needs, preliminary acceptance, and views on potential risks and boundaries regarding the use of LLM-VPs for communication training before they had used a dedicated LLM-VP system. The findings were intended to provide early evidence for the integration of this technology into nursing education.<h4>Methods</h4>This study employed a descriptive qualitative design. A total of 15 undergraduate nursing students were recruited through convenience sampling. Data were collected through semi-structured interviews after written informed consent had been obtained from each participant. Data collection and analysis were conducted concurrently until theoretical saturation was reached. The collected data were coded and analyzed using thematic analysis, with the assistance of NVivo 14 software.<h4>Results</h4>Four main themes were identified: emerging educational gaps and competence needs; expected system form and design requirements; mechanisms underlying trust and sustained use; and perceived risks and boundaries of applicability. Overall, nursing students expressed cautiously optimistic expectations toward LLM-VPs and articulated diverse functional expectations for aligning these systems with nursing-specific learning needs.<h4>Conclusions</h4>From the anticipatory perspective of undergraduate nursing students, LLM-VPs have practical appeal and potential utility in nursing communication education. However, their educational value remains conditional and requires further validation in real-use studies. Students expected LLM-VPs to function not as replacements for real clinical encounters but as supplementary training tools that support repeated practice, immediate feedback, and preclinical rehearsal. Future implementation should be grounded in nursing-specific adaptation, deep curricular integration, rigorous knowledge verification, ethical governance, and empirical evaluation of real-world educational effects to support standardized, cautious, and sustainable use in nursing education.","affiliation":"School of Public Health and Nursing, Hangzhou Normal University, Hangzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nursing Education","Qualitative Research","Large Language Models","Student Perceptives"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12912-026-04942-z"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-29"},{"id":"41696083","source":"MED","pmid":"41696083","pmcid":"PMC12901861","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12901861"]},"doi":"10.1177/20552076261421367","title":"Development and evaluation of an large language model-integrated chatbot intervention for physical activity habit formation in adults with prehypertension.","authorString":"Ma H, Cui H, Yu H, Pei R, Li S, Wang A, Tang X, Liu G, Piao M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ma H","firstName":"Haoming","lastName":"Ma","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0271-8409"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cui H","firstName":"Hongzhen","lastName":"Cui","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8890-2161"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Computer and Communication Engineering, University of Science and Technology Beijing, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yu H","firstName":"Hongyu","lastName":"Yu","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Pei R","firstName":"Runyuan","lastName":"Pei","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li S","firstName":"Sijia","lastName":"Li","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang A","firstName":"Aoqi","lastName":"Wang","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tang X","firstName":"Xingyi","lastName":"Tang","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu G","firstName":"Guangnan","lastName":"Liu","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Piao M","firstName":"Meihua","lastName":"Piao","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2436-6870"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8890-2161"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0271-8409"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2436-6870"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"12","journalIssueId":4085000,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jan-Dec","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"Digit Health","nlmid":"101690863","essn":"2055-2076","issn":"2055-2076"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"20552076261421367","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Individuals with prehypertension are at risk of developing hypertension, which affects many adults globally. Sustained physical activity (PA) can lower blood pressure, but maintaining long-term behavior change remains difficult. While PA habit formation interventions are promising, they face issues with scalability and accessibility. At the same time, behavior change chatbots have appeared, but their development often lacks systematic methods. Additionally, optimizing large language models (LLMs) to improve chatbot efficiency and reduce costs still needs more research.<h4>Objective</h4>This study introduces HabitBot, an LLM-integrated chatbot designed to foster PA habits in prehypertensive adults. HabitBot leverages LLMs for seamless interactions and integrates multidisciplinary insights, theoretical frameworks, and evidence to enhance the behavior change process.<h4>Methods</h4>HabitBot was developed through a systematic five-phase process: Phase 1, needs assessment via multidisciplinary discussions; Phase 2, literature review to identify relevant behavior change theories; Phase 3, selection of effective behavior change techniques (BCTs); Phase 4, intervention mapping for prototype design; and Phase 5, usability testing and focus group interviews for refinement.<h4>Results</h4>The process led to eight identified user needs and synthesized the Health Action Process Approach with Habit Formation Theory. Twelve effective BCTs were selected. The prototype was developed and refined across six dimensions based on user feedback. Evaluations indicated high usability, with a mean chatbot usability score of 3.84 (SD 0.82).<h4>Conclusion</h4>HabitBot integrates behavior change strategies with advanced LLM technology, representing a novel approach in chronic disease prevention. Future research should assess its long-term impact and generalizability.","affiliation":"School of Nursing, Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences & Peking Union Medical College, Beijing, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"2023-RC320-01","agency":"Non-Profit Central Research Institute Fund of Chinese Academy of Medical Sciences","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"2023zlgc0711","agency":"Peking Union Medical College 2023 Medical Education Scholar Program","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Physical Activity","Prehypertension","Mhealth","Health Behavior Change","Chatbot","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/20552076261421367"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12901861"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12901861?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-02-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-02-18","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},{"id":"41431473","source":"MED","pmid":"41431473","pmcid":"PMC12718344","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12718344"]},"doi":"10.1177/20420986251396023","title":"Orchestrating generative AI in pharmacovigilance: predicting and preempting the unpredictable.","authorString":"Ramcharran D, Painter JL, Kara V, Glaser M, Vanini M, Chalamalasetti VR, Golds C, Abdelkarim A, Bate A, Stegmann JU.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ramcharran D","firstName":"Darmendra","lastName":"Ramcharran","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-2787-5265"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, 1000 Winter Street, Waltham, MA 02451, USA University of Rhode Island, Kingston, RI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Painter JL","firstName":"Jeffery L","lastName":"Painter","initials":"JL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9651-9904"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, Durham, NC, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Kara V","firstName":"Vijay","lastName":"Kara","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Glaser M","firstName":"Michael","lastName":"Glaser","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, Upper Providence, PA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Vanini M","firstName":"Marco","lastName":"Vanini","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, Wavre, Belgium."}]}},{"fullName":"Chalamalasetti VR","firstName":"Venkateswara Rao","lastName":"Chalamalasetti","initials":"VR","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, Durham, NC, USA."},{"affiliation":"Tech Mahindra, Plano, TX, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Golds C","firstName":"Christopher","lastName":"Golds","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Abdelkarim A","firstName":"Azza","lastName":"Abdelkarim","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Bate A","firstName":"Andrew","lastName":"Bate","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, London, UK."},{"affiliation":"London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, London, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Stegmann JU","firstName":"Jens-Ulrich","lastName":"Stegmann","initials":"JU","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"GSK, Wavre, Belgium."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9651-9904"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-2787-5265"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":3880913,"dateOfPublication":"2025 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-01-01","journal":{"title":"Therapeutic advances in drug safety","medlineAbbreviation":"Ther Adv Drug Saf","isoabbreviation":"Ther Adv Drug Saf","nlmid":"101549074","essn":"2042-0994","issn":"2042-0986"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"20420986251396023","abstractText":"The advent of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) has introduced both remarkable opportunities and significant challenges in the field of pharmacovigilance (PV). This perspective review reﬂects on emerging trends, practical use cases, and conceptual frameworks shaping the integration of GenAI in high-risk domains such as drug and vaccine safety monitoring. We draw on current experiments and early real-world applications to examine the potential benefits, inherent risks, and propose a framework for integrating GenAI into PV systems, emphasizing the necessity of rigorous testing, human oversight, and ethical considerations. Our goal is to support PV professionals and stakeholders in navigating this rapidly evolving landscape by identifying promising strategies and implementation pathways.","affiliation":"GSK, 1000 Winter Street, Waltham, MA 02451, USA University of Rhode Island, Kingston, RI, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"GSK","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Drug safety","Pharmacovigilance","Generative Ai"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/20420986251396023"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12718344"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12718344?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":4,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2025-12-23","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-23","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-24","dateOfRevision":"2025-12-25","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-20","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-20"},{"id":"PPR1237482","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.05.20.26353755","title":"Machine-Assisted Topic Analysis of Large-Scale Health Experience Data: Identifying Sociodemographic Differences and Evaluating Bias in Large Language Models","authorString":"Bondaronek P, Ward E, Beecham E, Zhang E, Huang Y, Ive J, Naughton F, Wu H, Vindrola-Padros C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bondaronek P","firstName":"Paulina","lastName":"Bondaronek","initials":"P"},{"fullName":"Ward E","firstName":"Emma","lastName":"Ward","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Beecham E","firstName":"Emma","lastName":"Beecham","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1832-0286"}},{"fullName":"Zhang E","firstName":"Eric","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"E"},{"fullName":"Huang Y","firstName":"Yuqing","lastName":"Huang","initials":"Y"},{"fullName":"Ive J","firstName":"Julia","lastName":"Ive","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Naughton F","firstName":"Felix","lastName":"Naughton","initials":"F"},{"fullName":"Wu H","firstName":"Honghan","lastName":"Wu","initials":"H"},{"fullName":"Vindrola-Padros C","firstName":"Cecilia","lastName":"Vindrola-Padros","initials":"C"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1832-0286"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>  Large-scale free-text data with socio-demographic information can capture nuanced accounts of lived experience that are difficult to detect in structured measures. However, manual qualitative analysis is difficult to scale, while automated approaches may obscure subgroup variation or introduce bias. This is especially relevant for large language models (LLMs), whose use in qualitative health research is increasing despite limited evaluation in socio-demographically stratified analysis. <h4>Objectives</h4>  This study examined how socio-demographic differences in health and wellbeing experiences were manifested in a large-scale free-text dataset, and evaluated how different AI-assisted analytic approaches identified these differences. Specifically, it aimed to: (1) identify socio-demographic differences using Machine-Assisted Topic Analysis (MATA); (2) compare MATA outputs with topic modelling combined with LLM-based topic interpretation; and (3) examine potential bias in LLM-based analysis. <h4>Methods</h4>  We analysed 2,177 valid free-text responses from the UK COVID-19 Well-being Tracker, a longitudinal survey of adults recruited during the pandemic. Responses described factors influencing health behaviours, mood, and wellbeing over time. Data were preprocessed and stratified by gender, socioeconomic status (SES), age. MATA combined topic modelling, using Latent Dirichlet Allocation, with human-led qualitative interpretation of topic keywords and representative responses. The same topic model outputs were then interpreted using an LLM for comparison. Potential LLM bias was assessed using a demographic label-swap crossover design, with bias evaluated through Jaccard lexical similarity, VADER sentiment, and NRC emotion analysis. Grounded Review and Assessment of Computational Evidence (GRACE) was used to evaluate the AI outputs. <h4>Results</h4>  MATA identified meaningful socio-demographic thematic differences in pandemic-related mood and wellbeing across gender, SES, and age. Common themes included disruption, adaptation, uncertainty, routine, and the influence of work, relationships, and health on wellbeing. Male-stratified topics emphasised routines, habits, and coping with external pressures, whereas female-stratified topics were more relational and reflective, focusing on connection, isolation, family wellbeing, and anxiety. Lower SES narratives included practical strain, financial pressure, and loss of control, while higher SES narratives more often reflected adjustment, autonomy, and meaning-making. Older adults described health, gratitude, and family connection, whereas younger adults emphasised work-related stress and competing demands. LLM-based interpretation broadly reproduced the high-level subgroup patterns identified through MATA, but outputs were more generalised, less conceptually differentiated, and showed greater thematic overlap. Bias analysis showed systematic shifts in vocabulary, sentiment, and emotional tone when demographic labels were swapped, suggesting a risk of representational bias. <h4>Conclusions</h4>  MATA identified meaningful socio-demographic differences while retaining interpretative depth at scale. LLM-based topic interpretation showed utility for rapid thematic summarisation, but produced less conceptually differentiated outputs and was sensitive to demographic framing. The analysis also identified “LLM speak”, where outputs appeared coherent but relied on abstract, generalised, and overlapping interpretations. Human oversight, structured qualitative appraisal, and explicit bias evaluation are necessary when using LLMs to analyse socially stratified free-text health data. <h4>Author summary</h4>  This study explores how Artificial Intelligence can help researchers analyse very large collections of free-text health experiences without losing important human meaning. We analysed responses from more than 2,000 adults in the United Kingdom who described how the COVID-19 pandemic affected their wellbeing, routines, relationships, and daily lives over time. We applied a method called Machine-Assisted Topic Analysis, which combines computational topic modelling with human qualitative interpretation, to identify differences across gender, age, and socioeconomic groups. We then compared these findings with analyses generated using a large language model. Both approaches identified broad patterns in the data, including disruption, uncertainty, adaptation, and the importance of work, family, and health. However, the large language model produced more generalised and overlapping interpretations, whereas the human-led approach retained greater nuance and clearer distinctions between social groups. We also found that changing demographic labels, such as “men” or “women”, systematically altered the language and emotional tone generated by the model, suggesting a risk of representational bias. Our findings suggest that Artificial Intelligence may be useful for rapid summarisation of large-scale health experience data, but that human oversight remains necessary to preserve nuance and support fair interpretation of socially diverse experiences. As large language models become more widely used in health research and service evaluation, careful evaluation of potential bias will be important to avoid reproducing overly simplified or stereotyped accounts of different social groups.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"medRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.05.20.26353755"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-22"},{"id":"42305950","source":"MED","pmid":"42305950","pmcid":"PMC13267935","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13267935"]},"doi":"10.1177/2993091x261446348","title":"Retrieval-Augmented Generation in Oncology: Promises, Pitfalls, and Early Applications.","authorString":"Thaker NG, Liu W, Waddle M, Showalter T, Mastroleo F, Luh J, Levitt C, Ning M, Loaiza-Bonilla A, Hong J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Thaker NG","firstName":"Nikhil G","lastName":"Thaker","initials":"NG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Bayta Systems, Newtown, Pennsylvania, USA."},{"affiliation":"Capital Health, Newtown, Pennsylvania, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu W","firstName":"Wei","lastName":"Liu","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, Arizona, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Waddle M","firstName":"Mark","lastName":"Waddle","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Mayo Clinic, Rochester, Minnesota, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Showalter T","firstName":"Timothy","lastName":"Showalter","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Virginia, Charlottesville, Virginia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Mastroleo F","firstName":"Federico","lastName":"Mastroleo","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiation Oncology, Mayo Clinic, Phoenix, Arizona, USA."},{"affiliation":"Division of Radiation Oncology, IEO, European Institute of Oncology, IRCCS, Milan, Italy."},{"affiliation":"Department of Oncology and Hemato-Oncology, University of Milan, Milan, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Luh J","firstName":"Join","lastName":"Luh","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Providence St Joseph Health System, Eureka, California, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Levitt C","firstName":"Chad","lastName":"Levitt","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"ONCareMD, Atlanta, Georgia, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ning M","firstName":"Matthew","lastName":"Ning","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center, Houston, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Loaiza-Bonilla A","firstName":"Arturo","lastName":"Loaiza-Bonilla","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Hematology and Oncology, St Luke's University Health NetworK, Allentown, Pennsylvania, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Hong J","firstName":"Julian","lastName":"Hong","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of California San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"2-3","volume":"3","journalIssueId":4218394,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr-Jun","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"AI in precision oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"AI Precis Oncol","isoabbreviation":"AI Precis Oncol","nlmid":"9918994792206676","essn":"2993-0928","issn":"2993-0928"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"34-45","abstractText":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is rapidly emerging as a transformative paradigm for large language models (LLMs), especially in high-stakes domains like oncology that demand precision, factual grounding, and up-to-date knowledge. By pairing LLMs with external knowledge repositories, RAG systems explicitly ground model outputs in relevant retrieved documents, helping to reduce hallucinations and ensure responses reflect current evidence. In oncology, where clinical knowledge evolves continually with new research and drug approvals, RAG offers a way to integrate the latest data (e.g., trial results, guidelines, genomic databases) into decision-making. This review synthesizes the technical foundations of RAG, including its architecture and key components, and examines current applications in oncology such as clinical decision support, patient education, radiology reporting, pathology analysis, and genomics-driven precision medicine. We highlight recent studies that demonstrate RAG's potential-for instance, improving treatment recommendations by incorporating genetic profiles and literature, and enhancing diagnostic accuracy by integrating guidelines. We also discuss emerging developments like multimodal RAG (combining text with imaging or other data), ensemble model approaches, and new explainability tools that trace model outputs to sources. Finally, we critically analyze the limitations and challenges of deploying RAG in healthcare, including computational costs, retrieval errors, noise or conflicts in retrieved information, and ethical and regulatory considerations. While RAG-based systems show promise in augmenting oncologists' expertise with timely knowledge, careful implementation, high-quality curation of knowledge bases, and human oversight will be crucial for safe and effective adoption in clinical practice.","affiliation":"Bayta Systems, Newtown, Pennsylvania, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"R01CA280134","agency":"NIH/NIBIB","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01EB293388","agency":"NIH/NIBIB","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 CA280134","agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"R01 EB293388","agency":"NIBIB NIH HHS","acronym":"EB","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Treatment Planning","Decision Support","Ethical And Regulatory Issues In Ai","Machine Learning In Research"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2993091x261446348"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13267935"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13267935?pdf=render"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/2993091x261446348"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"Y","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"Y","manuscriptId":"NIHMS2183762","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-06-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-28"},{"id":"42191815","source":"MED","pmid":"42191815","doi":"10.1038/s41746-026-02788-y","title":"Feasibility and impact of a large language model pipeline for surgical trial abstracts.","authorString":"Abiola OJ, Nepogodiev D, Glasbey J, Omar O, Linder C, Ledda V, Bhangu A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Abiola OJ","firstName":"Obafunsho John","lastName":"Abiola","initials":"OJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Nepogodiev D","firstName":"Dmitri","lastName":"Nepogodiev","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2171-2862"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Glasbey J","firstName":"James","lastName":"Glasbey","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Omar O","firstName":"Omar","lastName":"Omar","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Linder C","firstName":"Cortland","lastName":"Linder","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Ledda V","firstName":"Virginia","lastName":"Ledda","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Bhangu A","firstName":"Aneel","lastName":"Bhangu","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5999-4618"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK. a.a.bhangu@bham.ac.uk."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5999-4618"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2171-2862"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4187087,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"NPJ digital medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","isoabbreviation":"NPJ Digit Med","nlmid":"101731738","essn":"2398-6352","issn":"2398-6352"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Abstracts of surgical randomised controlled trials (RCTs) are widely read but often omit key methodological details despite CONSORT guidance. We evaluated whether a tightly constrained large language model (LLM) pipeline could improve completeness and readability at scale. We conducted a three-phase in silico study of consecutive surgical RCTs indexed in PubMed (2005-2025) with open-access full texts in PubMed Central. A 14-item CONSORT-derived rubric (maximum 25 points) was developed and validated against expert scoring, demonstrating good agreement (concordance correlation coefficient 0.71, 95% CI 0.44-0.86) and high reproducibility (intraclass correlation coefficient 0.91, 95% CI 0.80-0.96). An automated pipeline using GPT-4o (OpenAI) generated rewritten abstracts from full texts under strict non-fabrication constraints. Among 651 RCTs, original abstracts showed low completeness (mean 9.06/25, 95% CI 8.58-9.53). Rewriting significantly improved completeness (mean increase 7.40 for 250-word and 8.06 for 300-word versions; both p < 0.0001), with gains across all CONSORT domains, particularly randomisation, harms, and trial registration. Readability improved slightly and correlated with completeness. A constrained LLM pipeline can substantially enhance the completeness of surgical RCT abstracts at scale, with potential applications in authoring, peer review, and editorial workflows, provided appropriate human oversight.","affiliation":"Surgical Data Institute, University of Birmingham, Birmingham, UK.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-026-02788-y"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-05-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-26"},{"id":"42099103","source":"MED","pmid":"42099103","pmcid":"PMC13320304","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13320304"]},"doi":"10.4274/dir.2026.264103","title":"Reply: Evaluating the reference accuracy of large language models in radiology: a comparative study across subspecialties.","authorString":"Güneş YC, Cesur T, Çamur E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Güneş YC","firstName":"Yasin Celal","lastName":"Güneş","initials":"YC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7631-854X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Kırıkkale Yüksek İhtisas Hospital, Clinic of Radiology, Kırıkkale, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Cesur T","firstName":"Turay","lastName":"Cesur","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2726-8045"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Mamak State Hospital, Clinic of Radiology, Ankara, Türkiye."}]}},{"fullName":"Çamur E","firstName":"Eren","lastName":"Çamur","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8774-5800"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ankara 29 Mayıs State Hospital, Clinic of Radiology, Ankara, Türkiye."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7631-854X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2726-8045"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8774-5800"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"32","journalIssueId":4223044,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Diagnostic and interventional radiology (Ankara, Turkey)","medlineAbbreviation":"Diagn Interv Radiol","isoabbreviation":"Diagn Interv Radiol","nlmid":"101241152","essn":"1305-3612","issn":"1305-3825"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"397-398","affiliation":"Kırıkkale Yüksek İhtisas Hospital, Clinic of Radiology, Kırıkkale, Türkiye.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["letter","Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.4274/dir.2026.264103"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13320304"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13320304?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-05-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-08-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-08"},{"id":"41576176","source":"MED","pmid":"41576176","pmcid":"PMC12829926","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12829926"]},"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0341631","title":"User experience and safety of generative AI-based mental health chatbots: Scoping review protocol.","authorString":"Olisaeloka L, Richardson C, Vigo D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Olisaeloka L","firstName":"Lotenna","lastName":"Olisaeloka","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Richardson C","firstName":"Chris","lastName":"Richardson","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Vigo D","firstName":"Daniel","lastName":"Vigo","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada."},{"affiliation":"School of Population and Public Health, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9131-0036"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4082298,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"PloS one","medlineAbbreviation":"PLoS One","isoabbreviation":"PLoS One","nlmid":"101285081","essn":"1932-6203","issn":"1932-6203"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0341631","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Mental health problems constitute a significant global health challenge due to their rising prevalence and substantial treatment gap. Digital Mental Health Interventions (DMHIs) including mental health chatbots have emerged as promising solutions due to their effectiveness and scalability. Recent advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) have improved the conversational abilities of these chatbots, further amplifying their potential. However, despite instances of inadvertent harm stemming from the unpredictable nature of GenAI, little attention has been paid to user experience and safety of these chatbots.<h4>Objective</h4>This proposed review will explore existing research on GenAI-based mental health chatbots. Specifically, it aims to identify and describe current chatbots, focusing on user experience, safety and risk mitigation strategies.<h4>Methods</h4>The review will follow the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) guidelines for conducting scoping reviews. It will also adhere to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses Extension for Scoping Review (PRISMA-ScR). A systematic database search of Medline (PubMed), Scopus, PsycINFO, ACM Digital Library, and IEEE Xplore will be conducted. The database search will be complimented by research-based search engines (Google Scholar and Consensus). Studies focusing on the development, evaluation or implementation of GenAI-based mental health chatbots will be included without limitations to specific disorders or population groups. Two independent reviewers will perform screening and data extraction. The analysis will include descriptive summary and thematic analysis, with results presented in tabular, graphical, and narrative formats.<h4>Conclusion</h4>This review will provide a comprehensive overview of GenAI-based mental health chatbots while identifying innovative practices and knowledge gaps relating to user experience and safety. Findings will inform the ethical development, evaluation and implementation of GenAI-based mental health interventions.","affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mental Disorders","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Scoping Reviews As Topic"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0341631"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12829926"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12829926?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-01-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-01-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-01-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-01-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-01-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-01-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-01-23"},{"id":"42011474","source":"MED","pmid":"42011474","pmcid":"PMC13092168","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13092168"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.105551","title":"Comparative Evaluation of Gemini 3.0- and ChatGPT 5.0-Generated Regional Language Informed Consent Forms in Ophthalmology: A Dual-Rater Study in Hindi and Kannada.","authorString":"Das D, Shetti B, Antalmarad V, Choudhary A, Thodupunoori KG, Grover S, Narayan A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Das D","firstName":"Deepsekhar","lastName":"Das","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Shetti B","firstName":"Bahubali","lastName":"Shetti","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Antalmarad V","firstName":"Venkatesh","lastName":"Antalmarad","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Choudhary A","firstName":"Anshum","lastName":"Choudhary","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Thodupunoori KG","firstName":"Kathyayini G","lastName":"Thodupunoori","initials":"KG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Grover S","firstName":"Sumit","lastName":"Grover","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Narayan A","firstName":"Atindra","lastName":"Narayan","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4139964,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e105551","abstractText":"<h4>Purpose</h4>To evaluate the accuracy, linguistic quality, clinical completeness, and real-world applicability of informed consent forms generated in Indian regional languages (Hindi and Kannada) by two large language model-based chatbots for common ophthalmic surgical procedures.<h4>Methods</h4>In this comparative, blinded observational study, two chatbots (Gemini 3.0 (Google, California, USA) and ChatGPT 5.0 (OpenAI, California, USA)) were prompted to generate informed consent documents in Hindi and Kannada for five ophthalmic scenarios: cataract surgery, traumatic corneal perforation repair, therapeutic penetrating keratoplasty, orbitotomy, and squint surgery. Outputs were independently assessed by four ophthalmologist raters (two for each language) using a 10-point scoring system based on correctness, completeness, language and readability, clinical relevance, and real-world applicability. Descriptive statistics were calculated. Paired t-tests were used to compare chatbot performance, effect sizes (Cohen's d) were estimated, and inter-rater reliability was assessed using intraclass correlation coefficients (ICCs).<h4>Results</h4>Across both languages, Gemini 3.0 demonstrated more consistent performance and higher combined mean scores. In the Hindi cohort, combined mean scores were comparable between Gemini 3.0 (7.85) and ChatGPT 5.0 (8.00), with significant rater-dependent preference variability. In contrast, in the Kannada cohort, Gemini 3.0 significantly outperformed ChatGPT 5.0 (8.8 vs 7.7, p<0.01), with large to extremely large effect sizes (Cohen's d: 1.23-3.8). Inter-rater reliability was moderate to good for Gemini 3.0 (ICC: 0.62-0.71) and lower for ChatGPT 5.0 (ICC: 0.38-0.59). ChatGPT 5.0 exhibited frequent grammatical and terminological inaccuracies, particularly in Kannada, affecting clinical usability.<h4>Conclusion</h4>Large language models can generate clinically usable informed consent forms in Indian regional languages; however, performance varies significantly between models. Gemini 3.0 demonstrated superior linguistic accuracy, consistency, and clinical suitability. Language-specific validation and mandatory human oversight are essential before clinical implementation.","affiliation":"Ophthalmology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, IND.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Informed consent","Ophthalmology","Large Language Model","Ai Generated Consent","Chatgpt 5.0","Gemini 3.0"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.105551"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13092168"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13092168?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-21","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-20"},{"id":"41963402","source":"MED","pmid":"41963402","pmcid":"PMC13230711","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13230711"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-46229-y","title":"A hybrid large language model framework for structured data entry from code-switched persian clinical speech.","authorString":"Tanhaei M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tanhaei M","firstName":"Mohammad","lastName":"Tanhaei","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Engineering, Ilam University, Ilam, Iran. mohamad.tanhaei@gmail.com."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4168316,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Scientific reports","medlineAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","isoabbreviation":"Sci Rep","nlmid":"101563288","essn":"2045-2322","issn":"2045-2322"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"16958","abstractText":"Converting spoken clinical dictation into structured electronic health record (EHR) entries poses significant challenges in low-resource languages. These difficulties are primarily driven by phonetic complexity and the prevalent phenomenon of code-switching with English medical terminology, both of which severely degrade the performance of automatic speech recognition (ASR) models. This paper presents a practical hybrid framework that combines ASR, Persian-specific normalization of code-switched and orthographically ambiguous terms, metadata-constrained field mapping, and uncertainty-triggered human review for structured EHR entry. Leveraging large language model (LLM) prompting and specialized bilingual glossaries, this module normalizes code-switched terms and disambiguates Persian-specific homophones. The proposed method employs embedding-based field mapping guided by form metadata and existing clinical annotations, supplemented by selective human-in-the-loop validation for high-stakes fields. Evaluation within the BioArc EHR system demonstrates substantial improvements in transcription quality and structured data accuracy. Experimental results and granular ablation studies confirm that explicitly addressing linguistic challenges such as code-switching and homophone variability is critical for improving field detection rates and supporting data integrity. Our findings underscore that while the framework serves as a robust assistive tool–achieving high field detection accuracy in controlled evaluations–a hybrid workflow remains essential to address residual error rates (approximately 5–10% in complex forms) and support the precision required for medical documentation.","affiliation":"Department of Engineering, Ilam University, Ilam, Iran. mohamad.tanhaei@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Language"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Speech Recognition Software"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Electronic Health Records"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Electronic Health Records","Data Extraction","Code-switching","Llm","Low-Resource Languages","Automated Data Entry","Persian Clinical Speech","Voice-to-form"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-46229-y"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13230711"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13230711?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-27","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-04","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-10"},{"id":"41980262","source":"MED","pmid":"41980262","pmcid":"PMC13094381","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13094381"]},"doi":"10.2196/90644","title":"User Experience and Early Clinical Outcomes of a Mental Wellness Chatbot for Depression and Anxiety: Pilot Evaluation Mixed Methods Study.","authorString":"Graupensperger S, Ward EJ, Baum G, Bentley KH, Dworkin ER, Brown M, Chekroud A, Hawrilenko M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Graupensperger S","firstName":"Scott","lastName":"Graupensperger","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8655-1190"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}},{"fullName":"Ward EJ","firstName":"Emily J","lastName":"Ward","initials":"EJ","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2789-2753"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}},{"fullName":"Baum G","firstName":"Graham","lastName":"Baum","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-5123-5535"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}},{"fullName":"Bentley KH","firstName":"Kate H","lastName":"Bentley","initials":"KH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6875-6440"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}},{"fullName":"Dworkin ER","firstName":"Emily R","lastName":"Dworkin","initials":"ER","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3704-5339"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}},{"fullName":"Brown M","firstName":"Millard","lastName":"Brown","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2225-9243"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}},{"fullName":"Chekroud A","firstName":"Adam","lastName":"Chekroud","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0497-596X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."},{"affiliation":"Psychiatry, School of Medicine, Yale University, New Haven, CT, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Hawrilenko M","firstName":"Matt","lastName":"Hawrilenko","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9556-4391"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9556-4391"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0497-596X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2789-2753"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3704-5339"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6875-6440"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8655-1190"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2225-9243"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-5123-5535"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4166928,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e90644","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Artificial intelligence-powered conversational agents (ie, chatbots) are increasingly popular outlets for users seeking psychological support, yet little is known about how users experience early-stage prototypes or which therapeutic processes contribute to clinical improvement. A transparent evaluation of emerging chatbot prototypes is needed to clarify if, how, and why artificial intelligence companions work and to guide their continued development.<h4>Objective</h4>This mixed methods pilot study evaluated user experience, acceptability, and preliminary clinical signals for an early-stage mental wellness chatbot. We also examined whether baseline symptom severity moderated clinical improvement.<h4>Methods</h4>Three sequential cohorts (n=125) completed a 2-week, incentivized chatbot exposure (approximately 60 min per week). Participants provided first-impression ratings, qualitative feedback, and pre-post assessments of depressive symptoms (PHQ-8 [Patient Health Questionnaire-8]), anxiety symptoms (GAD-7 [Generalized Anxiety Disorder-7]), psychological distress, well-being, and loneliness. Statistical models estimated symptom change and tested interactions with baseline symptom severity. Mixed methods analysis integrated quantitative outcomes with large language model-assisted qualitative content analysis of open-ended responses.<h4>Results</h4>Participants described the chatbot as accessible, easy to use, and emotionally validating, while citing limitations in personalization and conversational depth. Qualitative responses consistently highlighted early therapeutic processes such as emotional validation, goal setting, and perceived attunement. Regression models showed significant pre-post reductions in depressive (Hedges g=-0.32) and anxiety (g=-0.32) symptoms, alongside modest improvements in distress and well-being. Baseline severity moderated improvement, with marginal effects indicating larger predicted reductions at higher PHQ-8 and GAD-7 baseline scores (eg, PHQ-8=15: g=-0.84; GAD-7=15: g=-0.62).<h4>Conclusions</h4>This pilot provides a comprehensive view of early chatbot development and suggests promising user experiences and preliminary symptom improvements under structured pilot conditions. By integrating experiential and exploratory clinical data, the study identifies candidate process targets to inform ongoing refinement. Findings support continued development and demonstrate procedural feasibility for progression to larger, longer-term trials evaluating engagement and clinical outcomes under more naturalistic conditions.","affiliation":"Spring Health, 60 Madison Ave, New York, NY, 10010, United States, 1 855-629-0554.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Treatment Outcome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pilot Projects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Depression","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Anxiety","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Surveys and Questionnaires"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Psychological Well-Being"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Therapeutic Alliance","Digital Health","Mental Health.","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/90644"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13094381"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13094381?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-14","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-14"},{"id":"PPR1248729","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9529609/v1","title":"A Reproducible Framework for Safe LLM-Based Simplification of ICU Discharge Summaries in Clinical Information Systems","authorString":"Hassan M, Hassan T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hassan M","firstName":"Mahmudul","lastName":"Hassan","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Hassan T","firstName":"Tahomina","lastName":"Hassan","initials":"T"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Background  Clinical discharge summaries are increasingly accessible to patients through portals and other health information systems, yet they are often difficult for patients to understand. Large language models (LLMs) may improve readability, but patient-facing use requires safeguards against omission, hallucination, and loss of clinically important detail. Objective  To assess whether prompt-based large language model (LLM) simplification can improve the readability of intensive care unit (ICU) discharge summaries while preserving medication, diagnosis, and follow-up information. Methods  Using discharge summaries from the Medical Information Mart for Intensive Care III (MIMIC-III) database, we developed a deterministic pipeline to extract six sections intended for patient use and assembled a 500-note test set. We compared zero-shot, few-shot, and chain-of-thought prompting across GPT-4o and GPT-4o-mini without model fine-tuning. Evaluation included readability metrics, semantic similarity, lexical overlap, medication and diagnosis omission/recall, and blinded human review of 60 notes. Results  All six prompting conditions significantly improved readability versus the original notes (all p < 0.0001), increasing Flesch Reading Ease from 51.2 ± 6.1 to 58.3–60.3 and reducing grade level from 9.4 ± 1.2 to 7.6–8.0. GPT-4o few-shot provided the strongest automated overall balance of simplification and preservation (similarity 0.844 ± 0.071, lexical overlap 0.638 ± 0.072, medication omission 2.2%, diagnosis omission 0.4%). In blinded human evaluation, GPT-4o with chain-of-thought prompting achieved perfect faithfulness and 0% hallucination, while GPT-4o few-shot showed the best combined faithfulness, readability, and actionability. Conclusions  Prompt-based simplification can improve the accessibility of discharge summaries without model training, but patient-facing use requires section-aware constraints, explicit safety checks, and human oversight. The proposed framework offers a practical evaluation approach for LLM-assisted communication workflows in clinical information systems.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9529609/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1248729","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-09","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-09"},{"id":"42160120","source":"MED","pmid":"42160120","doi":"10.2214/ajr.26.34999","title":"Human-in-the-Loop Large Language Model-Augmented Diagnostic Reasoning in Thoracic Imaging: Impact of Radiologic Expertise.","authorString":"Song J, Ko H, Han DH, Hwang EJ, Cho HS, Lee JY, Jeong WG, Yoon SH, Kim H, Kim J, Park J, Park J, Son Y, Lee JE, Lee T.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Song J","firstName":"Jiyoung","lastName":"Song","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Ko H","firstName":"Hongseok","lastName":"Ko","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Han DH","firstName":"Dae Hee","lastName":"Han","initials":"DH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul St. Mary's Hospital, College of Medicine, The Catholic University of Korea, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Hwang EJ","firstName":"Eui Jin","lastName":"Hwang","initials":"EJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Cho HS","firstName":"Hye Soo","lastName":"Cho","initials":"HS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Uijeongbu Eulji University Hospital and Daejeon Eulji University, Daejeon, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee JY","firstName":"Ji Young","lastName":"Lee","initials":"JY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Jeong WG","firstName":"Won Gi","lastName":"Jeong","initials":"WG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, National Cancer Center, Goyang, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Yoon SH","firstName":"Soon Ho","lastName":"Yoon","initials":"SH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, National Jewish Health, Denver, Colorado, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim H","firstName":"Hyungjin","lastName":"Kim","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Kim J","firstName":"Jiho","lastName":"Kim","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Park J","firstName":"Jongwoo","lastName":"Park","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Park J","firstName":"Jiyoon","lastName":"Park","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Son Y","firstName":"Youbin","lastName":"Son","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee JE","firstName":"Joo Eon","lastName":"Lee","initials":"JE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee T","firstName":"Taehee","lastName":"Lee","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0592-8268"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4190584,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"AJR. American journal of roentgenology","medlineAbbreviation":"AJR Am J Roentgenol","isoabbreviation":"AJR Am J Roentgenol","nlmid":"7708173","essn":"1546-3141","issn":"0361-803X"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<b>Background:</b> Technical and regulatory constraints limit application of large language models (LLMs) for augmenting diagnostic reasoning in radiology. Reader-mediated text-based workflows may provide a practical alternative. <b>Objective:</b> To evaluate the impact on diagnostic performance of LLM assistance using reader-generated free-text image descriptions and to assess the effect of reader expertise on this LLM-augmented diagnostic workflow. <b>Methods:</b> This retrospective study included 93 cases (encompassing radiographic, CT, MRI, and PET/CT images) from the Korean Society of Thoracic Radiology quiz platform from January 2014 to December 2017. Five differential diagnoses (correct diagnosis and four distractors) were assembled for each case. Ten readers (five thoracic radiologists, five radiology residents) independently interpreted cases. In session 1, readers selected the most likely diagnosis and provided a free-text description of key findings. An LLM (Gemini 3.0 Pro) was inputted the free-text description-without case images-and outputted a ranking of the five differential diagnoses along with explanatory rationales for the top-three options. In session 2, readers were provided the LLM output from their own free-text description and re-selected a most likely diagnosis. LLM performance using images-without free-text descriptions-was also assessed. LLM accuracy was determined using top-ranked diagnoses. Reader groups were compared using generalized estimating equations. <b>Results:</b> LLM accuracy was 52.7% when inputted images and 63.9% when inputted reader-generated descriptions. LLM accuracy was greater using descriptions generated by thoracic radiologists than by residents (67.3% vs. 60.4%; P<.001). From session 1 to session 2, accuracy increased for thoracic radiologists from 56.3% to 65.6% and for residents from 42.4% to 58.5%, respectively. Accuracy improvement between sessions was greater for residents than thoracic radiologists (16.1 vs 9.2 percentage points; P=.02). Residents, compared with thoracic radiologists, demonstrated a greater rate of accepting LLM-favored diagnoses (73.2% vs 48.9%; P<.001), including a greater rate of switching to an incorrect diagnosis following misleading LLM output (60.6% vs 32.4%; P=.009). <b>Conclusions:</b> The text-based LLM-assisted workflow yielded improved reader accuracy although was heavily influenced by reader expertise. <b>Clinical Impact:</b> The utility of human-in-the-loop workflows arises from dynamic reader-LLM interactions shaped by the expertise of the operator formulating model inputs and critically evaluating model outputs.","affiliation":"Department of Radiology, Seoul National University Hospital, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul, Korea.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2214/AJR.26.34999"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"id":"42308097","source":"MED","reference":"AJR Am J Roentgenol. 2026 Jun 17. doi: 10.2214/AJR.26.35340.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":1},{"id":"42308098","source":"MED","reference":"AJR Am J Roentgenol. 2026 Jun 17. doi: 10.2214/AJR.26.35333.","type":"Comment in","orderIn":2}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-06-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-20"},{"id":"42333365","source":"MED","pmid":"42333365","pmcid":"PMC13283513","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13283513"]},"doi":"10.7759/cureus.109393","title":"Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Hospital Quality Management, Patient Safety, and Accreditation Readiness: A Systematic Review and Narrative Synthesis.","authorString":"Marathe N, Ranjan M, Singh MP, Trigunait P, Prakash A, Kaushal N, Pawar A, Kshatriya GS, Dubey R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Marathe N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Marathe","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranjan M","firstName":"Manish","lastName":"Ranjan","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Microbiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Jammu, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh MP","firstName":"Madhusudan P","lastName":"Singh","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Pharmacology and Therapeutics, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Trigunait P","firstName":"Pragati","lastName":"Trigunait","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Obstetrics and Gynaecology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Prakash A","firstName":"Avinash","lastName":"Prakash","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Anaesthesiology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kaushal N","firstName":"Nitin","lastName":"Kaushal","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Community Medicine and Public Health, Postgraduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Pawar A","firstName":"Amit","lastName":"Pawar","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, Military Hospital Doda, Jammu, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Kshatriya GS","firstName":"Girish Singh","lastName":"Kshatriya","initials":"GS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Deoghar, Deoghar, IND."}]}},{"fullName":"Dubey R","firstName":"Rounak","lastName":"Dubey","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Transfusion Medicine, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-0295-3262"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4188949,"dateOfPublication":"2026 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-05-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e109393","abstractText":"As healthcare systems become increasingly data-driven, artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are being explored as tools to support quality management, patient safety, and accreditation. This systematic review synthesizes the available evidence across these domains through a structured literature search of major biomedical databases, encompassing studies published over the past decade and narratively synthesized to capture implementation context alongside technical performance. Three electronic databases were searched, and 23 studies were included. The available empirical evidence suggests that these technologies are most useful for repetitive, operationally well-defined, and digitally captured tasks, while local validation, acceptance testing, and continuous monitoring consistently emerge as prerequisites for safe institutional deployment. Governance capacity and organizational readiness are identified as equally determinative of outcomes as algorithmic performance itself. Collectively, the evidence supports positioning AI and ML as augmentative instruments within human-led quality governance structures, rather than as autonomous operational systems. Evidence was heterogeneous, with most empirical studies being single-center, retrospective, or implementation-focused. The risk-of-bias assessment indicated limited high-quality comparative evidence, limiting the strength of the pooled conclusions.","affiliation":"Hospital Administration, All India Institute of Medical Sciences, Nagpur, Nagpur, IND.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Quality assurance","Accreditation","Patient Safety","Machine Learning","Natural Language Processing","Deep Learning","Healthcare Governance","Hospital Quality Management"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.109393"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13283513?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-24","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-05-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-05-21"},{"id":"42348542","source":"MED","pmid":"42348542","pmcid":"PMC13298971","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13298971"]},"doi":"10.1371/journal.pdig.0001446","title":"Feasibility and user evaluation of HopeBot: An LLM-powered conversational chatbot for depression screening.","authorString":"Guo Z, Lai A, Ive J, Petcu A, Wang Y, Qi L, Thygesen JH, Li K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Guo Z","firstName":"Zhijun","lastName":"Guo","initials":"Z","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1901-5191"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Lai A","firstName":"Alvina","lastName":"Lai","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8960-8095"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Ive J","firstName":"Julia","lastName":"Ive","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Petcu A","firstName":"Alexandru","lastName":"Petcu","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Lancashire & South Cumbria NHS Foundation Trust, Bamber Bridge, ‌United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Y","firstName":"Yutong","lastName":"Wang","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Qi L","firstName":"Luyuan","lastName":"Qi","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Thygesen JH","firstName":"Johan H","lastName":"Thygesen","initials":"JH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Li K","firstName":"Kezhi","lastName":"Li","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3073-3128"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom."},{"affiliation":"University College London Hospital NHS Foundation Trust, London, United Kingdom."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8960-8095"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3073-3128"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-1901-5191"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"5","journalIssueId":4204541,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"PLOS digital health","medlineAbbreviation":"PLOS Digit Health","isoabbreviation":"PLOS Digit Health","nlmid":"9918335064206676","essn":"2767-3170","issn":"2767-3170"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e0001446","abstractText":"Clinician-administered Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9) interviews allow clarification of ambiguous responses but are resource-intensive and difficult to scale for routine use. Self-administered versions are widely adopted for depression screening yet offer little opportunity for interaction or clarification, which may limit engagement and scoring accuracy. We developed HopeBot, a conversational chatbot powered by a large language model (LLM) that delivers the PHQ-9 via text or voice, providing real-time clarification and safety guidance through a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) layer drawing on validated psychological and helpline resources. The system aims to extend access to structured screening rather than replace clinician judgment. In a within-subject feasibility study, 132 adults from two countries completed both self-administered and chatbot-assisted PHQ-9 assessments followed by a 25-item evaluation survey. Chatbot and self-report scores showed high concordance (intraclass correlation coefficient = 0.92; median absolute difference = 1 point), indicating faithful replication of scoring. Of these participants, 75 completed a comparative feedback module (most with identical scores were not prompted for comparison); 71% (n = 53) reported greater confidence in chatbot-assisted scores, citing clearer structure, interpretive guidance, and a supportive tone. Mean usability ratings (0-10) were 8.4 for comfort, 7.7 for voice clarity, 7.6 for handling sensitive topics, and 7.4 for recommendation helpfulness; the latter varied significantly by employment status and prior experience with mental-health services. The self-administered PHQ-9 served as a pragmatic comparator reflecting real-world digital screening, allowing evaluation of whether the chatbot could faithfully reproduce the questionnaire's structure and scoring accuracy rather than diagnostic validity. These findings indicate that an LLM-powered conversational agent with RAG-based grounding can feasibly and acceptably administer the PHQ-9 with strong score concordance relative to self-report, suggesting potential as a scalable adjunct for early, low-burden depression screening. Further validation against clinician-administered assessments in real-world workflows is warranted. Trial registration ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier NCT06801925.","affiliation":"Institute of Health Informatics University College, London, London, United Kingdom.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0001446"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13298971"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13298971?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42059529","source":"MED","pmid":"42059529","pmcid":"PMC13133002","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13133002"]},"doi":"10.1177/07067437261445767","title":"Novel Abstract Screening Algorithm Using Delphi-Inspired Large Language Model Consensus for Systematic Reviews in Psychiatry: Nouvel algorithme de sélection des résumés utilisant un consensus issu d'un grand modèle de langage inspiré de la méthode Delphi pour les revues systématiques en psychiatrie.","authorString":"Tolend M, Halabi R, Ghaouari K, Lau YCY, Alda M, Hintze A, Mulsant BH, Ortiz A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tolend M","firstName":"Mirkamal","lastName":"Tolend","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4111-0156"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Campbell Family Research Institute, Centre for Addiction and Mental Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Halabi R","firstName":"Ramzi","lastName":"Halabi","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3784-9556"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, UT Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, Texas, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghaouari K","firstName":"Kousai","lastName":"Ghaouari","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-6714-6501"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Campbell Family Research Institute, Centre for Addiction and Mental Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Lau YCY","firstName":"Yvonne C Y","lastName":"Lau","initials":"YCY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-1399-4541"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Campbell Family Research Institute, Centre for Addiction and Mental Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Alda M","firstName":"Martin","lastName":"Alda","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9544-3944"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Dalhousie University, Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada."},{"affiliation":"National Institute of Mental Health, Klecany, Czech Republic."}]}},{"fullName":"Hintze A","firstName":"Arend","lastName":"Hintze","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of MicroData Analytics, Dalarna University, Falun, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Mulsant BH","firstName":"Benoit H","lastName":"Mulsant","initials":"BH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0303-6450"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Campbell Family Research Institute, Centre for Addiction and Mental Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"The Royal Ottawa Hospital, Ottawa, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Ortiz A","firstName":"Abigail","lastName":"Ortiz","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6886-767X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Campbell Family Research Institute, Centre for Addiction and Mental Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Faculty of Medicine, UT Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, Texas, USA."},{"affiliation":"Department of Psychiatry, Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6886-767X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9544-3944"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0303-6450"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3784-9556"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4111-0156"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0001-6714-6501"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-1399-4541"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4173657,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"Canadian journal of psychiatry. Revue canadienne de psychiatrie","medlineAbbreviation":"Can J Psychiatry","isoabbreviation":"Can J Psychiatry","nlmid":"7904187","essn":"1497-0015","issn":"0706-7437"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"7067437261445767","abstractText":"BackgroundLarge language models (LLMs) may reduce the burden associated with performing systematic reviews by prescreening abstracts from a literature search for eligibility for inclusion in full-text review.MethodsWe developed an iterative, LLM-based workflow for screening abstracts: after manual specification of eligibility criteria and seed examples, an ensemble of five LLMs deliberates through a Delphi process to classify a batch of abstracts; these labels are used to train a logistic regression model that ranks the remaining abstracts and identifies a new batch of abstracts for LLM escalation until all abstracts are labelled by the LLM or probability thresholds. We tested our workflow on abstracts screened in three published systematic reviews in psychiatry. Our primary endpoint was the recall metric, and secondary endpoint was the work saved over sampling at 95% recall metric (WSS@95%).ResultsIn a dataset on autism biomarkers, 1,655 (35%) of 4,745 retrieved abstracts were judged to be relevant by the original authors. The Delphi-LLM workflow correctly identified 1,605 (97.0%) of these 1,655 abstracts (precision = 54.2%, WSS@95% = 38.1%). The performance metrics were better than non-LLM approaches (recall ≤ 91%, WSS@95 ≤ 26%), and, overall, balanced these metrics optimally compared to single-LLM agents (recall = 84.9-99.9%, WSS@95% = 16.7-39.8%). The recall and work saved metrics were similarly reliable and among the top in two low-prevalence datasets on an attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder treatment review (10% of 2,891 relevant) and a posttraumatic stress disorder trajectory review (7% of 4,453 relevant). For these two datasets, recall was 100.0% and 96.4%, and the WSS@95% was 17.3% and 18.5%, respectively.ConclusionsWe presented the design and validation of a novel abstract screening workflow that centres around a Delphi-style aggregation process to harness the strengths of five open-source LLMs that can be run on consumer-level workstations. This multi-LLM workflow showed acceptable and reliable performance for use as an automated prescreening method to facilitate systematic reviews.","affiliation":"Campbell Family Research Institute, Centre for Addiction and Mental Health, Toronto, Ontario, Canada.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Systematic Reviews","Delphi Method","Abstract Screening","Text Embedding","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/07067437261445767"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13133002"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13133002?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by-nc","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-04-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-30","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-30","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-30"},{"id":"41952677","source":"MED","pmid":"41952677","pmcid":"PMC13053261","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13053261"]},"doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1795441","title":"Large language models in patient education for brain tumors: opportunities, risks, and ethical considerations.","authorString":"Christodoulou RC, Papageorgiou PS, Lucio Pereira AC, Solomou EE, Papageorgiou SG, Vassiliou E, Georgiou MF.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Christodoulou RC","firstName":"Rafail C","lastName":"Christodoulou","initials":"RC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Neuroimaging and Neurointervention, Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Papageorgiou PS","firstName":"Platon S","lastName":"Papageorgiou","initials":"PS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Lucio Pereira AC","firstName":"Ana Carolina","lastName":"Lucio Pereira","initials":"AC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Oncological Research Institute (IPON)/Discipline of Gynecology and Obstetrics, Federal University of Triângulo Mineiro, Uberaba, Brazil."}]}},{"fullName":"Solomou EE","firstName":"Elena E","lastName":"Solomou","initials":"EE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Internal Medicine-Hematology, University of Patras Medical School, Rion, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Papageorgiou SG","firstName":"Sokratis G","lastName":"Papageorgiou","initials":"SG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"1st Department of Neurology, Medical School, National and Kapodistrian University of Athens, Eginition Hospital, Athens, Greece."}]}},{"fullName":"Vassiliou E","firstName":"Evros","lastName":"Vassiliou","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biological Sciences, Kean University, Union, NJ, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Georgiou MF","firstName":"Michalis F","lastName":"Georgiou","initials":"MF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Radiology, University of Miami, Miami, FL, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6102-3344"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":4110619,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in oncology","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Oncol","isoabbreviation":"Front Oncol","nlmid":"101568867","essn":"2234-943X","issn":"2234-943X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1795441","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Patients with brain tumors often struggle to understand their condition because of complex imaging findings, multidisciplinary care pathways, and frequent cognitive and emotional vulnerability. Effective patient education is, therefore, essential but difficult to deliver within routine clinical encounters.<h4>Objective</h4>This narrative review evaluates the role of large language models (LLMs) in supporting patient education for individuals with brain tumors.<h4>Content</h4>We synthesize evidence from neuro-oncology, radiology, and digital health literature on the use of LLMs to explain imaging results, diagnoses, and treatment options in patient-centered language. Potential benefits include improved health literacy, accessibility, and continuity of education. Key limitations are also discussed, including hallucinations, output variability, overtrust, data privacy concerns, and ethical challenges. A clinician-guided framework for responsible integration is proposed.<h4>Conclusion</h4>When used under clinician supervision as educational support tools, LLMs may enhance patient understanding and engagement in brain tumor care. Safe implementation will require structured governance, oversight, and alignment with ethical standards.","affiliation":"Division of Neuroimaging and Neurointervention, Department of Radiology, Stanford University, Stanford, CA, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Brain tumors","Patient education","Neuro-oncology","Artificial Intelligence In Healthcare","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fonc.2026.1795441"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053261"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13053261?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-24"},{"id":"41836319","source":"MED","pmid":"41836319","pmcid":"PMC12983229","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12983229"]},"doi":"10.3389/fragi.2026.1712785","title":"Evaluating the performance of large language models in sarcopenia-related patient queries: a foundational assessment for patient-centered validation.","authorString":"Huang T, Kirk B, Close J, Lim JY, Duque G, Ebeling P, Yang M, Tian M, Chui CS, Liu C, Zhang N, Cheung WH, Wong RMY.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang T","firstName":"Tao","lastName":"Huang","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Kirk B","firstName":"Ben","lastName":"Kirk","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0176-776X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, Melbourne Medical School, The University of Melbourne, St Albans, VIC, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Close J","firstName":"Jacqueline","lastName":"Close","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Falls, Balance and Injury Research Centre, Neuroscience Research Australia, Randwick, NSW, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Lim JY","firstName":"Jae-Young","lastName":"Lim","initials":"JY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Rehabilitation Medicine, Seoul National University College of Medicine, Seoul National University Bundang Hospital, Seongnam, Republic of Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Duque G","firstName":"Gustavo","lastName":"Duque","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, The McGill University Health Centre, Montreal, QC, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"Ebeling P","firstName":"Peter","lastName":"Ebeling","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medicine, School of Clinical Sciences, Monash University, Clayton, VIC, Australia."}]}},{"fullName":"Yang M","firstName":"Minghui","lastName":"Yang","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, Beijing Jishuitan Hospital, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Tian M","firstName":"Maoyi","lastName":"Tian","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Public Health, Harbin Medical University, Harbin, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chui CS","firstName":"Chun Sing","lastName":"Chui","initials":"CS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu C","firstName":"Chaoran","lastName":"Liu","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang N","firstName":"Ning","lastName":"Zhang","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Cheung WH","firstName":"Wing-Hoi","lastName":"Cheung","initials":"WH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wong RMY","firstName":"Ronald Man Yeung","lastName":"Wong","initials":"RMY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0176-776X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"7","journalIssueId":4110941,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in aging","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Aging","isoabbreviation":"Front Aging","nlmid":"9918231199706676","essn":"2673-6217","issn":"2673-6217"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1712785","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in clinical applications but their performance in specialized areas such as sarcopenia remains understudied.<h4>Methods</h4>A panel of sarcopenia clinician researchers developed 20 standardized patient-centered questions across six clinical domains. Each question was input into all three LLMs, and responses were anonymized, randomized, and independently assessed by three clinician researchers. Accuracy was graded on a four-point scale (\"Poor\" to \"Excellent\"), and comprehensiveness was evaluated for responses rated \"Good\" or higher using a five-point scale.<h4>Results</h4>All LLMs achieved good performance, with no responses rated \"Poor\" across any domain. Deepseek had the longest and most detailed responses (mean word count: 583.75 ± 71.89) and showed superior performance in \"risk factors\" and \"prognosis.\" ChatGPT provided the most concise replies (359.5 ± 87.89 words, p = 0.0011) but achieved the highest proportion of \"Good\" ratings (90%). Gemini excelled in \"pathogenesis\" and \"diagnosis\" but received the most critical feedback in \"prevention and treatment.\" Although trends in performance differences were noted, they did not reach statistical significance. Mean comprehensiveness scores were also similar across models (Deepseek: 4.017 ± 0.77, Gemini: 3.97 ± 0.88, ChatGPT: 3.953 ± 0.83; p > 0.05).<h4>Conclusion</h4>Despite minor differences in performance across domains, all three LLMs demonstrated acceptable accuracy and comprehensiveness when responding to sarcopenia-related queries. Their comparable results may reflect similarly recent training data and language capabilities. These findings suggest that LLMs could potentially serve as a valuable tool in patient education and care on sarcopenia. This study provides an initial, expert-based assessment of LLM information quality regarding sarcopenia. While the responses demonstrated good accuracy, this evaluation focuses on content correctness from a clinical perspective. Future research must complement these findings by directly engaging older adult cohorts before clinical implementation can be considered. However, human oversight remains essential to ensure safe and appropriate assessment and individually tailored advice and management.","affiliation":"Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Sarcopenia","Gemini","Large Language Models","Chatgpt","Deepseek"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fragi.2026.1712785"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12983229"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12983229?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-27","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-27"},{"id":"42080058","source":"MED","pmid":"42080058","pmcid":"PMC13128582","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13128582"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1809142","title":"MedChat: a fully offline multimodal AI system for privacy-preserving clinical anamnesis.","authorString":"Ruhland JB, Bahcivan D, Sowa JP, Canbay A, Heider D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ruhland JB","firstName":"Jan Benedikt","lastName":"Ruhland","initials":"JB","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences, Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Düsseldorf, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Bahcivan D","firstName":"Doguhan","lastName":"Bahcivan","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences, Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Düsseldorf, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Sowa JP","firstName":"Jan-Peter","lastName":"Sowa","initials":"JP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, University Hospital Knappschaftskrankenhaus Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Canbay A","firstName":"Ali","lastName":"Canbay","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Internal Medicine, University Hospital Knappschaftskrankenhaus Bochum, Bochum, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Heider D","firstName":"Dominik","lastName":"Heider","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, Philipps University of Marburg, Marburg, Germany."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Medical Informatics, University of Münster, Münster, Germany."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1809142","abstractText":"Recent advances in large language models made it possible to achieve high conversational performance with substantially reduced computational demands, enabling practical on-site deployment in clinical environments. Such progress allows for local integration of AI systems that uphold strict data protection and patient privacy requirements, yet their secure implementation in medicine necessitates careful consideration of ethical, regulatory, and technical constraints. In this study, we introduce MedChat, a locally deployable virtual physician framework that integrates an LLM-based medical chatbot with a diffusion-driven avatar for automated and structured anamnesis. The chatbot was fine-tuned using a corpus of LLM-generated medical dialogues derived from publicly available symptom-disease datasets, enabling scalable and privacy-preserving training. A secure and isolated database interface was implemented to ensure complete separation between patient data and the model's inference process. The avatar component was realized through a conditional diffusion model operating in latent space, trained on researcher video datasets and synchronized with mel-frequency audio features for realistic speech and facial animation. We demonstrate that the complete multimodal pipeline can operate fully offline on consumer-grade hardware while maintaining interactive response times (average latency: 2.9 ± 0.3 s) and stable system performance. Preliminary evaluation of generated dialogue indicates high linguistic coherence, supporting its suitability for structured anamnesis tasks. MedChat provides a privacy-preserving, resource-efficient, and multimodal solution for clinical data collection. While clinical validation is ongoing, the presented framework establishes a foundation for secure, locally deployable AI-assisted anamnesis in real-world healthcare settings.","affiliation":"Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences, Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Düsseldorf, Germany.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Diffusion model","Machine Learning","Llm","Autoencoder","Ai In Medicine"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1809142"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13128582"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13128582?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-05-04","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-05-19","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-16"},{"id":"41989882","source":"MED","pmid":"41989882","pmcid":"PMC13085985","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13085985"]},"doi":"10.2196/76734","title":"Large Language Models in Cardiology: Systematic Review.","authorString":"Gendler M, N Nadkarni G, Sudri K, Cohen-Shelly M, S Glicksberg B, Efros O, Soffer S, Klang E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gendler M","firstName":"Moran","lastName":"Gendler","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7789-3801"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Azrieli Faculty of Medicine, Bar-Ilan University, Henrietta Szold St 8, Safed, Israel, Safed, 1311502, Israel, 972 542354444."}]}},{"fullName":"N Nadkarni G","firstName":"Girish","lastName":"N Nadkarni","initials":"G","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6319-4314"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Windreich Department of AI and Human Health, Mount Sinai Medical Center, Mount Sinai, New York, NY, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Sudri K","firstName":"Karin","lastName":"Sudri","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5118-8619"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sagol AI Hub, ARC Innovation Center, Sheba Medical Center, Ramat Gan, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Cohen-Shelly M","firstName":"Michal","lastName":"Cohen-Shelly","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6532-9252"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Sagol AI Hub, ARC Innovation Center, Sheba Medical Center, Ramat Gan, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"S Glicksberg B","firstName":"Benjamin","lastName":"S Glicksberg","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4515-8090"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Windreich Department of AI and Human Health, Mount Sinai Medical Center, Mount Sinai, New York, NY, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Efros O","firstName":"Orly","lastName":"Efros","initials":"O","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6024-9110"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel."},{"affiliation":"National Hemophilia Center and Thrombosis Institute, Sheba Medical Center, Ramat Gan, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Soffer S","firstName":"Shelly","lastName":"Soffer","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7853-2029"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel."},{"affiliation":"Institute of Hematology, Davidoff Cancer Center, Rabin Medical Center, Petah-Tikva, Israel."}]}},{"fullName":"Klang E","firstName":"Eyal","lastName":"Klang","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4567-3108"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Windreich Department of AI and Human Health, Mount Sinai Medical Center, Mount Sinai, New York, NY, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6319-4314"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4567-3108"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5118-8619"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6024-9110"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7853-2029"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4515-8090"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-7789-3801"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-6532-9252"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4180270,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-04-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR cardio","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Cardio","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Cardio","nlmid":"101718325","essn":"2561-1011","issn":"2561-1011"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e76734","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in health care, but their role in cardiology has not yet been systematically evaluated.<h4>Objective</h4>This review aimed to assess the applications, performance, and limitations of LLMs across diverse cardiology tasks, including chronic and progressive conditions, acute events, education, and diagnostic testing.<h4>Methods</h4>A systematic search was conducted in PubMed and Scopus for studies published up to April 14, 2024, using keywords related to LLMs and cardiology. Studies evaluating LLM outputs in cardiology-related tasks were included. Data were extracted across 5 predefined domains and the risk of bias was assessed using an adapted QUADAS-2 tool (developed by Whiting et al at the University of Bristol). The review protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD42024556397).<h4>Results</h4>A total of 33 studies contributed quantitative outcome data to a descriptive synthesis. Across chronic conditions, ChatGPT-3.5 (OpenAI) answered 91% (43/47) heart failure questions accurately, although readability often required college-level comprehension. In acute scenarios, Bing Chat omitted key myocardial infarction first aid steps in 25% (5/20) to 45% (9/20) of cases, while cardiac arrest information was rated highly (mean 4.3/5, SD 0.7) but written above recommended reading levels. In physician education tasks, ChatGPT-4 (OpenAI) demonstrated higher accuracy than ChatGPT-3.5, improving from 38% (33/88) to 66% (58/88). In patient education studies, ChatGPT-4 provided scientifically adequate explanations (5.0-6.0/7) comparable to hospital materials but at higher reading levels (11th vs 7th grade). In diagnostic testing, ChatGPT-4 interpreted 91% (36/40) electrocardiogram vignettes correctly, significantly better than emergency physicians (31/40, 77%; P< .001), but showed lower performance in echocardiography.<h4>Conclusions</h4>LLMs show meaningful potential in cardiology, especially for education and electrocardiogram interpretation, but performance varies across clinical tasks. Limitations in emergency guidance and readability, as well as small in silico study designs, highlight the need for multimodal models and prospective validation.","affiliation":"Azrieli Faculty of Medicine, Bar-Ilan University, Henrietta Szold St 8, Safed, Israel, Safed, 1311502, Israel, 972 542354444.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cardiology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Cardiology","Natural Language Processing","Generative Ai","Large Language Models","Llms"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/76734"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13085985"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13085985?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-15","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-20","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-16"},{"id":"42477820","source":"MED","pmid":"42477820","doi":"10.1186/s43058-026-01043-5","title":"Barriers and facilitators to the implementation of a complex nursing intervention: a human-in-the-loop LLM-Assisted longitudinal and Cross-Case analysis across twelve cardiac surgery centres.","authorString":"Jordens YJ, Lechner L, Pelgrum-Keurhorst M, Ettema RGA.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Jordens YJ","firstName":"Yvonne J","lastName":"Jordens","initials":"YJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Department Anesthesiology and Intensive Care, Isala, Zwolle, The Netherlands. y.j.jordens@isala.nl."},{"affiliation":"Department of Health Psychology, Faculty of Behavioural and Health Sciences, Open University of the Netherlands, Heerlen, The Netherlands. y.j.jordens@isala.nl."}]}},{"fullName":"Lechner L","firstName":"Lilian","lastName":"Lechner","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health Psychology, Faculty of Behavioural and Health Sciences, Open University of the Netherlands, Heerlen, The Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Pelgrum-Keurhorst M","firstName":"Myrna","lastName":"Pelgrum-Keurhorst","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Health and Wellbeing, Research Group Person-Centered Health and Social Care, Windesheim University of Applied Science, Zwolle, The Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Ettema RGA","firstName":"Roelof G A","lastName":"Ettema","initials":"RGA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Research Group Personalised Integrated Care, Institute for Nursing Studies, HU University of Applied Science Utrecht, Utrecht, The Netherlands."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4224735,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01","journal":{"title":"Implementation science communications","medlineAbbreviation":"Implement Sci Commun","isoabbreviation":"Implement Sci Commun","nlmid":"101764360","essn":"2662-2211","issn":"2662-2211"}},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Implementation of complex nursing interventions remains challenging, with success varying substantially across healthcare settings. Most implementation research assesses barriers and facilitators at single timepoints, limiting understanding of how these determinants evolve. Therefore, we aimed to understand how barriers and facilitators evolve over time during different implementation phases across three categories of implementation outcomes.<h4>Methods</h4>Following the \"Exploration, Preparation, Implementation, and Sustainment\" (EPIS) framework, this longitudinal qualitative study examined barriers and facilitators during implementation of the PREDOCS nursing intervention across twelve Dutch cardiothoracic centers from 2016 to 2019. We conducted 252 structured interviews across eight measurement waves with diverse healthcare professionals. Data were systematically coded for barriers and facilitators using the Capability, Opportunity, Motivation-Behaviour (COM-B) model through a hybrid approach combining Large Language Model (LLM)-assisted analysis with rigorous human verification. Hospitals were categorized into three implementation outcome groups: Implementation (n = 4), Delayed-Implementation (n = 5), and No-Implementation (n = 3).<h4>Results</h4>Barriers and facilitators evolved systematically across EPIS phases. Motivation remained universally high across all outcome groups throughout all phases. Fundamental knowledge gaps about intervention components during the Exploration phase were present in all three No-Implementation hospitals and absent in the Implementation group. Opportunity-domain factors, specifically the presence or absence of organizational infrastructure, resources, and management support, differed systematically between outcome groups across phases. As implementation progressed, the Implementation group reported a shift in the Capability domain from training barriers to available training and competent staff, a pattern absent in the Delayed and No-Implementation groups. Successful hospitals reported compensatory opportunity facilitators alongside persistent barriers, whereas hospitals that discontinued implementation reported sustained belief in intervention value in the absence of enabling organizational conditions.<h4>Conclusions</h4>Implementation success depends less on motivation-building than on developing organizational infrastructure to translate intention into practice. Implementation determinants are not static: early knowledge gaps and absence of organizational facilitators functioned as early signals of later failure, and support strategies should therefore be phase-specific and longitudinally monitored rather than assessed at a single point in time.","affiliation":"Research Department Anesthesiology and Intensive Care, Isala, Zwolle, The Netherlands. y.j.jordens@isala.nl.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Implementation","Barriers","Facilitators","Nursing Intervention","Ai-assisted Analysis","Llm-assisted Analysis","Qualitative Longitudinal Synthesis"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s43058-026-01043-5"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-21","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-20"},{"id":"PPR1251094","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-9568253/v1","title":"AI Safety Training Can be Clinically Harmful","authorString":"BN S, Sherrill AM, Arriaga RI, Wiese CW, Abdullah S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"BN S","firstName":"Suhas","lastName":"BN","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Sherrill AM","firstName":"Andrew","lastName":"Sherrill","initials":"AM"},{"fullName":"Arriaga RI","firstName":"Rosa","lastName":"Arriaga","initials":"RI"},{"fullName":"Wiese CW","firstName":"Christopher","lastName":"Wiese","initials":"CW"},{"fullName":"Abdullah S","firstName":"Saeed","lastName":"Abdullah","initials":"S"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Large language models are being deployed as mental health support agents at scale, yet only 16\\% of LLM-based chatbot interventions have undergone rigorous clinical efficacy testing \\citep{hua2025charting}. We argue that the dominant alignment paradigm, RLHF-based safety training, can itself become a source of clinical harm when models are placed in protocol-driven psychotherapeutic settings. We evaluated four generative models on 250 Prolonged Exposure (PE) therapy scenarios \\citep{suhas2025thousand} and 146 Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) cognitive restructuring exercises \\citep{nie2024caiti} (plus 29 severity-escalated variants), scored by a three-judge LLM panel. All models score near-perfectly on surface acknowledgment ($\\approx$0.91 to 1.00), while therapeutic appropriateness drops to 0.22 to 0.33 at the highest severity for three of four models and protocol fidelity reaches zero for two. Under CBT severity escalation, one model's task completeness falls from 92\\% to 71\\% while a frontier model's safety-interference score falls from 0.99 to 0.61. Across both modalities we observe a consistent pattern: safety-aligned models intervene with grounding, false reassurance, or crisis-resource insertion during therapy phases that require sustained engagement with distress, or abandon the therapeutic task entirely. These behaviors are protocol violations in PE and CBT despite being precisely what RLHF rewards in general conversation. We treat this as a perspective contribution: the central tension is between RLHF safety objectives and the mechanisms of action of evidence-based therapy, not a deficit in model capability. Because our evidence is built on synthetic scenarios and LLM judges, we report the findings as directional and call for clinician-adjudicated replication before any deployment claims.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9568253/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1251094","source":"PPR","firstPublishDate":"2026-06-12","versionNumber":1,"pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-12"},{"id":"41343858","source":"MED","pmid":"41343858","pmcid":"PMC12677872","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12677872"]},"doi":"10.2196/78186","title":"The Efficacy of Rule-Based Versus Large Language Model-Based Chatbots in Alleviating Symptoms of Depression and Anxiety: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis.","authorString":"Du Q, Ren Y, Meng ZL, He H, Meng S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Du Q","firstName":"Qiuxue","lastName":"Du","initials":"Q","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-1787-7284"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Beijing Yuxin Technology Co., Ltd, Room 2-5, 13th Floor, Building 2, No. 48, Zhichun Road, Haidian District, Beijing, 100086, China, 86 010-81377053."}]}},{"fullName":"Ren Y","firstName":"Yongliang","lastName":"Ren","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-7935-8129"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Beijing Yuxin Technology Co., Ltd, Room 2-5, 13th Floor, Building 2, No. 48, Zhichun Road, Haidian District, Beijing, 100086, China, 86 010-81377053."}]}},{"fullName":"Meng ZL","firstName":"Ze-Long","lastName":"Meng","initials":"ZL","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2652-5812"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Psychology, School of Humanities and Social Sciences, Beijing Forestry University, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"He H","firstName":"Han","lastName":"He","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-6087-1315"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Beijing Yuxin Technology Co., Ltd, Room 2-5, 13th Floor, Building 2, No. 48, Zhichun Road, Haidian District, Beijing, 100086, China, 86 010-81377053."}]}},{"fullName":"Meng S","firstName":"Shasha","lastName":"Meng","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-0508-4070"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Beijing Yuxin Technology Co., Ltd, Room 2-5, 13th Floor, Building 2, No. 48, Zhichun Road, Haidian District, Beijing, 100086, China, 86 010-81377053."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2652-5812"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-6087-1315"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-1787-7284"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-7935-8129"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-0508-4070"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"27","journalIssueId":4059736,"dateOfPublication":"2025 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2025,"printPublicationDate":"2025-12-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2025","pageInfo":"e78186","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>The global mental health crisis is becoming increasingly severe. Due to the shortage of mental health professionals, high treatment costs, and insufficient accessibility of services, there is an urgent need for scalable and low-cost intervention methods. In recent years, chatbots have shown potential for psychological interventions. The efficacy differences between large language model (LLM)-based and rule-based chatbots have not been systematically evaluated, with few studies directly comparing the two; existing meta-analyses have notable limitations: there is high heterogeneity in intervention design (eg, dialogue structure, interaction frequency, and duration) across studies, and there is a lack of direct comparison of differentiated intervention effects on depressive and anxiety symptoms, making it difficult to integrate conclusions.<h4>Objective</h4>By integrating studies from the past five years, this research evaluates the differences in effectiveness between LLM-based and rule-based chatbots in alleviating depressive and anxiety symptoms. It also analyzes the impacts of control group type, intervention duration, and age on intervention outcomes. By analyzing chatbot functionality, the study aims to provide evidence-based technological pathway options and optimization recommendations for differentiated interventions for depression and anxiety.<h4>Methods</h4>A systematic search of 7 databases included 15 studies published between 2020 and 2025. Robust variance estimation (RVE) was used to account for non-independent effect sizes, and standardized mean differences (SMDs) were calculated using Hedges g. Based on the expectation of clinical and methodological heterogeneity among studies, a random-effects model was preselected, and the pooled effect size was estimated using restricted maximum likelihood estimation (REML) and interpreted according to Cohen criteria. Publication bias was assessed using the RVE-adjusted Egger test, funnel plot asymmetry, and a fail-safe N.<h4>Results</h4>For depression, rule-based intervention achieved a small but significant effect (g=0.266; 95% CI 0.020-0.512; P=.04), while LLM-based intervention showed a nonsignificant effect with wide confidence intervals (g=0.407; 95% CI -0.734 to 1.550; P=.17). For anxiety, rule-based intervention did not yield a significant effect (g=0.147; 95% CI -0.073 to 0.367; P=.15). Similarly, LLM-based intervention showed a higher point estimate but also with nonsignificance and wide confidence intervals (g=0.711; 95% CI -0.334 to 1.760; P=.13). Subgroup analysis showed that the rule-based chatbot was more effective than the blank control for depression, with the greatest effect in the medium term (4-8 weeks).<h4>Conclusions</h4>Rule-based chatbots have a modest effect on improving depressive symptoms and are suitable for environments with limited psychological resources; 4-8 weeks may be a critical intervention window. Intervention duration and participant age did not significantly influence intervention effectiveness. Limited by the sample size, robust evidence supporting the effectiveness of LLM-based chatbot interventions is lacking, and further sample size expansion is warranted.","affiliation":"Beijing Yuxin Technology Co., Ltd, Room 2-5, 13th Floor, Building 2, No. 48, Zhichun Road, Haidian District, Beijing, 100086, China, 86 010-81377053.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Meta-Analysis","Systematic Review","review-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Depression","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Language"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Anxiety","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Generative Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Depression","Anxiety","Mental health","Prisma","Preferred Reporting Items For Systematic Reviews And Meta-analyses","Chatbots","Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/78186"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12677872"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12677872?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":5,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2025-12-04","dateOfCreation":"2025-12-04","firstIndexDate":"2025-12-05","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-12-23","dateOfRevision":"2025-12-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2025-12-04","firstPublicationDate":"2025-12-04"},{"id":"42263176","source":"MED","pmid":"42263176","pmcid":"PMC13249113","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13249113"]},"doi":"10.2196/79242","title":"SleepPathfinder: A Socratic Questioning and Self-Decision-Based Chatbot to Support User Engagement in Digital CBT-I: Usability and Feasibility Study.","authorString":"Roh Y, Yoon AS, Oh H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Roh Y","firstName":"Youjin","lastName":"Roh","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5825-6623"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Artificial Intelligence, Sungkyunkwan University, #32541, Dasan Hall of Economics, Humanities and Social Sciences Campus, Seoul, Republic of Korea, 82 2-740-1785, 82 2-740-1785."}]}},{"fullName":"Yoon AS","firstName":"Anderson Sungmin","lastName":"Yoon","initials":"AS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2185-4382"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Social Welfare, Sungkyunkwan University, Seoul, Republic of Korea."}]}},{"fullName":"Oh H","firstName":"Hayoung","lastName":"Oh","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7362-5138"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Applied Artificial Intelligence, Sungkyunkwan University, #32541, Dasan Hall of Economics, Humanities and Social Sciences Campus, Seoul, Republic of Korea, 82 2-740-1785, 82 2-740-1785."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7362-5138"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2185-4382"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5825-6623"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"10","journalIssueId":4204625,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR formative research","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Form Res","nlmid":"101726394","essn":"2561-326X","issn":"2561-326X"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e79242","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Chronic insomnia is a highly prevalent sleep disorder that adversely affects quality of life and mental health. Cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is internationally recommended as the first-line treatment, and digital CBT-I (dCBT-I) has been developed to improve accessibility and scalability. While existing dCBT-I systems effectively support structured behavioral training through standardized protocols, they provide relatively limited support for users' cognitive exploration and meaning-making processes, particularly in helping users reflect on and internalize the rationale behind CBT-I practices in daily life. These limitations may contribute to challenges in sustained engagement and long-term adherence.<h4>Objective</h4>This study aimed to examine the usability and feasibility of SleepPathfinder, a conversational CBT-I support chatbot that integrates Socratic questioning and a self-decision mechanism to support users' understanding of and engagement with CBT-I practices.<h4>Methods</h4>SleepPathfinder was designed around a 4-stage conversational flow: education on CBT-I techniques, Socratic cognitive exploration, self-decision, and advice provision. We conducted (1) a single-session pilot usability study (n=45) to assess system stability and user experience and (2) a 5-day condition-based comparative experiment (n=30) consisting of daily sessions, comparing an exploratory dialogue condition with a directive, protocol-guided dialogue condition. Quantitative measures assessed usability, cognitive appraisals related to sleep problems, autonomy-related experiences, and behavioral readiness, while qualitative feedback and conversational log analyses were used to examine interaction patterns and engagement characteristics.<h4>Results</h4>In the comparative experiment, the exploratory dialogue condition showed a tendency toward reduced perceived threat and severity appraisal of sleep problems compared with the directive condition, accompanied by moderate effect sizes in cognitive perception measures. Autonomy-related experiences, including perceived choice and engagement, demonstrated suggestive upward trends in the exploratory condition. Behavioral intention changes were comparable across conditions, while overall readiness for change increased across participants. Conversational log analyses indicated that greater depth and volume of user self-narrative were associated with larger shifts in cognitive appraisals, whereas the frequency of chatbot questions alone was not. The pilot usability study indicated generally positive evaluations of system usability and content credibility, while identifying areas for improvement in emotional responsiveness and conversational naturalness.<h4>Conclusions</h4>These findings suggest that a Socratic questioning-based and self-decision-based conversational structure is usable and feasible as a supportive interaction layer within dCBT-I systems. Rather than altering the directive behavioral structure of CBT-I, such an approach may complement existing protocols by facilitating cognitive exploration and supporting user-perceived autonomy. This study provides design-oriented evidence to inform the refinement of dialogue-supported digital CBT-I systems aimed at enhancing user engagement with CBT-I practices.","affiliation":"Department of Applied Artificial Intelligence, Sungkyunkwan University, #32541, Dasan Hall of Economics, Humanities and Social Sciences Campus, Seoul, Republic of Korea, 82 2-740-1785, 82 2-740-1785.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Sleep Initiation and Maintenance Disorders","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"PX","qualifierName":"psychology","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Feasibility Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pilot Projects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Adult"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Middle Aged"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Male"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cognitive Behavioral Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"IS","qualifierName":"instrumentation","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"ST","qualifierName":"standards","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cbt","Cognitive Behavioral Therapy","User Engagement","Digital Health","Conversational Agent"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/79242"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13249113"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13249113?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-10","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-09"},{"id":"42347687","source":"MED","pmid":"42347687","pmcid":"PMC13295427","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13295427"]},"doi":"10.2196/91727","title":"Personal Health Large Language Models and the Negotiation of Medical Authority in Clinical Care: Opportunities, Risks, and Governance.","authorString":"Xie W, Liu J, Liu S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Xie W","firstName":"Wenyi","lastName":"Xie","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7826-2927"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical Intensive Care Unit, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu J","firstName":"Jialin","lastName":"Liu","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Information Center, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, No. 37 Guo Xue Xiang Street, Chengdu, Sichuan, 610041, China, 86 28-85422416, 86 28-85582944."},{"affiliation":"Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu S","firstName":"Siru","lastName":"Liu","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Informatics, Vanderbilt University Medical Center, Nashville, TN, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1369-4625"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5003-5354"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7826-2927"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e91727","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Personal health large language models (PH-LLMs) are patient-facing conversational systems that synthesize user-entered information, patient-generated health data, wearable data, and selected personal health records-where users choose to connect them-into personalized, longitudinal, action-oriented health narratives. Unlike generic health chatbots that mainly provide one-off responses to isolated questions, PH-LLMs may generate continuing interpretations, priorities, and candidate next steps that patients bring into clinical encounters. In contrast to electronic health record-tethered clinical artificial intelligence (AI), they often originate outside institutional oversight and may be selected, used, or trusted by patients before professional review. This viewpoint examines how PH-LLMs may reshape the negotiation of medical authority by contributing to a shift from the traditional dyadic clinician-patient relationship toward a triadic model of negotiated authority, in which clinicians may increasingly need to mediate among clinical evidence, patient values, and algorithmic narratives. PH-LLMs may support patient participation by organizing symptoms, contextualizing home-monitoring and wearable data, improving health literacy, assisting chronic disease self-management, and preparing patients for more collaborative visits. Patient-facing AI narratives may also introduce distinct risks. At the individual level, these include inaccurate or incomplete responses arising from imprecise queries or missing context, misinterpretation of otherwise accurate information in the absence of clinical context, and contextually biased or poorly matched advice across demographic, cultural, linguistic, disability-related, or socioeconomic contexts. At the system level, they include authority conflict when AI recommendations diverge from clinical judgment, fragmentation of clinical truth, privacy and data-governance concerns, diffusion of accountability when harm results from advice produced outside clinical governance, and inequitable access to premium tools and continuous monitoring devices. To address these challenges, we propose a 3-layer clinical governance framework for patient-brought PH-LLM narratives. The first layer, evidence and provenance, makes AI-generated narratives epistemically legible by clarifying platform identity, data sources, temporal anchoring, uncertainty, and privacy-relevant data-use and retention conditions. The second layer, clinical arbitration and workflow integration, uses risk-stratified intake, proportionate documentation, escalation triggers, and equity-preserving workflows to embed PH-LLM outputs into routine care. The third layer, competence and accountability, defines the communication competencies, AI literacy supports, institutional responsibilities, vendor accountability, and risk-proportionate verification duties needed for triadic care. This framework is a conceptual and governance-oriented proposal rather than a validated clinical protocol. Future empirical work should evaluate its feasibility, documentation burden, equity effects, clinical safety impact, and acceptability among patients, clinicians, and health systems. Governed through these interdependent layers, PH-LLMs may serve as supporting infrastructure for safer, person-centered longitudinal care.","affiliation":"Medical Intensive Care Unit, West China Hospital of Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Negotiating"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Physician-Patient Relations"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Health Records, Personal"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Electronic Health Records"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Clinical Governance","Accountability","Shared Decision-making","Medical Authority","Digital Health","Clinician-patient Relationship","Patient-generated Health Data","Personal Health Large Language Models"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/91727"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295427"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13295427?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-27","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-25"},{"id":"42068864","source":"MED","pmid":"42068864","doi":"10.1016/j.prevetmed.2026.106908","title":"A rule-based large language model screening framework for evidence synthesis in animal health: Feasibility evaluation in a veterinary scoping review.","authorString":"Bernal-Córdoba C, Valldecabres A, Borona-Valencia M, Canales-Muñoz O, Silva-Del-Río N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bernal-Córdoba C","firstName":"Christian","lastName":"Bernal-Córdoba","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4295-4107"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Veterinary Medicine Teaching and Research Center, 18830 Road 112, Tulare, CA, United States; Population Health and Reproduction, School of Veterinary Medicine, University of California, Davis, CA, United States. Electronic address: crbernalcordoba@ucdavis.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Valldecabres A","firstName":"Ainhoa","lastName":"Valldecabres","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Facultad de Veterinaria, Universidad Cardenal Herrera-CEU, CEU Universities, Valencia 46115, Spain. Electronic address: ainhoa.valldecabres@uchceu.es."}]}},{"fullName":"Borona-Valencia M","firstName":"Manuel","lastName":"Borona-Valencia","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Facultad de Veterinaria, Universidad Cardenal Herrera-CEU, CEU Universities, Valencia 46115, Spain. Electronic address: manuel.boronavalencia@alumnos.uchceu.es."}]}},{"fullName":"Canales-Muñoz O","firstName":"Oliver","lastName":"Canales-Muñoz","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Veterinary Medicine Teaching and Research Center, 18830 Road 112, Tulare, CA, United States; Population Health and Reproduction, School of Veterinary Medicine, University of California, Davis, CA, United States. Electronic address: olicanales@ucdavis.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Silva-Del-Río N","firstName":"N","lastName":"Silva-Del-Río","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Veterinary Medicine Teaching and Research Center, 18830 Road 112, Tulare, CA, United States; Population Health and Reproduction, School of Veterinary Medicine, University of California, Davis, CA, United States. Electronic address: nsilvadelrio@ucdavis.edu."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4295-4107"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"254","journalIssueId":4237681,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","journal":{"title":"Preventive veterinary medicine","medlineAbbreviation":"Prev Vet Med","isoabbreviation":"Prev Vet Med","nlmid":"8217463","essn":"1873-1716","issn":"0167-5877"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"106908","abstractText":"Evidence synthesis is essential for summarizing existing knowledge and identifying research gaps in animal health, but study screening is resource-intensive and time-consuming. This study evaluated the feasibility of a rule-based large language model (LLM) screening framework developed to support protocol-aligned study selection in a veterinary scoping review on feed additives in calves. The framework was compared with consensus decisions from three independent human reviewers across two screening stages: title-and-abstract screening and full-text screening. Agreement between framework-generated and human consensus decisions was assessed descriptively, using human consensus as the operational reference standard. At title-and-abstract screening, overall agreement was 96.8% (211/218 records), with seven discordant decisions. At full-text screening, overall agreement was 97.5% (39/40 records), with one discordant exclusion. Discrepancies in stage 1 reflected ambiguity or incomplete reporting, as well as differences in eligibility criteria interpretation relative to the intended screening scope. In stage 2, discrepancies occurred when the framework diverged from human decisions because information was distributed across narrative and tabular elements that required integration. Operational screening time was substantially shorter for the LLM screening framework than for the human screening process. These findings support the feasibility of a structured, rule-based LLM screening framework as a decision-support tool for veterinary evidence synthesis when implemented with predefined eligibility criteria, documented prompts, and human oversight.","affiliation":"Veterinary Medicine Teaching and Research Center, 18830 Road 112, Tulare, CA, United States; Population Health and Reproduction, School of Veterinary Medicine, University of California, Davis, CA, United States. Electronic address: crbernalcordoba@ucdavis.edu.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Scoping Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cattle"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Feasibility Studies"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Veterinary Medicine","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Veterinary medicine","Evidence Synthesis","Animal Health","Scoping Review","Large Language Models","Study Screening"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.prevetmed.2026.106908"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-03","dateOfCreation":"2026-05-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-05-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-28","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-28"},{"id":"42445655","source":"MED","pmid":"42445655","doi":"10.7759/cureus.110790","title":"AI-Augmented Mentorship in Orthopedic Surgery: A Conceptual Framework for Expanding Access for Underrepresented and Less-Resourced Students.","authorString":"Salem-Hernández J, Santiago-Gadea PA, Pérez-Vicente CA, Señeriz-Ortiz R, Marrero PV, Ramírez N.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Salem-Hernández J","firstName":"Joseph","lastName":"Salem-Hernández","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Ponce Health Sciences University, Ponce, PRI."}]}},{"fullName":"Santiago-Gadea PA","firstName":"Peter A","lastName":"Santiago-Gadea","initials":"PA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Central University of the Caribbean, Bayamon, PRI."}]}},{"fullName":"Pérez-Vicente CA","firstName":"Camilla A","lastName":"Pérez-Vicente","initials":"CA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Central University of the Caribbean, Bayamon, PRI."}]}},{"fullName":"Señeriz-Ortiz R","firstName":"Rafael","lastName":"Señeriz-Ortiz","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Ponce Health Sciences University, Ponce, PRI."}]}},{"fullName":"Marrero PV","firstName":"Pablo V","lastName":"Marrero","initials":"PV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Ponce Health Sciences University, Ponce, PRI."}]}},{"fullName":"Ramírez N","firstName":"Norman","lastName":"Ramírez","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Ponce Health Sciences University, Ponce, PRI."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-0690-9120"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4206252,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Cureus","medlineAbbreviation":"Cureus","isoabbreviation":"Cureus","nlmid":"101596737","essn":"2168-8184","issn":"2168-8184"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e110790","abstractText":"Orthopedic surgery remains one of the least diverse medical specialties in the United States, with women and racial/ethnic minorities being significantly underrepresented. Traditional mentorship models often depend on institutional prestige, geographic proximity, and professional networks, creating barriers for underrepresented minority students and trainees from lower-resourced institutions. Increasing residency competitiveness further highlights disparities in mentorship access. The aim of this study was to examine how artificial intelligence (AI) technologies may expand mentorship access and reduce disparities in orthopedic surgery training pathways. This conceptual framework paper narratively synthesizes published literature on mentorship disparities in surgical training, AI applications in medical education, and residency selection equity, identified through database searches (PubMed, ERIC, Google Scholar) using terms including \"orthopaedic surgery\", \"mentorship\", \"artificial intelligence\", \"large language models\", \"residency diversity\", and \"underrepresented minorities\", supplemented by hand-searching reference lists of key articles. Three AI-driven approaches are integrated: (1) large language model (LLM)-based virtual mentorship platforms, (2) residency data dashboards, and (3) social media analytics for network mapping. AI-augmented mentorship platforms may improve accessibility, scalability, and transparency compared with traditional mentorship models. LLM-based tools provide continuous access to educational support, while dashboards and social media analytics may reduce informational and networking barriers. However, these are proposed benefits extrapolated from adjacent fields; none have been empirically validated in an orthopedic surgery context. Risks including algorithmic bias, unequal technology access, and limited psychosocial support must be addressed. Equity-centered implementation, diverse training datasets, human oversight, and bias monitoring are essential prerequisites to ensure these technologies reduce rather than worsen disparities. AI technologies have the potential to broaden mentorship access in orthopedic surgery, though this remains unproven empirically and requires prospective validation before programmatic adoption. Strategic integration of AI-based mentorship tools may help complement, rather than replace, traditional human mentorship. Rigorous evaluation, equity-centered design, human oversight, and strong data governance are essential. Collaboration among orthopedic organizations, medical schools, and residency programs will be necessary to ensure equitable implementation.","affiliation":"Department of Orthopedic Surgery, Ponce Health Sciences University, Ponce, PRI.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Diversity","Medical education","Orthopaedic Surgery","Mentorship","Residency Training","Social Media","Health Equity","Underrepresented Minorities","Large Language Models"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.110790"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"N","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-15","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-13","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-13"},{"id":"41719483","source":"MED","pmid":"41719483","pmcid":"PMC12923100","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC12923100"]},"doi":"10.2196/79044","title":"AI and Digital Tools in Dermatology: Addressing Access and Misinformation.","authorString":"du Crest D, Madhumita M, Enbiale W, Ruiz Postigo JA, Malvehy J, Wongvibulsin S, Gupta S, Kittler H, Skayem C, Mahto A, Adamson AS, Lipoff JB, Papier A, Cartier H, Garson S, Freeman E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"du Crest D","firstName":"Dominique","lastName":"du Crest","initials":"D","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2496-3018"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"SkinAid SAS, 50 Rue Danton, Levallois Perret, 92300, France, 33 620715128."}]}},{"fullName":"Madhumita M","firstName":"Monisha","lastName":"Madhumita","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5652-8785"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Saveetha Medical College, Chennai, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Enbiale W","firstName":"Wendemagegn","lastName":"Enbiale","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0692-850X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Medicine and Health Sciences, Bahir Dar University, Bahir Dar, Ethiopia."}]}},{"fullName":"Ruiz Postigo JA","firstName":"Jose Antonio","lastName":"Ruiz Postigo","initials":"JA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7165-8358"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"World Health Organization, Geneva, Switzerland."}]}},{"fullName":"Malvehy J","firstName":"Josep","lastName":"Malvehy","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6998-914X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Centro de Investigación Biomédica en Red de Enfermedades Raras Instituto de Salud Carlos III, Barcelona, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Wongvibulsin S","firstName":"Shannon","lastName":"Wongvibulsin","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1390-7440"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Dermatology, Department of Medicine, David Geffen School of Medicine, University of California, Los Angeles, CA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Gupta S","firstName":"Somesh","lastName":"Gupta","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3015-8602"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"All India Institute of Medical Sciences, New Delhi, New Delhi, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kittler H","firstName":"Harald","lastName":"Kittler","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0051-8016"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Medical University of Vienna, Vienna, Austria."}]}},{"fullName":"Skayem C","firstName":"Charbel","lastName":"Skayem","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4498-0683"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hôpital Ambroise-Paré, Boulogne Billancourt, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Mahto A","firstName":"Anjali","lastName":"Mahto","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-1908-1350"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Self London, London, United Kingdom."}]}},{"fullName":"Adamson AS","firstName":"Adewole S","lastName":"Adamson","initials":"AS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1493-1776"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Texas at Austin Dell Medical School, Austin, TX, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Lipoff JB","firstName":"Jules B","lastName":"Lipoff","initials":"JB","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0557-4260"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, Lewis Katz School of Medicine, Temple University, Philadelphia, PA, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Papier A","firstName":"Art","lastName":"Papier","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0181-0481"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Rochester College of Medicine, Rochester, NY, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Cartier H","firstName":"Hugues","lastName":"Cartier","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3334-7601"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Saint-Jean Clinic of Dermatology, Arras, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Garson S","firstName":"Sébastien","lastName":"Garson","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-1679-6552"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"SELARL Dr GARSON, Senlis, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Freeman E","firstName":"Esther","lastName":"Freeman","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7751-9466"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Massachusetts General Hospital, Department of Dermatology, Boston, MA, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5652-8785"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7751-9466"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0051-8016"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0181-0481"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0557-4260"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-0692-850X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1390-7440"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1493-1776"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2496-3018"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4498-0683"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6998-914X"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7165-8358"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3015-8602"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3334-7601"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-1679-6552"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-1908-1350"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4116161,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-02-01","journal":{"title":"JMIR dermatology","medlineAbbreviation":"JMIR Dermatol","isoabbreviation":"JMIR Dermatol","nlmid":"101770607","essn":"2562-0959","issn":"2562-0959"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e79044","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Digital dermatology, which is defined as the use of digital technologies that leverage individual- and population-level skin data to improve the diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of skin diseases, has emerged as a critical frontier for bridging persistent gaps in dermatologic care. This transformation holds particular promise for addressing long-standing inequities linked to geography, income, and skin type. According to the Global Burden of Disease 2023 study, skin and subcutaneous diseases remain among the most prevalent global health conditions, contributing substantially to disability-adjusted life years. Digital tools (including teledermatology, artificial intelligence [AI], and large language models) offer new ways to extend diagnosis, education, and patient empowerment to historically underserved populations. However, these same innovations risk amplifying disparities if they are not designed and deployed intentionally. Algorithmic bias, uneven digital access, and the absence of culturally responsive models can undermine progress. In this conceptual and narrative review, we draw on expert dialogues and illustrative literature, including multistakeholder exchanges at the Skin and Digital Summit (2023-2025) and related global forums, to examine how digital dermatology can promote equitable skin health. We focus on 3 interlinked priorities: expanding access through scalable digital platforms, ensuring AI fairness via comprehensive and diverse datasets, and countering dermatological misinformation. Central to the latter is a bot concept described here as a dynamic cycle that analyzes scientific literature; ranks evidence; translates complex research into clear language; and delivers trustworthy, personalized guidance to both consumers and clinicians. By embedding expert oversight and evidence prioritization, such tools can ensure that accurate, actionable information reaches users at the speed and scale of the internet. Drawing on case studies (including lessons from the World Health Organization's AI skin health app) and insights from the Skin and Digital Summit, we highlight both the transformative potential and the ethical complexities of these digital solutions. To navigate this evolving landscape, we propose the concept of radical dermatology, which confronts the reality that big tech is reshaping skin health whether we like it or not and insists that dermatologists and stakeholders lead the transformation through bold collaboration and unwavering clinical relevance.","affiliation":"SkinAid SAS, 50 Rue Danton, Levallois Perret, 92300, France, 33 620715128.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"001","agency":"World Health Organization","acronym":"WHO_","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Skin Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"DI","qualifierName":"diagnosis","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Communication"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Dermatology","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"TD","qualifierName":"trends","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Telemedicine"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Artificial Intelligence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Health"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Digital Media"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","SKin","AI","Dermatology","Misinformation","Teledermatology","Global Health","Social Media","Digital Health","Llm","Health Tech","Ethical Ai","Digital Dermatology","Large Language Model","Radical Dermatology","Ai In Health Care"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/79044"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12923100"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC12923100?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-02-20","firstIndexDate":"2026-02-21","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-02-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-02-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-02-20"},{"id":"41897273","source":"MED","pmid":"41897273","pmcid":"PMC13026511","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13026511"]},"doi":"10.3390/healthcare14060820","title":"From Tool to Agent: A Semi-Systematic Review of Human-AI Alignment and a Proposed Tiered Healing Ecosystem for Mental Health.","authorString":"Ma A, Chen J, Yang Z.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ma A","firstName":"Anran","lastName":"Ma","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-4552-440X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Engineering Research Center for E-Learning, Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan 430079, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen J","firstName":"Jingying","lastName":"Chen","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Engineering Research Center for E-Learning, Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan 430079, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yang Z","firstName":"Zhiyi","lastName":"Yang","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Engineering Research Center for E-Learning, Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan 430079, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0009-4552-440X"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"14","journalIssueId":4161913,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-03-01","journal":{"title":"Healthcare (Basel, Switzerland)","medlineAbbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","isoabbreviation":"Healthcare (Basel)","nlmid":"101666525","essn":"2227-9032","issn":"2227-9032"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"820","abstractText":"<b>Background</b>: This study aims to systematically analyze the structural transition of AI in mental health, differentiating between passive tools and autonomous agents, and to propose a governance framework to facilitate responsible integration or mitigate integration risks. <b>Methods</b>: Employing a semi-systematic approach, we screened records from IEEE Xplore, PubMed, and ACM DL, ultimately analyzing 61 included studies. We track the transition from the first paradigm, AI-as-Tool (AI-T) to the second paradigm, AI-as-Agent (AI-A). <b>Results</b>: Early empirical evidence suggests that AI-A systems may assist in fostering preliminary working alliances and demonstrate potential for symptom reduction in controlled settings; however, their efficacy cannot currently be equated with, nor serve as a replacement for, standard low-intensity clinical care. <b>Conclusions</b>: To mitigate these risks, we propose the Tiered Human-AI Healing Ecosystem (THHE) for mental health. This framework utilizes dynamic autonomy modulation-automatically restricting AI agency based on real-time risk markers-to manage transitions between AI-led support and human-led care, promoting clinical safety.","affiliation":"National Engineering Research Center for E-Learning, Faculty of Artificial Intelligence in Education, Central China Normal University, Wuhan 430079, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"62377018","agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Therapeutic Alliance","Paradigm Shift","Artificial Intelligence In Mental Health","Ai Agents And Large Language Models","Human–Ai Alignment","Tiered Human–Ai Healing Ecosystem (Thhe)"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare14060820"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13026511"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13026511?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-03-28","dateOfCreation":"2026-03-28","firstIndexDate":"2026-03-29","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-03-28","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-03-23","firstPublicationDate":"2026-03-23"},{"id":"42022146","source":"MED","pmid":"42022146","pmcid":"PMC13095736","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13095736"]},"doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1748544","title":"Leveraging chatbots for enhanced decision-making: a comprehensive literature review.","authorString":"Simelane PM, Kittur J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Simelane PM","firstName":"Phiwe M","lastName":"Simelane","initials":"PM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."},{"affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kittur J","firstName":"Javeed","lastName":"Kittur","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."},{"affiliation":"Engineering Pathways, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"9","journalIssueId":4117457,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"Frontiers in artificial intelligence","medlineAbbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","isoabbreviation":"Front Artif Intell","nlmid":"101770551","essn":"2624-8212","issn":"2624-8212"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1748544","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Chatbots using large language models such as ChatGPT, Google Bard, etc., have become increasingly popular in recent years. The chatbot's ability to simulate conversations with users and process input data and respond based on that information has prompted researchers to investigate the applicability of chatbots in decision-making processes across multiple fields. Current literature has investigated the benefits and challenges of using chatbots in a broad context. This paper presents a systematic literature review of current literature discussing chatbots and decision-making processes, exploring the quality of the decision-making process and user perceptions of using chatbots in the decision-making process.<h4>Methods</h4>This SLR aims to provide a comprehensive view of the disciplinary fields in which chatbot decision-making has been evaluated, how chatbots can be used in various fields, and how it compares to human decision-making. Thirty-six articles from seven databases were reviewed in this paper and categorized into six themes: benefits of using chatbot for decision-making, challenges of chatbot-supported decision-making, ethical considerations of using chatbot-supported decision-making, algorithms/tools used in designing chatbots, human vs. AI decision-making, and chatbot decision-making in different fields.<h4>Results</h4>An analysis of these themes revealed (i) benefits of personalized recommendations in decision-making, (ii) issues with inconsistency in output, (iii) ethical concerns about chatbots using sensitive information to make decisions, (iv) ChatGPT's decision-making is the most studied, and (v) human vs. AI decision-making.<h4>Discussion</h4>The practical and research implications of these findings are further explained in the paper.","affiliation":"Electrical Engineering, Gallogly College of Engineering, University of Oklahoma, Norman, OK, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Systematic Review","systematic-review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["decision-making","Systematic Literature Review","Chatbot","Chatgpt","Llm-Based Chatbots"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frai.2026.1748544"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13095736"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13095736?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-04-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-04-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-04-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-04-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-04-07","firstPublicationDate":"2026-04-07"},{"id":"42269012","source":"MED","pmid":"42269012","pmcid":"PMC13252702","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13252702"]},"doi":"10.2196/93470","title":"Ethical Governance of Large Language Models in Health Care: Trust, Responsibility, and Equity in Routine Use.","authorString":"Yang X, Xu C.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yang X","firstName":"Xiongwen","lastName":"Yang","initials":"X","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4968-8953"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507."},{"affiliation":"NHC Key Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunological Diseases, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, Guiyang, Guizhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu C","firstName":"Chuan","lastName":"Xu","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-5849-8780"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507."},{"affiliation":"NHC Key Laboratory of Pulmonary Immunological Diseases, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, Guiyang, Guizhou, China."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4968-8953"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-5849-8780"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"28","journalIssueId":4204496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","journal":{"title":"Journal of medical Internet research","medlineAbbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","isoabbreviation":"J Med Internet Res","nlmid":"100959882","essn":"1438-8871","issn":"1439-4456"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e93470","abstractText":"<h4>Unlabelled</h4>Large language models (LLMs) are becoming increasingly embedded in routine health care communication, raising ethical challenges that extend beyond model performance alone. This Viewpoint argues that ethical risks in LLM-enabled health care emerge through patterns of reliance, institutional embedding, and governance during real-world use. Using \"adoption-phase ethics\" as an analytic lens, this paper examines 3 interrelated dimensions of ethical risk. First, trust in LLM-enabled health care is shaped not only by technical accuracy, but also by institutional and relational conditions surrounding its use. Second, responsibility may become distributed and ambiguous when LLM-mediated information influences clinical communication and decision-making. Third, equity concerns arise from unequal capacities to interpret, contest, and benefit from LLM-generated information. We argue that ethical governance of LLMs in health care requires continuous, system-level oversight that extends beyond model evaluation alone, including clear accountability structures, role-sensitive implementation, and equity-oriented governance practices. By reframing ethical analysis around routine integration rather than technical performance alone, this Viewpoint aims to support more responsible and sustainable use of LLMs in health care.","affiliation":"Department of Thoracic Surgery, Guizhou Provincial People's Hospital, 83 Zhongshan East Road, Guiyang, Guizhou, 550002, China, 86 18620726507, 86 18620726507.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Trust"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Social Responsibility"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Delivery of Health Care","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ES","qualifierName":"ethics","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Large Language Models"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Medical Ethics","Responsibility","Technology Adoption","Health Care Communication","Ethical Governance","Large Language Models","Equity And Trust"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2196/93470"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13252702"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13252702?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","license":"cc by","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-12","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-13","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-10"},{"id":"42317822","source":"MED","pmid":"42317822","pmcid":"PMC13274269","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13274269"]},"title":"Prompt injection of OpenAI custom GPTs leaks informatics secrets.","authorString":"Truong VQ, Ritchie MD.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Truong VQ","firstName":"Van Q","lastName":"Truong","initials":"VQ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Genomics & Computational Biology PhD Program, University of Pennsylvania, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ritchie MD","firstName":"Marylyn D","lastName":"Ritchie","initials":"MD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Biomedical Informatics & AI, Medical University of South Carolina, USA."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"2026","journalIssueId":4218929,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01","journal":{"title":"AMIA Joint Summits on Translational Science proceedings. AMIA Joint Summits on Translational Science","medlineAbbreviation":"AMIA Jt Summits Transl Sci Proc","isoabbreviation":"AMIA Jt Summits Transl Sci Proc","nlmid":"101539486","essn":"2153-4063","issn":"2153-4063"}},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"438-447","abstractText":"Low-code platforms like OpenAI custom GPTs (cGPTs) promise easy development of specialized AI assistants for complex bioinformatics and clinical tasks, allowing researchers to integrate proprietary data into intuitive chatbot interfaces. However, these commercial frameworks operate as opaque \"black boxes,\" fundamentally clashing with open-science values and principles of reproducibility. To audit their hidden configurations, we performed \"jailbreak\" (prompt injection) attacks. We found that all tested cGPTs were critically vulnerable, leading to the leakage of private system instructions, full knowledge base files, and proprietary API details. This systemic failure poses severe security and privacy risks, particularly when handling sensitive patient data, clinical notes, or proprietary basic science assets. While low-code tools lower the barrier to AI adoption, their commercial nature and security flaws warrant extreme caution, forcing biomedical researchers to weigh convenience against the non-negotiable standards of data integrity and security.","affiliation":"Genomics & Computational Biology PhD Program, University of Pennsylvania, USA.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13274269"},{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13274269?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-06-19","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-20","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-06-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-01","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-01"}]}}